      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>NOW IS THE TIME.</head>
        <p>NOW IS THE TIME.</p>
        <p>Do you enjoy perfect health? If n:t. vvhy not? The simplest way for/ man to secure immunity from disease is to follow, as far as practicable, the laws of nature, and if illness overtakes him, to take as remedies only such preparations as are supplied by nature. The only natural remedies are vegetables, and the chief vegetable remedy of the present age is Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, the Vegetable Tonic. GWILYM EV.A.NS' QUININE BITTERS. GWilyM Evans' Quinine Bitters is a preparation now extensively taken throughout the country by patients suffering from debility, nervousness, and general ex- haustion and if any value be attached to human testi- mony, the efficacy of this medicine is established. Its claims have been tested and proved by the medical pro- fession, and others, and corroborated by the written testimonials of eminent men._</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>OUGHT TO BE IN EVERY HOUSE.</head>
        <p>OUGHT TO BE IN EVERY HOUSE.</p>
        <p>Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters contains not only. a suitable quantity of quinine in each dose, buttheactwe principles of the following well known herbs:—sarsapar- illa, saffron, gentian, lavender, burdock, and dandelion. At this season of the year no one should be without Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, the Best Remedy of the Age for various ailments. GWILY3I_ EV A.NS' QUININE BITTERS. _ _</p>
        <p>Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is recommended for indigestion in its different forms, such as sick head- aches, heartburn, cramp, flatulency, sense of fulness and oppression after eating, drowsiness, and pains in the region of the heart. It is, without exception, the best remedy for depression of spirits and melancholia. Being a vegetable" Pick-me-up," it is strongly recom- mended for nervous diseases, such as undue anxiety, despondency, fainting fits, neuralgia, and nerve pains generally.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>STRONGLY RECOMMENDED.</head>
        <p>STRONGLY RECOMMENDED.</p>
        <p>Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters has successfully treated (after all other known preparations have failed) severe cases of affections of the chest, such as common colds, bronchitis, asthmatic colds, shortness of breath.influenza, Scc. It purifies and enriches the blood. It is a healthy and safe tonic. Should be taken by all who are delicate, as it strengthens the system and promotes good health. People suffering great wgikness, either after an illness, or through long confinement in ill-ventilated rooms, or any other cause, find Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters most valuable as a great strength reviver. GWILYM EvANs' QUININE BITTERS. Owilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is sold everywhere in bottles 2s. 9d. and 4s. Gd. each or will be sent, on re- ceipt. of stamps, carriage free' direct from the Sole Pro- -prietors :—Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales.</p>
        <p>, BE W ARE OF ITATTONS. When you ask for Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, see that you get it, with the name " Gwilym Evans" on the label, on the stamp, and on the bottle, without which none is genuine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>RIFLING SLOT ME7rERS.-BOY SENT TO REFORMATORY.</p>
        <p>Edwin Muckley, aged fourteen years, residing with is parents at 32' Lloyd-street, pleaded guilty at War- forington Borough Police Court ort Monday to three charges of stealing cash from the Corporation gas slot meters. In the first place he was charged with having stolen 275. from the house, 24, Ernest-street, on the 2nd inst., also with stealing ss. 10d. from 6, Beswick-street, on the 15th February; and further with stealing on the 9th Ma7ch from 34, Lloyd-street, the sum of is. 3d.— The Chief Constable said for a long time the Gas Com- mittee had been continuously complaining about robber- ies from slot meters in houses mostly unoccupied, and also from some that were occupied. The Gas Committee had' gone so far as to offer a reward for any information given that would lead to detection. The prisoner had been caught in the immediate neigh- bourhood where something like 15 slot meters had been robbed during the last few weeks. The police had only charged him with three offences, to which he pleaded guilty. However he (the Chief Constable) had more than a moral ~ortainty that prisoner was the cause of all the robberies which had taken place in the neighbour- hood alluded to. Not only had he committed the thefts but hl had endeavoured to corrupt otker boys to follow that evil course by telling them what', lucrative busi- ness it was. About two o'clock on Saturday afternoon Mr. and Mrs. Legge who lived at 21, Ernest street, left their house. The meter contained over a' pound in cash, owing to the money not having been collected through the frequent ab- sence of the tenants. Shortly before nine on Saturday night Mrs. Legge's daughter happened to be passing her parents' house. She knew the house to be unoccupied but saw a light in the window. Upon looking in she saw the prisoner kneeling on the room floor opposite the meter. The lock was off the meter and apiece of iron with which he apparently knocked off the lock was lying near, and prisoner was scooping the coppers out of the meter into his cap. An alarm was given and the youth bolted througti the back window, by which he had obtained access to the house. Inquiries were immediately set on foot with the result that prisoner was apprehended in Wilderspool-road by P.C. Wise with ss.ld. in his posses- sion. It had transpired since that prisoner had tried to persuade two other boys to go with him to Mrs. Legge's house, but they refused. He (the Chief Constable) con- sidered it was a case for condign punishment, and he asked for him to be sent away'.—ln answer to an in- quiry from the Bench, the Chief Constable said the prisoner was as callous as the magistrates' clerk'sidesk. The magistrates ordered the prisonerto be detained in a -reformatory for five years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>Minor Cases.</head>
        <p>Minor Cases.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>NONDAY.—BerorP the Mayor (presiding), Alderman Francomb,Councillor Thornton, and Messrs. Birchall, F. W. Monks, and Hayward.</p>
        <p>IMPUDENT SHOP .THEFTS.—Kate O'Malley, 8, Dolman's-lane, was charged with having stolen from the shop of Messrs. Park and Bowden, Buttermarket-street, on the 29th March a pair of boots, value 3s. 6d.—The Chief Constable stated that prisoner went into the shop of Messrs. Park and Bowden on some trivial errand,and secreted a pair of lady's boots that were lying on the counter, under her shawl. The boots were afterwards pawned in Mersey-street, and prisoner was apprehended. She at first denied the offence, but subsequently admit- ted it.—Prisoner said it ivAl " all through drink." —She was fined 10n. and costs or 14 days' imprisonment. —William Mulligan, 3. Leslie's-court, was charged with having stolen on the previous Saturday, a tin bath, value 9d., from the shop of Mrs. Kite Davies, Lyme- street.—The prisoner went into prosecutor's shop, and frightened Mrs. Davies, who was alone, by demanding that she should give him something, saying if she did not it would be "the worse for her." Mrs. Davies refused, and, being afraid of prisoner went into the back kitchen and turned round to see prisoner marching ofr with a tin-bath.—Mulligan was later apprehended by P.C. Stewart. Prisoner, who now made his 13th .appearance, was sent to goal for a month with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before the Mayor (presiding), Aldarman Smethurst, Messrs. H. Roberts, James Hepherd, FL Thornton, and Councillor John Roberts. HUSBANDS AND WlVES.—Samuel Silcock, 2:5, Cart- wright-street, was charged with assaulting his wife, Martha Silcock on the 26th March.—The complainant stated that she returned home on the date named, tell- ing her husband that she had got a situation as he had requested. He thereupon thrashed her and put her out into the street.—The defendant, who complained that his wife repeatedly left him and found situations, ad- mitted striking her.—He was fined ss. and costs or seven days' imprisonment.—Mary Harris, 15, Pierpoint- street was charged with assaulting Mary Lawrence, 7, Pierpoint-street on the 31st March.—The defendant • admitted giving the complainant two blacki eyes in Mrs. M.annion's kitchen,but complained that IVlrs.Harris had upbraided her with a theft. She was fined 5s and costs. AN UNPROVOKED ASSAULT.—ThOMaS Mullaney, 88, !Wellington-street, was charged with assaulting Walter Rigby, 18, Greenall-street on the 4th April.—The com- plainant stated that he was walking down Fairclough's Avenue on the date named, when the defendant turned round and struck him on the right side of the face. He fell on the left side and the defendant further assaulted him by beating and thrashing him and tearing his (collar and shirt. The defendant contended that the complainant interfered in a dispute. This was denied by the complainant, who said he had never seen the man before.—P.C. Corbett also gave evidence.—Defend- ant was fined 10s. and costs. AN UNWELCOME SON-IN-LAW.—James Griffin, 5, ;Appleton-street, was charged with assaulting William Broadhurst, greengrocer, 227, Winwick-road.—Mr. Browne prosecuted, and said this was a serious matter. Every time the defendant 'got 'drunk or was under the influence of drink he entered the complainant's house, used threats, and did some damage. to the furniture or crockery. He was a son-in-law of Mr. Broadhurst's, and unfortunately, a separation order was granted between , the defendant and his wife. She was now in a situa- tion, earning her own living, apart from her husband. On the date named, the defendant entered Mr. Broad. hurst's house,put his fist in her face and threw her down. He also struck Mr. Broadhurst across the shoulder with the poker, and threatened to take his life.—Mr. Wm. Broadhurst gave corroborative evidence.—The defend- ant was bound over for twelve months in two sureties of £lO, and himself in a surety of £2O, or go to prison for two months. WIPE DESERTION.—Adrian Shirley, of Pierpoint- street, was charged with having deserted his wife, Sarah Shirley, on the 26th larch.—The Complainant said she went to a public-house to find him, but he evaded her by jumping over the wall.—The defendant said he was ashamed to go home as he had no wages to take.—The complainant agreed to give him another trial and the case was adjourned for a month. CRUELTY TO A HORSE.—Michael Toole, 25, Cloth Hall-yard, was fined ss. including costs for cruelty to a horse b 7 working it in an unfit state.—P.C.Newey proved the case and stated that there were two wounds on the horse. one on the back and another on the chest. It was working in the saddle at the time.—lnspector Gibson, R.S.P.C.A., in answer to the Mayor, as to why he had not summoned the owner, said Mr. Lunt and his fore- man had instructed the defendant not to work thehorse I in the saddle, but simply as a chain horse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>Warrington County Patty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Warrington County Patty Sessions.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. W. H. Bleckly (in the chair), and Captain Withington. BA.D!LANGUAGE.—For ralking use o f pro fane language James Ears, Winwick-road, Warrington was fined 5s and costs. ALLEGED ADITLTERATtoN OF PEPPER.—Peter Heath Warrington-road, Penketh, was summoned for having sold pepper which was adulterated 2 per cent with ground olive stones.—Mr. William Rudd, of Liverpool defended.—Mr. Wilson, of Preston, prosecuted on behalf of the Lancashire County Cluncil, and said they hoped that after many similar prosecutions, such cases as the one now before the Court would have been put a stop so. The adultera- tion was now going down to something like 2 per cent., but it had been up to 15, 10 and 4 per cent.—Sergeant Ross, of Leigh, stated that on February 16th he went into the defendant's shop and was supplied with 6 ounces of white pepper. This he divided into three parts, giving one to Mrs. Heath. The second portion *was retained by the police superintendent, and the remainder sent to the county analyst, who reported that it was adulterated 2 per cent. with ground olive stones. —Mr. Rudd said defendant had had his sample analysed by a leading London analyst, and it was found to be pure. He asked for an adjournment in order that the sample could be sent to the Government analyst.—The case was adjourned sine die. Tar.FT or A Ducx.—James Bleakley and John Webster, both of Burtonwood, were charged with having stolen a duck, of the value of 3s. 6d., the property of Ellen Fairclough, Alder-lane Farm, Burtonwood. The evidence showed that on the first inst. the duck was missed from the farm yard, and a little later two men attired like the prisoners were seen coming from the direction of the farm, carrying a duck. The police were informed, and P.C. Rainford visited the men's lodgings but defendants denied being concerned in the theft. Upon a search being made, the officer found the duck being stewed in a can on the fire.—A fine of 108. and costs was imposed. THEFT OF A WALKING STlCK.—Matthew Littler,3o9 Millford Lane, Haydock, was charged with having stolen a walking stick value 10s 6d, the property of Richard B. Bott, Rigby street, St. Helens. from the Black Horse Inn, Haydock.—Prosecutor visited the public house on the 2nd April, and on going out left the stick in the tap room. A short time afterwards he discovered that he had forgotten it; but when he re- turned to the inn, the stick had gone. Defendant had been seen to take it away.—Defendant,who pleaded that he had no felonious intention, was fined 103. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>ViZAR AND TEAR OF CITY LIFE.</head>
        <p>THE ViZAR AND TEAR OF CITY LIFE.</p>
        <p>Why do so many of our City men look so sickly? Because they drink too much tea, and when they are fagged ont cannot take more substantial and nutritive food. The system thus gradually becomes weakened and debilitated; there is no relish for food, no energy for work, and the whole of the physical and mental con- dition becomes seriously deranged. One City clerk—Mr. H. W. Elliott, 23, NOrfolk-road, Canonbury, N.—writes :—" I work late hours, but I find Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa a delicious and stimulating drink. My doctor, grocer, and chemist speak very highly of it." And if you use Vi-Cocoa regularly you will speak very highly of it too. Sold in 6d. packets and 9d. and Is. 6d. tins everywhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>SPECIAL RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL MEETING. A special meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council was held on Tuesday, Mr. S. Woodward presid- ing. The Clerk read the minutes of a meeting of the Helsby Parochial Committee held on March 31st for the purpose of investigating complaints made at the last Council meeting with regard to the construc- tion of the new sewerage works at Helsby.—lt was resolved that the Chairman and Messrs.Pilling,Linaker, Mounfield, Antwis, Guest and Roylance form a com- mittee to meet at Helsby and further discuss the matter. —Mr. John Eyes, of Hartford, was appointed clerk of works to superintend the erection of the isolation hospital at Dutton. A BOGUS DETECTIVE AT RUNCORN. sOn Saturday Frederick Potter Greases, a smart-look- ing youth of nineteen, of Manchester. was charged at Runcorn with falsely representing himself to be a Liverpool detective. About eight on Friday night prisoner went to Eversleight, the residence of Mr. F. Rowlands, in Monghland-lane, stated that he was a Liverpool detective, and told Miss Rowlands two burglars were on their way from Liverpool and she must liberate the dogs and securely fasten the house. In great alarm Miss Rowlands sought her brother, and later Police-constable Brayford apprehended prisoner who had a revolver loaded in five chambers and six cartridges in his possession. Asked what he meant by such conduct, prisoner replied" Playing the fool." His mother, a highly respectable looking lady. said she thought hi S mind had become unhinged through reading penny dreadfuls. She promised to take care of him if he were discharged. Prisoner was bound over under the First Offenders Act.</p>
        <p>Drowning Mystery. Coroner Brighouse held an inquest at the Police Station on Tuesday evening, touching the death of Joseph Harrisoa (29), gamekeeper, residing in Appleton-road, Stretton, whose body was recovered from the Mersey near Atherton's Quay on Sunday morning.—The evidence showed that on March 14th, deceased, who was principally employed at Croft, accompanied his father-in-law, Michael Lafferty, to Warrington. They called at several publichouses for drinks, and at seven in the evening arrived at the Packet House Inn. Deceased expressed his intention of visiting his father in James-street and Lafferty left him at the publichouse and went home. Nothing more was heard of the deceased until his body was recovered from the river by Constable Bennett. There were no marks of violence. A verdict of "Found drowned "was returned.</p>
        <p>FINED FOR GAMING AND ASSAULT. On Saturday, Edward Roberts, of Crow-lane, Earles- town, was charged at the Newton-le-Willows Police Court with gamine and assaulting the police on Friday. Roberts was surprised by Police-constable Campsil, and as he refused to give his name and address, he was taken into custody. On the way to the police station he violently assaulted the officer. For gaming he was fined is. and costs, and for the assault 10s. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, April 9 1904.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 9 1904.</p>
        <p>EASTER VESTRY MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON PARISH CHURCH RITUAL DISCUSSED.</p>
        <p>The Inteodotiol of Altar Lion.</p>
        <p>RECUR'S EXPLANATION -LIVELY MEETING.</p>
        <p>The harmony which generally characterises War- rington Parish Church vestry meetings was disturbed on Tuesday evening. As usual the annual Easter gathering for the transactign of parochial business was held in the. National Schools, Church- street, and it was evident from the crowded attendance that lively doings were anticipated. The recent introduction of altar lights into the Church appears to be a sore point with some parishioners, and at the close of the meeting, Jr.'A F. G-. Tpylor, a sidesman who resigned his position as superintendent of the b)ys' Sunday school in conse- quence of the Rector's action, voiced the feelings of the anti-ritualists by severely criticising the innova- tion and moving a resolution of protest. The Rector replied at length to the various objections and was given a patient hearing. When he exercised his pre- rogative as chairman and declined to take a vote on the resolution, there was 50013 hooting and dernonstra, tions of_ disapproval, bat the .meeting afterwards quietly concluded, having lasted over two hours. The Rector was supported on the platform by Nix% J. G. Craik (rector's warden), Mr. John Dewhurst (people's warden) and Jr. E. k. White (vestry clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>FINANCIAL 'NfA.TrkRs.</head>
        <p>FINANCIAL 'NfA.TrkRs.</p>
        <p>BAZAARS AND TEA PARTIES A SOURCE OF REVENUE.</p>
        <p>The financial statement was submitted by Mr. Dew- burst. The receipts arnoanted to £703 6s. 21., and the expen liture to £7lB Os. 103., lowing a debit balance of £8 14s. 81 The ordinary offertories had totalled £286 4s. 61, and the special offer- tories £231 its. 101. Offertories in aid of the Liverpool Cathedral and the Newchurch rebuilding funds had realized nearly £3O. In consequence of that they were not quite so well off as they otherwise would have been, but in each case he had namel they were in duty bound to render assistance. (Haar. hear). NVith regard to the electricity account the cost of blowing the organ and lighting the Ichoir was £l4 3s 7d; the blowing by hand prat iou-dy cost £l3 93 11.. There had been a return for the use of the motor by pupils of the organist of £6 Hs 61., so that not only had they the organ better blown, but there was no waiting at all ; the org.an could be played at any time. They would be pleased to hear that they had deemed it advisable to increase the insurance of the church against fire, burglary and accident. The a uount they paid now was £ll 14s. against £9 10s 3d, last year. The parochial tea realized £45 Bs.—as compared with £3l 10361 last year. He should like to see the church self-supporting. He did not think they ought to be compelled to fall back upon either sales of work or tea parties for the main- tenance of the church from which they all derived very great benefit awl many privileges. They had an excel- lent Rector and staff of clergy, an exaellent organist, organ and choir, and a clmfortable church, and he was sure that every one of them av3ald feel that it was his duty to maintain the ordinary cost of the services of the church. With regard to outside matters, he conteaded that there should be a fund created to rep air the fabric of the church. They coald not tell what damage a stirrn might da to the roof, Szo. This was, Mr. Dewhnrst said in conclusion, his sixth year of office. He had one object at heart, and that was to see the cost of the rea:, ).-atioh of the church defrayed.</p>
        <p>DA.VLE3 movel tii pain; ot th 3 accounts. He too ttougt th C!IllrY1 ou.h to b 3 S ,lf-iupparting. On Sun lay I tit, w.t'l3ut tniia a s93oilt effort, they raised over £l6:they out therefore to hs able to raise from LEO t £ 111 every ittaiiv. (Er. hstr). ?qr. T. M.t.s) seoidllali th 3 m3i32 wa4 agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>RECTO:VS WARDEN'.</head>
        <p>RECTO:VS WARDEN'.</p>
        <p>The RECTDR nominatel 11r. Crtik a 3 his warden for the ensuing year au Ire n that t-kwl tt it wts always his object to hare a wlrdan wh coal n 3 aled him 3911 just as much to the rolple as to th% clergy. 31r. CRAIK than'cel th) R33tar fa: h)n yar con- ferred upon him. PEOPLT-3 W1R,131.;N:</p>
        <p>Mr. JOiEt'll LEE propo3ed thAt N 1•:. S t:m lel Taylor be elected people's warden..</p>
        <p>Mr. cl%itt:ETr se2onded,</p>
        <p>The REcrota said Mr. Decrhurst wa3 willing to stand again. The coming year would be a very important one as they had still to raise about3£2so for the restoration fund, and he did not, think it would be wise to make a change. Mr. GEORGE ARCHER proposed the re-election of Mr. Dewhurst,ane Mr. T. J. Hulse seconded. Mr. TAYLOR stid that if he consulted his own feel- ings he should have nothing to do with the matter, but if it was the wish of the parishioners that he should accept office he would do so. At the present time they weregoing through troubled wtters,and it was ne3essary that something should be done to throw oil upon those waters.</p>
        <p>After further discussion a vote was taken and Mr. Taylor was declared to have been elected unanimously. The result was received with loud applause. SIDESIMEN.</p>
        <p>Mr. ORME n-rdnttei M. W. E At'cin hig Stl33- man and Mr. Taylor nominated Mr. James Wood.</p>
        <p>The RECTOR saad that although he deeply regretted • Mr. Daw'aurst's loss he welcomed with pleasure the election of Jr. Satinet Taylor as paaple's warden. and he could not express his pleasure in seeing Sir. Wood back again in office. . (Applause.) The meeting' then proceeded to elect eight sidesmen, an amendment by Mr. Archer that the number be incr used to 12, falling to the ground. Mr. Thomas Mason was nomintted lint declined to serva, as h 3 would like to be in a poiition of greater freedom and less responsibility. The show of hands resulted in the election of the following:—Meisrs. John Dawhurst, Edward Isherwood, F. G. Ttylor, W. Daintith, T. J. Hulse, W. Roter3. R. W. Celli% and W. L. \Williams. Mr. Joseph Wilson wIC el 3:to:I si-lasmin for St. George's Mission, while for St. Clement's Mission Mr. Charlesworth•WAS selected. Colonel Buckton and Mr. James Waod were elected the lay representatives to the diocesan and ruri-decanal conferences,and Mr. P.C. Price cal Mr. J. W. Whittle, auditors. Mr. E. A. White was re-appointed .vestry clerk.</p>
        <p>THE C ICJ Er,' I 'II rum,. S:IOULD Tfl C3NGREGA.TION BE CONSULTEO ? A P.IOTE 3T AI UNS AL VA.% CS THE RECTOR EKPLAINS.</p>
        <p>The ordinary business c mcludal, the questioa of the ritual in use at the Parish Clutch was introduced by Mr. F'. G. Taylor. He invited Cal Rector to at once address the meeting on the introduc-ion of altar lights. but Canon Willis preferred to hear the objections and then reply. Mr. TA.vi.on then proceeded to go fully into the matter. The first ground of his ohjection to tne lights was that he looked upon the action of the Rector in introducing them as a direct breach of a promise made in the past to the congregation—not once or twice, but half a dozen times at least—that he would not do any- thin.. o which would be contrary to their wishes. (Hear. hear) There were many in the rcorn who had heard him say so. He did not say for one moment that it was a direct breach of faith and that the Rector wilfully disregarded his past promises, but the promise in ques- tion was made, and before the new form of ritual was introduced he (Mr. Taylor) reminded the Rector of it in a letter which he chose merely to acknowledge. Was that the way to assist the spread of the Christian religion in these days when there was so much so-called lawlessness in the Church, and so many thousands of people who never attended church ? The second point was that the alteration, was made without the con- gregation having an opportunity of being consulted. The answer of the Rector to that was that he had never consulted the congregation upon any point of doctrine or ritual. On doctrine he never had, but on the point of ritual he consulted the Church Council at the meeting in July, and furthermore he (Mr. Taylor) said that the Rector then promised to call a special meeting of the Church Cour cil, bat that that was never done. When the matter did come before the Church Council in January they were told that the Rector did not wish for any discussion on the point, and that it was his intention to introduce the new form of ritual three days later. The Rector had also told him privately that if the lights assisted some:he did not mind if they offended a few others. The REcron. : I never said anything of the kind. Mr. TAYLOR: I was so extraordinarily struck with ; the phrase thalkwhen I got home I made a note of it, I The RECTOR I never said it. Mr. TAYLOR : Pardon me, you said it. If you now say you did not intend it I accept the explanation with ! pleasure. The RECTOR : I never said it. Mr. TAYLOR then alluded to the points urged by the Rector in the "Parish Magazine.' Although in the famous Lincoln judgment is was held that' the lights were legal, the Archbishop added :—" It is not :ikely . that they will cease to be distasteful to many minds, and when that is the case,even in a small degree,charitv and good sense ought not to be violated." And he would specially commend that plea of charity and good sense to the notice of the Rector in a case where not in a small degree but to a considerable extent the lights were distasteful to members of the congregation. (Hear, hear.) Whether the lights were legal or illegal inthem- selves there could be no question that the method of their introduction into thechnrch had been illegal as no faculty had been obtained.Mr.Taylor quoted several well- , knownauthorities who held that a facultywas required for</p>
        <p>Ithe introduction of permanent orn 'meats into the church. Th e Bishop of] the diocese said:—"l Can have no possible objection to yourstating in public my views on the question of obeying the law of the diocese and church—that no perm:an-nit ornament must be introduced into a church without a faculty." (Loud applause.) He was sure none of those present desired to go to the Courts and obtain a faculty for the removal of the slights, although one watild undoubtedly be granted if applied for. He appealed to the Rector to reconsider has decision with reference to the matter; on moral grounds, on charitable grounds, and on legal grounds, he appealed to him to withdraw the ornamelts. They were objectionable to him (Mts. Taylor), and judging from the manner in which he had been listened to that night there was no doubt they were objectionable to many other people in the parish. (Applause.) He appealed to him to recreate the harmony which had existed in the parish since he c tme into it 15 years ago, until now. They had no wish to have discord, and there was not a person in the perish who would wish for the disturbances which had occurred in other towns. They wished matters to be conducted in a peaceable way and in a charitable spirit. They wanted the clergy to help them and not to hinder them, and that help had been received from the Rector in the pest. if these things were to be continued they were,as a perish, not going to be helped, but hindered. He proposed "That this meeting views with regret the action of the Rector in introducing the advanced ritual into the church, and hereby expresses its desire that the tame be not continued.' (Applause).</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Dlfyiriff, ha: 'resigned his position in the choir in consequence of the Rector's action,seconded and he said he did so on parsontl grounds because he thought the way in which they had been treated by the Rector had not been fair. Ha had been connected with the church for 20 years, and the Rector gave them thoroughly to understand that he never would, without first consulting them and explaining his reason, make any alteration in the ritual. He ventured to think that if they had only known what the Boteler chapel was going to be turned into, some of them, himself included would have objected to any restoration. He thought they had been shamefully deceived., (Hear, hear). He used to thin' clergyman were Samuels and had all been "called," but during the past few years he had changed his opinion. It gave him ve:y great pain to have to sever his connection with the Pariah Guirch, and all for the sake of pleasing a few emotional people. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>RECTOR AND RONIANISM.</head>
        <p>THE RECTOR AND RONIANISM.</p>
        <p>In his reply, the RECTOR, said he had purposely re- frained from speaking until that night because he knew that he would be chargedaas he was charged, with taking advantage of the pulpit to sp silk where there could be no right of reply. Therefore, though it was very painful to him to speak upon such a—to hirn—soleinn subject at a mixed gathering like an Easter Vestry, still it was an opportunity where they would all be able to meet him, and, if they wished, to reply. He had given, in the magazine for February,three reasons why he put the candlesticks in the church. The first was this: that the presence of Our Lord in the Holy Eucharist was in accordance with the teaching of the Bible and the Church. The second reason he gave was that the two lights had been from time immemorial the custom of the Church of England. The third reason was the Church's own order in the Ornaments Rubric. That Rubric was put in its present form in 1662, when their last Prayer Book was issued, and which referred them back to the second year of Edward Vi., 1549, when they knew that the two lights were in use in the Church of England. He had giren those three reasons, anct they were weighty reasons. (Laughter.) The whole and sole signification of those lights was, as he said—and as Archbishop Crammer who died for the faith said,—the 'presence of our Lord in the Holy Communion. But there was one more reason he would now add—that it was in accordance with the Lincoln judgment given 14 years ago. These were the words from his Church Law Book: "lii Read v. Bishop of Lincoln the question whether the presence of two lighted candles on the Holy Table, when not required for the purpose of giving light, was lawful, was much discussed, and the Archbishop came to the conclusion that two candles so used were legal when and after the prayer book became law and so re- mained—a scheme to make them otherwise not having been completed, the Acts of Uniformity having no bear- ing upon them and their very general disuse being due to causes other than legal enactments. The Archbishop also held that as cicere nony in worship is an action or act in which material objects may or may not be used, but is not in itself any material object,andas the candles were not C3nn9cted with any. erroneous or strange teach- ing as to the Sacrament, their mare prasence throughout the service was not illegal. The Privy Council, without intimating any opinion as to their legality, held that the bishop was not, by merely participating in the ser- vice, rendered responsible for their preseace." There were, he thought, three main objeetions made to the lights. Tne first was waat he might call the cemmon- sense one—that of the man of the world, who said: "Why divide your people for such a trifle as this ?" If it was a trifle he (the Re3tor) would agree that it would be foolish, but it W. 13 not a trifle—(hear, hear,)—it had very deep rennin... The second objection cams from thee who said t leve.had not Woe consulted. He had always consulted his people—he thought no man more so —about everything which he thought concerned the welfare of the parish ever since he came here. He had consulted them times out of mind, and had often been begged not to do so,about such things as acquisition of sites, building schools, restoration of the church—any- thing in which he thought they had a right to be con- sidered he had always consulted them upon. But he had never consulted them upon any question of doctrine or ritual. He put the present altar table in the chancel directly after he came, and he did it because he thought it was within his rights and power without consulting any- body. He never consulted anybody about putting on the cross—(A Voice: It was not required); nor about the colours ; nor about the flowers; nor the dress or position of the celebrant. He did all those things after the most careful and earnest thought, but without con- sulting anybody. Also he had never consulted anyone on questions of doctrine. He had tested the feeling because he was not a young man. He had had 15 years' experience in another parish before he came to Warring- ton,ancl there there was a very advanced ritual compared with that in use at the Parish Church, but he said"No, I will go down to the bottom and work as quietly, and as wisely, ancras kindly as I can." (Laughter) "You may laugh gentlemen," continued the Rector," but laughter is not a sign of spirituality." (Hear, hear.) He had caraully and earnestly considered every step he.had taken, and had not consulted anybody about any of those things he had just mentioned, because he thought they were In his province. He came there to teach not to be taught. (Hear, hear.) He did not want to divide his people, and he felt, if he had consulted them, he would have had two parties—those who wished it and those who did not. He felt, therefore, he would rather take the responsibility and bear the brunt of it himself, andhe thought they would say that that was right. The third objection came from those who had an honest fear of 'Rome. No man could honestly and from the bottom of his heart dislike what was really Roman more than hedid. He did intensely love the Church of England and all her doctrines, and he abominated from the bottom of his heart everything which was distinctly Roman. They must not be afraid of being thought like Rome in some views. They must hold some views together. Thew held the doctrine of the Trinity in common with Rome and Nonconformists, and they held the doctrine of the ministry in common with Rome, but that did not make them Roman. (Hear, hear). This Question of lights was not Roman. It was English and Catholic in the truest sense and not distinctly Roman. He had had a great deal of experience of 'Rome, and could say this, that he never yet knew of any person going over to Rome who was not intensely in earnest and what many had said to him was this: "In the Church of England you do what you like and believe what you like. lam going over to a communion where they don't do what they like but what they are taught is right." .He never wanted to do what he liked or to teach them what he liked, but what the Church taught them. He had in fact been the means of keep- ing back people from going to Rome. He loved the Church of England from the bottom of his heart, and should never do anything except what he knew to b 3 in accordance with her doctrine. He held that there ma; no need to ask for a faculty for what was covered by the Ornaments Rubric. The min they ought to find fault with was the one who took away the candles from the church, not the man who put them on again. (Hear hear.) They did not know who that was—it was lost in past history—hut what right had that man to take away what was always the custom of the Church of England? Lastly, how was it there was no request made for a faculty up to the present moment? They never asked him to ask for a faculty for the altar table, the cross, the vases, the colours, the cross in the Boteler Chapel, the litany desk, or the choir stalls. He should think these were things which they might think required a faculty, so why should they be tied down to require a faculty for the altar lights? A VOICE: You know we objected to them. Continuing, the REcroa said he had spoken openly. He knew there were some of them who felt very strongly on the matter, and ha respected their feeling and had the deepest sympathy with it. He thought they would give him credit for having been open and honest during the 15 years he had been with them, and he should think that when a man had had 30 or 40 years' experience and done everything in his power for the sake of the Church, they should have confidence in him. (Applause.) Mr. TA.YLOR : One moineat. Do If understand that the Rector said I had a right to reply? The RECTOR: I gave you the qpportunity to speak with a right of reply myself, but you may speak if your wish. (Hear, hear.) Mr. TIYLOR said the explanations were all very well but what they would like to know was whether the congregation were to have any voice in matters of ritual, or be governed entirely by his (the Rector's) views and be driven out of the church, if they did not approve of them. The Rector bad not made any com- ment upon the resolution he had proposed. and which had been seconded, and he iherefore asked the vestry clerk to put the resolution, to see Whether it met with the approval of the meeting. The CLERK : It is entirely for the Chairman (the Rector) to decide whether a resolution is to be put to the meeting or not. The RECTOR: I think that concludes our business. Thank you. There was some hissing and demonstrations of dis- approval when the Rector declined to put the resolu- tion, but the meeting afterwards quietly concluded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>sr. ANTS.</head>
        <p>sr. ANTS.</p>
        <p>Charchinilen3 and Insurance of the</p>
        <p>Vicange.</p>
        <p>The VICA.R. (Bev. J. D Ivies) presided at the St. Ann's Vestry meeting on Tuesday evening. The parishioner present were the Rev. D. Kernin (curate), and Messrs. F. Taylor (vicar's warden), H. H. Bretherton (people's warden) T. Winter, G. Armstrong, S. Daniels, J Harrison, W. Thomas, Qaarter-Master Sergeant Taylor, and Mr. R. Woods (vestry clerk).</p>
        <p>THANKS.</p>
        <p>The VECA.R. Slid they must first return their thanks, not only to the chanhwardens and all the church officers, for their sorvicns daring the past year, but to all who were workers in connection with their parish. (Eisa,r, hear).</p>
        <p>THE INSURANCE OF THE VICARAGE. NOT 10 BE PAID OUP OF CHURCH FUNDS.</p>
        <p>The VICAR nominated AL:. Frank Taylor as his warden and Mr. Taylor accepted the office. Mr. ARMSTRONG then proposed the re-election of Mr. Bretherton as people's warden. Quarter-master Sergeant Taylor seconded. Mr. BREMERTON rose to refer to the insurance of the Vicarage which had been introduced before the Chu rch,Co The VICA.Ft ruled Mr. Bretherton out of order. Mr. BagTFIER,TaN pressed fora hearing as the matter was one which ought to be thrashed out now. The VICAR said the matter to which Mr. Bretherton wished to draw attention was not before the meeting. The vestry had nothinr to do with the ineura.noe of the Vicarage. If he (the Vicar) thought he was under any legal obligation to ply the insurance premium he would do so himself. He contended that a Vicarage was a parochial property and ought to beinsured by the parish and not by tha Vicar. Mr. WINTER thought the matter was a very small one, and hoped Mr. Bretherton would not allow this matter to stand in the way of his election as pe )ple's warden. Mr. BRETEIERTON said it was not customary for the insurance of vicarages to he paid out of church funds, ! only on special occasions. He had consulted rLsolicitor whose advice was that there was no claim on the church to pay the insurance premium of vicarages. He objected on principle to. the church paying the insur- ance. The flu Lnoes of the church wOuld not allow of such a payment.</p>
        <p>The VICAR: Thtt is not a principle. If it is principle it is not affected by finance. Mr. BRETHERT3N: I don't think it is the right thing to do. I would rather have the mttter thrashed out, and I would rather somlone else act as people's warden, under the circumstances. Mr. F. TA.YLOR SAid he wts in full sympathy with Mr. Bretherton's views.</p>
        <p>Quarter-master Sergeant Taylor proposed Mr. Winter as peoples' warden and Mr. Bretherton seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. 'WINTEFt declined to stand. The Vicait slid it had 113 en decided to insure the new vicarage for £1,700. He took his stand on principle. If he was legally bound to insure the vicarage he would do so, but if the parishioners were under a legal obliga- tion to insure it they must do so. He understood that property was insured by the owners and not by the tenants. The parishioners owned the vicarage,and that being so it was their duty to insure the building. Mr. BRETEIFIRTON said he was surprised when he saw Lit the "Parish Magazine" that the Church Council had decided to insure the Vicarage. _ _</p>
        <p>The VICA.It : -.4:re you willing to stand as warden? Mr. 13a.wr.irFarroN: No., I are not willing. Mr. ARMiTRONG was ttl3ll proposel as warden, but he also declined to stand. He suggested that the Vestry Clerk be asked to give his opinion on the point raised by Mr._Bretherton.</p>
        <p>The VESTRY CLERIeSrlid the Vicar wasa freehold in- habitant of the Vicarage so long as he was Vicar and it was the Vicar's duty, according to law, to insure the Vica.rage. The VICAR : That is the law is it?</p>
        <p>The VESTRY CLERK : Yes. You are just as liable for the payment of the insurance premium as you are for the repair of the Vicarage. The VICA.R : If that is the legal position, the matter need not be discussed further. I would not receive a gratuity. Mr. WINTER : The matter might be referred to the Archdeacon.</p>
        <p>Mr. BRET HERTON : We have had our vestry clerk's opinion—that is sufficient. Thq VESTRY CLERK : I expressed an opinion and I stand by it. _</p>
        <p>The VICA.R : That settles the point with regard to insurance. If it is the duty of the Vicar to pay it, then personally I shall pay it, notwithstanding the resolution of the Church Council. Mr. BnurtiEirroN said the Vicar must not think that this mater had been brought forward with any disres- pect to him. It was a matter he felt justified in bringinz before the Vestry. Tne VICA.R said ha did nit object to the discussion, but it was certainly not Vestry work. Mr. BREMERTON was then unanimously elected people's warden, and he accepted the position.</p>
        <p>OTHER BUSINES3-AN UNSATISFACTORY BALANCE SREET.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of the VICAR, seconded by Mr. BKETHERTON, the following gentlemen were re-elected sidesmen :—Aessrs. Armstrong. Bunch, Daniels, Gleave, Harrison, Owen, F. Taylor, H. Taylor, and T. Winter.— Mr. Armstrong an-I Quartermaster-Sergeant Taylor were elected lay representatives at the ruri-decanal and dio- cesan conferences.—Mr. F. Taylor' in submitting the balance sheet for the past year, said he regretted very much to have to inform them that there was a consider- able deffcit on the year's working. That was attribu- table, as they were all aware, to the heavy expenditure incurred in connection with the erection of anew boiler for heating the church. They started with a debit balance of £7 3s. 8d„ and closed with liabilities amounting to £43 Os. 5d., which included the sum of £4O 17s. Id. eKpFmcled on heating apparatus. Mr. Taylor further stated that there was a supplementiry account still owing for the heating appara- tus. The total offerings for the year amounted to £lOO 10s. 3d. and there had been collected during the year l-1,325 coins, which WAS less than the previous year.—The Vicar said the heating apparatus was perfect as regarded the hot water, hilt it WAS far from perfect as regarded the hot air. Alluding to thel finances of the church, the rev. gentleman s Lid he had,, received 23 lis. special donations tow irds the expanse ' incurred by the heating apparatus. The balance sheet was adopted, and the meeting concluded with an ack- nowledgment of the Vestry Clerk's services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>ST. THOMAS'S, STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>ST. THOMAS'S, STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>At noon on Tuesday, the vestry meting in connec- tion with this church was held. The (Rev. 11. V. Pigot) presided, and there were alsl present the Rev. C. A. Nlarkham Evans (curate). lessrs. Henry Greenall (vicar's warden), William Richardson (people's warden), F. Pearson, T. R. Westbrook, S. W. Dallow. T. Sharrocks, F. Timparley, C. Spencer, F. Rose and T. J azkson. THE IMPROVED LIGHTING OF TRS _CHURCH.</p>
        <p>---- Mr.RIWIARDSOg presented the statement of accOunts, which showed a balance due to the wardens of £5 is. as against £2 Os. 101. last year. £4O was drawn from the Repair Fund (for contract for re-lighting of the church with incandescent lights) a big increase on the £l6 43. drawn from the fund the year previous. Mr Richar,lson remarked that they had now three times as much light as they got under the old system,and where- as the.averaga cast previously was £l6 per annum they expected it would be reduced with the new lighting to about £B. (hear, hear). They had replaced 98 new burners with 48 ineandescents, and he thought the re- sults fully justified the expenditure. (hear, hear). The work had been done well, and had given satisfaction all round. The insarance or the church had been increased' from £4.750 to £10,950, and the total premium now was £8 10s. 3d.. an extra charge of £3 as ad., per annum.— The financial statement was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>ELECTION OF WARDENS AND SIDESMEN.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF WARDENS AND SIDESMEN.</p>
        <p>The VICAR said he had great pleasure in asking Mr. Greenall to ac.lept oqice again as warden. As he lived a few yards out of the parochial boundary he thought he had no legal power to force him to serve, but Mr. Greenall knew that it was not only his own wish, but the unanimous wish of everyone connected with the church that he shduld do so. (Hear, hear.)—Mr. Greenall said he would be pleased to again accept office —The Vicar said that with reference to the appoint- ment of parishioners' warden he had received a letter from Mr. T. J. Down, who was staying at Llandudno. and who had often been the principal voice in nominating the parishioners' warden, in which he expressed the hope that Mr. Richardson would be re-appointed.—Mr. Dallow said he would move a resolution to that effect with great pleasure. Mr. Shatrocks seconded, and it was unanimously agreed to.—Mr. Richardson thanked the vestry for this mark of their confidence, and said he was only too pleased to do what, he could in the interests of the church. Mr. GREEN.A.LL proposed the re-election of the follow- ing gentlemen aq sidesmen—Aessrs. A. Down. T.. 7. Lee, J. H. Clarke, J. Jeffes. T. R. Westhrook,J. A. McGregor, W. Mclntyre, S. W. Dallow, T. Sharrocks, L. G. Davies J. Dunbabin and J. Parkes.—Mr. Pearson seconded. The names were then approved. Messrs. McGregor and Sharrocks Were appointed lay</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>BREAD AND PASTRY FOR DYSPEPTICS.</head>
        <p>BREAD AND PASTRY FOR DYSPEPTICS.</p>
        <p>Many people wit'a poor digestions are unable to eat , home-made bread and pastry. This is very often ' because the raising agent used by the cook has not done its duty, and the pastry is doughy and heavy. Brown &amp; Poison's new raising powder, called "Paisley Flour" changes all this. it is used mixed dry, one part with to 8 parts of ordinary flour, and takes the place of,yeast and baking powder. It is a real heln in baking and pastry making, enabling the work to be done quickly, easily and pleasantly. There is nothing at all harmful in it. and it makes little crusty loaves and delicious pastry, so well raised, light, and free from any suspicion of doughiness, that they can be eaten with benefit by those of weak digestion. Brown &amp; Poison's "Paisley Flour" is packed in 30. and 7d. packets, the latter being enough for half-a-stone of flour. Try it now.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <p>CORNW &amp;LL. ENGLAND'S IDEAL SPRING, HEALTH AND PLEASURE RESJIT. %Vint UTOU'Et Tkl TRISEILB AND EXPIINSO OF IA LONG Folmar; JOUIINKY ? WHEN</p>
        <p>GREAT WESTERN RAILWAY</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>CAN TARR YOU IN A PIM HOURS TO THE</head>
        <p>CAN TARR YOU IN A PIM HOURS TO THE</p>
        <p>'FOR INrHRESTUST4 AND 0011PRBEIBINSIVII</p>
        <p>ILLUSTRA.TED BOOKLET FREE.</p>
        <p>representatives, the Vicar re.narking that they had done their duties very satisfactorily,</p>
        <p>COMPLIMENTARY. Mr.GREENALL proposed a vote of thanks to the organ- ist and choirmaster and the cho'r, and said they had every reason to be satisfied with the way they all per- formed their duties. Visitors coming to the church always spoke of the excellent choir and organist.—lr. Jackson seconded.—The Vicar remarked that in Nfessrs. Down and Sharrocks they htd excellent deputies, and they might be included in the thanks.—The vote having been agreed to, Alessrs. Rose, Sharrocks and Pearson returned thanks. _ .</p>
        <p>Tne VIC4R prop vsei a vote of thanks to the church- wardens, who, he said, had served them faithfully and well for a number of years. Mr. Greenall and Mr. Richardson worked very harmoniously, and he did not think it would be possible to iPaprove upon them.— Mr. JaArson seconded, and said he did not think they could make a more happy choice in their wardens.—The motion was passed with itcdlammation.—The wardens returned thanks. _ _</p>
        <p>The Vic I.R was hEmrtily thanked for presiding.—ln reply the Vicar said he had just completed five years' service there, audit had been a very happy time indeed. He hoped they would be spared for many years to work on together in the same harmonious manner as they had worked in the past. He would like to take that oppor- tunity of acknowledging very gratefully the handsome Easter offering they had given him. It was a record amount, and it was a great pleasure to him to have that mark of their continued confidence. (Hear, hear.) A reference to the death of Mr. James Tinsley, who was formerly a warden at tha church, will be found in another column.</p>
        <p>DELIGHTFUL CORNISH RIVIERA. WRITS TO TEIS SUPBRINTSNDSNT OP TIII LINH, PADDINGTON STATION, LONDON, :7.,</p>
        <p>CONTENTS :—CORNWALL AND ITS CLIMATE; THE CORNISH RIVIERA., AND WHAT TO SEBeTHERE ; SCILLY ISLANDS. WITK PULL INFORMATION AS TO TRAIN AND MOTOR CAR SERVIOSS; FARES: apanAL TOUR TICKETS; AND A HANDY MAP. JAMES C. INGLIS, General Manager.</p>
        <p>Itunity of thanking both wardens for their kindness and consideration and the help they had given him ever since he came into the parish He had only been with them three months, ' but he was quite sure no new incumbent could have had kinder and better friends than the two wardens. They had given very valuable help to the parish during the past year, For the six months immediately pre- ceding his coming into the parish, they were without a resident clergyman, and the main responsibility—in fact almost the wnole respensibility—of providinr, ser- vices Sunday by Sunday fell upon the wardens. 'They had also the responsibility of keeping the parish in an efficient state, and superintending the affairs of the church ; and they deserved the thanks of the whole parish for the way in which they had performed their duties. Mr. Houghton had held the office of Vicar's warden for three years but as he lived a considerable distance from the church and felt that a warden ought to reside in the parish, he desired to retire. He (Mr. Firth) had great pleasure in nominating Mr. J. Fargher who lived in the very midst of the parish, took a very great interest in the work,and rendered great help in the Sunday school. Mr. W. Eckersley was then unanimously re-elected people's warden. Mr. Fargher nominated Alderman Hutchinson as his sidesman and Mr. Eckersley nominated Mr. Astley. THE FINANCES. Mr. H. N. HOUGHTON reported, that although they commenced the year with a balance due to the bank of £ll O. 9d., they had now a credit balance of .21 68.8 d. The balance sheet, which was considered very satis- factory, was adopted.</p>
        <p>OTHER ELECTIONS. • Messrs Houghton and Burgess were re-appointed lay representatives._ _</p>
        <p>The VICAR said it had been customary to elect two sidesmen to represent St. Hilda's mission. At present the mission was not in a workable condition because they had not, as yet. been able to secure the assistance of a curate. Re was doing his best to get, one and at the present moment be was in communication with a gentleman who might pos- sibly come to work amongst them in September. It was impossible to carry on the servives at St. Hilda's now, but he hoped that at the beginning of next year, at any rate, the mission would be in a workable state and have a good congregation- of its own. The club was still being conducted in the building almost every night. It was decided npt to elect sidesmen for St. Hilda's. Voting by ballot resulted in the appointment of the following sidesmen for St. James's :—Messrs. T. Tonge, S. Acton, A. Rathbone, T. Morris, J. Carruthers and J.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>ST. PETER'S cHuazH.</head>
        <p>ST. PETER'S cHuazH.</p>
        <p>The annual vestry meeting in connection with this church was held on Wednesday evening, the Vicar (Rev. B. Percival) presiding. There were also present the Rev. D. R. James (curate), Captain Langton (Church Army), Councillor John Roberts (vicar's warden), Mr. Jose-51i Balinforth (people's warden), Messrs. Joseph Taylor, J. J. Hinds, B. Burdett, P. Green,James Taylor T. Reddish, W. H. Williams, John Taylor, P. Gleave Joseph Heron, F. Webster, W. Stanley, F. Lashford and P. Edgar (vestry clerk). EtNA.NCIAL.</p>
        <p>Mr. BA.LIIFORTH presented the balance sheet which showed total receipts of £lB3 19s. id., being about £lO less than last year. There was a balance in hand of 19s. 4d. Mr. Balmforth explained that the cost of the incandescent lights (£7 195.) had not been entirely de- frayed, the amount yet to be paid being £4 4s. 9d. The balance sheet was passed. T TIE VICAR REVIEWS THE YEAR'S WORK.</p>
        <p>• The VICAR said ha was glad to congratulate the church wardens on the satisfactory condition of the finances. Considering it was a rather shorter year than last year and work had not been so very good the income was very satisfactorl.. In a wcirking class parish like that, poor employment certainly affected their offertories, and he had not the slightest doubt that if work had been as good as last year, they would have been able to show money to the good. He was perfectly certain the decrease in income was not due to any falling off in the congregation. The extra work undertaken included the incandescent lighting and the painting of the outside of the church and vicarage. The collections for church expenses were considerably above last year. It was only in the special collections that they had fallen off. They had increased their con- tributions to home and foreign missons by at least V.O. Looking back upon the past, they had every reason to be thankful that they had done so well. During the coming year they ought to try and raise something for the Liverpool Cathedral Fund. A poor parish like their's could not be expected to give a large sum, but he thought £25 would be a suitable amount. He hoped the Bishop would visit them in September and he would like to be able to hand his lordship a cheque for £25. In conclusion the Vicar thanked his colleagues for their loyal and hearty support the wardens, sidesmen, organist and deputy organist, and all who had rendered assistance. He specially acknow- ledged the valuable help received from outside sub- scribers. They had had good congregations during the year, and the communicants had been very regular and numerous. He felt that the Church of St: Peter, although it was hidden away in a back street was fulfill- ing a useful mission in the midst of a large population of WOrking.people.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>-ELECTION OV_OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>-ELECTION OV_OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>The VICAR re-nominated Councillor Roberts as his warden. _• _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS a cknowledged the compliment and referred to hiq long associations with the church. On the proposition of Mr. Joseph Taylor, seconded by Mr. Hands, Mr. Joseph, Rilmforth was re-elected people's warden. The folowing sidesmen were elected :—Messrs. Joseph Taylor, T. Cowdell, R. Knight, J. J. Hands, James Taylor, T. Armstrong, and B. Clayton (nominated by Councillor Roberts) ;W. Stanley (nominated by M. Balmforth) ; P. Green, R. Burdett, T. S. Steel, F. T. Lashford, J. Heron, John Taylor,Thomas Reddish \V. H. Williams, and Harry Hebbes(appointed by the meeting.) Alderman HEATON' and Nfr. J. W. Kay were elected lay representatives, and Messrs. T. S. Steel and F. Webster auditors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>HOLY TRINITY.</head>
        <p>HOLY TRINITY.</p>
        <p>The Vicar (R3v. E. C. E. Carleton) presided at the Easter Vestry meeting which was held on Tuesday evening. There were also present :—.Messrs. J. H. Halton, T. Welsby, R. W. Rowson, John Hesketh, H. Ferguson, E. Rose, Shirlaw, E. Dyke, Joseph Hesketb, P. Randall, A. Walsh, G. F. F. White (vestry clerk), and others.—Mr. Halton submitted tho balance sheet, which showed that the income during the year had been £292 15s. Oid., as against £296 Pis. 20. the previous year. The year closed with a balance in hand of Bs. 3d. The apparent decreAse in the amount of the receipts was doubdess due, he said, to the fact that during the chtt-ch year just closed there had only been 51 Sundays, as against 52 in the previous year. The special offertories had been heavier during the year and had amounted to £l2l ss. Gd. in the previous year against £96 13s. 11d. The general offertory had been £l3l 16s. 9d. as against £146 6s. 10d, in the previous year.—On the motion of Mr:Rovvson, seconded by 'Mr. Rose, the balance sheet was approved.—The balance sheet of the Weedall charity was submitted by Mr. Rowson, and showed a balance in hand of 7s. Gd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>APPOINTMENT OF OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>APPOINTMENT OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>The VICIR re-appointed Mr. Haltonas his warden for the ensuing year.—On the motion of Mr. John Hesketh, seconded by Mr. Rowson, Mr. T. Welsby was unanimously re-eledted people's warden.—The Vicar's warden appointed Mr J.Harding as his sidesman,and Mr. Welsby appointed Mr. R. W. Rowson as his sidesman. The sidesmen were appointed as follows :—Messrs. James Bowcock, E. Dyke, John Dunn, H. Ferguson, J. Gerrard, W. Gill, George Gill,John Hesketh,J. Hignett, T. Hunt, H. Milling, jun., H. W. Naylor, E. Rose, W. J. Forshaw. Shirlaw, G.F.F. White, A. Walsh, and Pickles. —Mr. White was re-elected vestry clerk, Mr. Naylor auditor,and Messrs. Rowson and NV. Gill, lay delegates.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>RE-SEATING OF THE GALLERY.</head>
        <p>THE RE-SEATING OF THE GALLERY.</p>
        <p>Before the conclusion of the meeting Mr. Welsby said although they at Holy Trinity had always striven to keep out of debt, they were at present in debt to the extent of £1,030, in the shape of a mortgage on the Vicarage and the parish room. He felt that an effort should be made to clear off the debt and he moved that a committee be formed of the clergy, churchwardens and sidesmen, with power to add to their number, to take intp consideration the question of the re-seating of the gallery and the clear- ing off of the debt to which he had referred.— The Vicar quite agreed with the remarks made by Mr. Welsby. In the year 1909 they would be celebrating the two hundredth anniversary of the consecration of their church, and he thought it would be well if they took steps to get everything suggested by Mr. Welsby completed by ;that year. He would like to mention another matter, and that was the erection of a large sized assembly room on the land which they possessed and which adjoined the schools. He would like to see that done and thought that that question could be considered along with the suggestions which had been made by Mr. Welsby. (Hear, hear).—Mr. Halton said the scheme had his heartiest support and he seconded Mr. Welsby's motion.—Mr. Welsby incorporated the suggestion made by the Vicar in his motion and on its being put to the meeting it was heartily carried.—There was no other business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>ST. JAMES'S, LATCHFORD.</head>
        <p>ST. JAMES'S, LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>The vestry meeting was held in the schoolroom on Thursday evening, the Vicar (Rev. H. B. Firth) presid- ing. The attendance included Mr. H. N. Houghton (vicar's warden) and Mr. Eckersley (people's warden). The Vicar announced that Mr. C. S. Burgess was unable to be present owing to illness. That was the first meeting he had missed for twenty years and they all regretted his absence.</p>
        <p>THE VICAR'S THANKS. APPOINTMENT OF WARDENS. The VicAn said he was very glad to have the oppor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>ST. JOHN'S WALTON.</head>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S WALTON.</p>
        <p>The vestry meeting in connection with this church was held on Monday morning, the Vicar presiding, and there were also present Messrs. William Weir, Williani Turner, John Cooper, and Alfred Mom Male (apparitor). The accounts for the past year were submitted, and showed a balance in hand of about £6. The Vicar nominated Sir Gilbert Greenall as his warden, and on the proposition of Mr. John Cooper, seconded by Mr. Turner, Mr. Williatn Weir was re-elected people's warden.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>CON.QUERED AT LAST.</head>
        <p>CON.QUERED AT LAST.</p>
        <p>Rheumatism is caused by Uric Acid in the system. Kidney failure is responsible for the presence of this Uric Acid. No man or woman with sound Kidneys can have Rheumatism, and no case of Rheumatism can continue when the Kidneys have been cured. Mr. A. Mills, of 3, Belmant Cottages, Ludgershall, Andover, Hants, tells how he was cured of Rheumatism, as follows :</p>
        <p>"I served two and a half years in South Africa, and when I came home I returned to my work, which com- pells me to be out in all weathers. The hardships I endured wnile serving with the colours had under- mined my constitution, and I became subject to severe attacks of Rheumatism, caused, as I afterwards found, by my Kidneys being out of order. I tried different remedies, but nothing seemed to give me relief until I took Dodd's Kidney Pills. I was surprised at the relief they gave me, for they seemed to act on my Kidneys at once, and I soon got rid of the Rheumatism. I can recommend Dodd's Kidney Pills to all sufferers, for theY have quite cured me."</p>
        <p>Dodct's Kidney Pills have conquered Rheumatism in every case where they have been given a fair trial.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>CHARGE AGAINST A BREWER'S 'TRAVELLER.</head>
        <p>CHARGE AGAINST A BREWER'S 'TRAVELLER.</p>
        <p>The Sentence. Joseph George Harrison (38), traveller, was indicted on three counts at the Cheshire Quarter Sessions, held at Knutsford on Tuesday, with having embezzled money belonging to his employers, Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., brewers, and with having falsified the books in his charge as their representative at Macclesfield. He was represented by Mr. Trevor Lloyd, and pleaded guilty. Mr. Lance Bentley prosecuted, and said the prisoner entered the employ of the prosecutors fifteen years ago, and during the whole time of his service, he had Possessed the confidence of his employers. \ The charge against him was of a most serious kind, so serious, in fact, that it was impossible for anyone looking after their own interests to look over it with- out some sort of trial of this kind. Two years ago the prisoner had been appointed the firm's representative at Macclesfield, and as such received about £344 per year for his various duties, besides being allowed travell- ing expenses. Towards the end of last year his weekly statements almost ceased to come to Wilderspool, and his firm thought it very desirable to have him before them to give an account of how he was carrying on his , business. Unfortunately, when the prisoner did put in an appearance he was in such a state as not to be able to go into details of what he had been doing, and his employers thought the best thing to do was to give him a holiday for a fortnight, and send a man to overlook the books. The prisoner promised to stay in Macclesfleid,anda man was sent to Macclesfield to look into affairs. When he got there he found things in such a wretched state that he thought the better thing would be to take the books to the headquarters at Warrington. An accountant was called in,and it was found that almost from the moment of his appointment as the Macclesfield representative he had falsified his accounts. When this discovery was made , the prisoner bolted. A warrant was issued for his arrest, and it was not until March Ist that he was apprehended at Jersey. The amount which he had mis- appropriated totalled £439, but notwithstanding that fact, the firm did not wish to be vindictive ; but they could not overlook the fact that the offences had been going on so long. Mr. Trevor Lloyd, for the defence, spoke of Mr. Harrison's previous excellent character. The prisoner had assured him that the whole of the money had been spent in advancing the interests of the firm and not in benefiting himself at all. He had spent the firm's money for the firm's benefit and not for his own. Some time before he absconded he had written to the flint stating that his accounts were in such a hopeless muddle that he did not know what to do; so it had been himself who had let it be known first that things were wrong ; and he had told them that they had become what they were through his endeavouring to increase the firm's business. ince his arrest in Jersey he had been in gaol for nearly a month. Consequently he had suffered much, for he was not an ordinary criminal, and Mr. Trevor Lloyd appealed to the Bench to deal as leniently as they possibly could with him. • The Chairman (Sir Horatio Lloyd),said the Bench took into consideration the fact of the prisoner having been a trusted servant before the offences, and that he had conducted himself well, and though he (the Chair- man) felt obliged to pass a substantial sentence upon prisoner it was in reality a very light one. The sen- tence of the Court was that prisoner be sent to gaol for six months on the first indictment, and a day each on the other two. CATARRH AND INFLUENZA.</p>
        <p>CATARRH is the result of a series of Golds; the symptoms are :—Nose stopped up, difficulty in breath- ing, loose cough, with continual hawking and expector- ating. VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE, quickly cures this condition. INFLUENZA is epidemic and contagious, most prevalent in low-lying districts in spring months. During the severe epidemic which raged in North America, in 1897, VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE was the remedy chiefly used, and it reduced the death rate to such an extent that doctors never hesitated to pre- scribe it. It is peculiarly well adapted to the speedy cure of both Influenza and Catarrh. Ask for VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE. Price 90., is. Id., and. 2s. td., at Chemists and Drug Stores everywhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>• ; ,1 ----.,-__ – lIIIIIIki .;. –</head>
        <p>• ; ,1 ----.,-__ - lIIIIIIki .;. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Denmark Queen's own Country—The Royal gathering in L'ellmark for the celebration of King Christian's eighty- Sixth birthday reminds us that our relations with the Dines were not always of the happy kind they have be- ,Trrig since Queen Alexandra came amongst us, more larla forty years ago. The ties of affection which then ;Prang up between the two nations have long since ob- literated any bitter memories which Lord Nelson's vic- tory at Copenhagan might have left behind. That was the 2nd April, 1801, and, had it not been forgotten,it nuld scarcely:, have been possible for King Edward to !'lave spent the anniversary in the Danish capital, the honoured guest of King Christian. The Danes however always claimed that Nelson was, by descent, one of themselves, and for that reason they the more readily aookited their naval overthrow. Tennyson they also say u-as of Scandinavian origin but both were at any rate children of the parsonage, which has given us so many of our greatest men and women in history. The Oldest Peeress.—The Dowager Duchess of Aber- corn, who has been laid up with an attack of bronchitis at Coates Castle, her seat in Sussex is the oldest living Peeress, and is happily recovering from her illness. The ,da, lighter of the sixth Earl of Bedford she was bornmore ran 91 years ago, when George 111 was King, and lVaterloo had still to be fought. The venerable Duchess bas thus lived hi five reigns, and her life story is a re- markable one in many ways. She was marrie.l nearly seventy-two years ago, and enjoyed over half a century of married life. To haveAived to see her grandchild's grandchild is a privilege permitted to few indeed, and for some years past her birthday has been the occasion °,,t. ,ua gathering of her descendants, who number some- 4;Lng like 200 ,and comprise with the Dowager Duchess,no 'ewer tfian five generations. It would be impossible to say how many dukes, duchesses, countesses, and people Of title therinclude, but her descendants would alone constitute a numerous peerage in themselves. Cottage Industries.—The several associations for the Promotion of cottage industries in different parts of the country may always count upon the ready assistance of society women, many of whom are, as usual, lending Purpose London houses for exhibitions and sales for this "urPose. There is to be one for the sale of the produce of the lace-makers of Buckingham and Devonshire, for Which Lady Esther Smith has lent her house in Gros- senor Place. For the annual sale and exhibition,organ- lsed by the Welsh Industries Association, whieh will be held on June 9th, Lady Naylor-Leyland has lent her Mansion at Albert Gate. This will afford the many People interested in Welsh handiwork, an opportunity ,Of seeing one of the finest houses in London, and it is 'loped the Princess of Wales will be able to open the exhibition. . Pinery for Men.—The prospect would be rather alarm- leg if the protests against the uniformity of dress for men were to be taken seriously. Just now there seems be an epidemic of these complaints, and Sir William Richmond is only one of many well known public men Who are urging some or other departure from the ii?orlotonous style of the masculine attire. The incen- tive to most of these suggestions is the hankering after colours, and some of these people, wko boast of their artistic tastes, would speedily„turn men into peacocks, /..I,lore gorgeous than even the Highlander in his full tiress uniform. Needless to say this would introduce a new problem for women, as the wife wohld have to first consult what the husband was going to wear, and vice rersa. One trembles to think of the fearful contrasts of colours that would be seen in the streets,ifithe ordinary ciao were given full liberty to go about in finery, of his OW n choosing. Enough of this is seen at the seaside resorts in the summer time,whenthe youth of the period takes to his holiday costume, and his " blazers " of the most highlw • coloured and fantastic kind. fiFashionable Headgear.—ln the millinery world, Z.?.l,vers reign supreme, and these are either of the 44,Sliest, or largest varieties available. With very few thr Miens the tiny flowers are the more successful, but need an experienced hand to scatter them over the 'l'cui or straw shapes, and the home worker would do to leave all such millinery attempts severely alone. fle best milliners build all their own shapes, and their ;.elv straws though very beautiful, run a little too large for the average woman. Only a tall girl can wear a their hat successfully, an item both the milliners and customers are apparently overlooking at the pre- ejent time. The straw braids most in favour, hail from aPnn, and the beauty and brightness of the designs, excel anything yet seen. .Spring Wraps.—The sleeves of all the new coats are of very ample proportions, but nevertheless many vromen object to the small amount of crushing inflicted these coverings upon the dress sleeves, and aro there- fore patronising the simple all-round cape, which for the past two or three seasons has been little in evidence. These capes and coats are all in the palest pastel colours, Which, with all tones of white are more than ever the vogue this spring. For useful' morning wear, the three- quarter semi-fitting coat, finished with some dainty embroidery at the throat, will be correct, but this should match the skirt with which it is worn.</p>
        <p>• Jerseys.—The new knitted jerseys are more fascinat- ing than ever before,and with a serge walking skirt, and aeat straw hat, look charming for morning wear. Some are knitted in one coloured wool 'only, but those in check or stripe patterns are more dressy, provided of course the colours are repeated in the skirt,and headgear. These jersey, are all shaped carefully at the waist. and many have long revers in contrasting or plain colours finished with herring-bone or knitted,wool. Some button !a front, othersat theside or back, and the buttons are la all cases very ornamental. To the holiday girl and woman, these knitted jerseys will prove a very real comfort. Their price too is moderate, and they wear sPlendidly. Servants' Characters.—Suppressing the truth in ser- Pnts' " characters "is the cause of trouble' in many uouseholds, and it would be a good thing if Um) mis- chief could be checked by legislation. The False -C,haracters Bill, has been introduced in the House of 'ramons with this object in view, and if it should be- come law, it would not only severely punish fraud, but also make it an indictaile offence for a master or mis- tress to tamper with the truth in giving servants' char- acters. It is a very common failing to gloss over the shortcomings of a discharged servant even amongst 1133:°Ple of the highest principles. It is done out of cevolence, but is not any real kindness to the ser- l'aat, and it is certainly an injustice to the new master e'r mistress. The best 'way is, of course, always to seek ea interview with former employers, and not trust to a written character when engaging a servant, but this is ;lot always possible,and it will be all to the good if the laW insists upon a more strict regard for truth •in these Matters. , Japanese Story.—Mr. W. S. Gilbert in "The tlikado " represents Yum-Ymn as looking at herself in cPe glass and saying "Yes, I am indeed beautiful. '3ometimes I sit and wonder in my artless Japanese Zay, why it is that I am so much more attractive than uillYbcdy else in the whole world." This is funny, but „ e whole comedy is almost paralleled by a passage in r,-ce of the reports of the Church Missionary Society. 'llloted by Sir Richard Temple. A missionary :poke to a • allege lady about all bein., sinners, whereupon she trat into uncontrollable laughter. "I do beg your Ird°ll," she said," but I a sinner ! It is really too ridiculous." MADGE.</p>
        <p>ATTEMPTED SUICIDE AT RUNCORN. At the Runcorn Police Court on Saturday, befor Ilessrs. James Pritchard and E. Imison, Julia Cooke alton-road, Runcorn, was charged with attempting to _e°. 'Wait suicide by throwing herself into the Bridge- canal on Friday night.—Harry Moores, a Ship .k.7;anal constable' said he heard a cry of someone in the `,.!fter near the Custom House about ten o'clock on 13.tf day night. He proceeded to the spot and found the t risoner struggling in the water. He got her out and 11).1k her to the Bridgwater House and rendered first aid. E141%' tl",len took her to her parents'home.—Sergeant Bratt aa„l ne arrested the woman and charged her with having p',lseinpteil. to commit suicide. She made no reply.— ner now said that she went to the canal on pur- "se to drown herself. She was remanded for a week</p>
        <p>A GUARRANTEED CURE FOR PILES. lltching, blind, bleeding, or protruding. All chemists t!eauthorised by the manufacturers of Pazo Pile Oint- clent to refund the money where it fails to cure any err of Piles, no matter of bow long standing. Cures iirjlnarY cases l'n six days. One application gives ease rest- This is a new discov cry, an,d is the only Pile he e'ilY sold on apositive guarantee. Price 2s. 3d. of all 241sts, orLirect from PARIS MEDICINE CO. Dept. S,) hoe-lane, London, E.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY,</head>
        <p>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY,</p>
        <p>There is nothing smarter for morning weal than a tailor-made gown, and this season there are many such to be seen every day, which serves to show off the charms of a neat figure to greater advantage. There are, how- ever, two distinct classes of tailor-mades, namely, the severely orthodox style, whick requires a really good figure to carry it ofl successfully, and is best suited to morning wear, and the more elaborate gown which. although of tailor origin, is generally adorned with a considerable amount of trimming and is tucked or gauged liberally. This mode is too elaborate for the early hours of the day as it is distinctly designed for the afternoon. Tailors have nowadays condescended to</p>
        <p>fART TOILETTE.</p>
        <p>adopt elaborate styles which a short time age would have been considered ,by them as only within the province of a dressmaker. The short walking skirt will be seen this spring to great advantage as the comfort ensured by a skirt cut to clear the ground all round is so great that its continued popularity is ensured. Some of these skirts are made with pleats, which are deep at the hem but diminish as they go upwards till they conclude in very tiny pleats at the waist, a style undoubtedly suited to figures at all inclined to embonpoint. Another short cut skirt fits the figure closely to the knees and has then some 'extra fulness inserted by means of pleats. My first sketch shows a smart toilette in which the skirt is rendered fuller round the feet by inserted pleats but is not perfectly tight fitting above as it is gauged at either side of the waist. The coat is exceedingly modish, while the full sleeves are one of the features of this season's gowns, for the cuffs are not tight, as heretofore, but loose and are finished by ruffles of lace which fall over the hands. The deep collar is finished at the outer edge by rows of stitching and is further enhanced by motifs of cream lace, which are also used to ornament the cuffs. The material employed is brown canvas ov'er coffee - coloured silk, the waist being finished by a folded band of the silk and a blouse to match inserted with lace and adorned with numerous tiny tucks is worn</p>
        <p>e a ...:•,..„ I`,ll , ti it' irf' . 1•:: i - \ 1 \ . • • A SM</p>
        <p>re 1- Cia • ks ' ; .0:11,11,7-,.. _ • A,., 714.1' Atrifirr7 ' kVS.E-rde”..sfAlirt 4 f ' di'''''.:rf• - . -s. .. k S • •• 'S ' • s. • • . • % ~, S. s, • 0 ' If' I 1 \\A) •</p>
