      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>SHOULDER CAPE.</head>
        <p>A SHOULDER CAPE.</p>
        <p>tendency to drapery at the back threatens to become extreme, and we hear of yetis which fall almost to the edge of the skirt at the backki. There is something very un-English about these loose veils, however becoming they may be, and those which are left to hang loose over the face in front and are embellished by a deep border are still more so. They are also fat from easy to adjust and are apt to look ridiculous on a windy day. • The shoulder cape sketched is a pretty adjunct of dress whose merits will be appre- ciated as the season advances and the wea- ther, becoming warmer, forces us to abandon our furs, to which, however, we cling fondly to the last possible moment. The shoulder cape or ruffle comes in usefully to fill the blank, and when made of black pleated chine silk spotted with black velvet and finished by strands of black chenille is suited to all ages of woman. These dainty wraps are more durable than the marabout or ostrich feather boas and keep more easily in place, as a boa requires much care to adjust it success- fully and cannot be said to be very com- fortable when worn with a walking costume. They look, however, extremely well when worn as a finish to a carriage toilette and will be greatly in evidence with dainty dresses throughout summer.</p>
        <p>COQUETTEi</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday April 95 1904. L RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS. More Discussion about ~,Workhouse Music. The monthly meeting of this Board was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday afternoon, Mr. J. Stokes presiding. There were also present :—Messrs. A. R. 'Norman (vice-chairman), S. Woodward, T. Baxter, T. Dallington, C. Roylance, G. Pilling, T. Lea, T. Wright, T. Clarke, Wallington. E. Lewis, J. L. Jones, J. Moun- field, J. T. Collier, J. Edwards, G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (deputy clerk), W. Cook, T. Riley, and W. de Prez (relieving officers), and J. Cunnah (workhouse master).</p>
        <p>MUSIC FOR THE WORKHOUSE INMATES. Miss Bessie Dutton, of Moore, wrote on behalf of the Ladies' Committee thanking the Board for their kind • consideration of the plant) scheme which they felt was ; a useful one. The funds already obtained amounted to '£62s Od but the ladies hoped to add substantially to that if the Guardians would kindly encourage them by making a grant. They thought it would be much more satisfactory to obtain a new instrument which could be had from £l7 to .£2O. A secondhand piano if cheap was always on the verge of break-down if not already in a state that would involve constant expense for repair. The chairman remarked that if the idea was to have a collection, it would be best to defer it until the first meeting of the new board, when they would have a "full house." Mr. Woodward: The old members can leave their subscriptions. (Hear, hear). Mr. Wallington: I don't know whatever we • Guardians are coming to. If we have to provide music for the inmates, I should at once propose that we get one of the best dancing masters in Paris to come and teach them. (Laughter). I think it is a great shame • to spend money in that way. Mr. Collier moved that the matter be deferred until the next meeting. Mr. Roylance seconded. Mr. Wright suggested that the Clerk should send circulars to the retiring and present members of the Board asking for subscriptions. Mr. Woodward said he would strongly oppose any proposition to give a subscription from the public funds. The Guardians might personally support the scheme. Mr. Norman said they could not /make a grant from the public funds without the sanction of the Local Government Board. He believed it ought to be a voluntary business. (Hear, hear.) At the same time the piano would be supplying a need, but the only point was whether it would be possible to obtain a good second-hand instrument at a much cheaper price than a new one. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Edwards said the ladies had mooted the scheme ' and the choice of. a piano ought to te left entirely ; with them. I It was agreed to defer the matter until the next ; meeting and to issue a circular as suggested by Mr. Wright. Mr. Darlington thought the Guardians should be in- vited to appeal to their constituents for subscriptions, as he saw no reason why the members only should be , asked to find all the extras." I Mr. Norman pointed out that it was hardly correct to say that the Guardians only -,,ere appealed to. The I ladies had, already raised £5 or £6.</p>
        <p>THE EMIGRATION OF CHILDREN. I A letter was read from the Sheltering Home, Myrtle- ! street, Liverpool, an institution certified by the Local I Government Board for the training and emigration of children from the care of Guardians. It was stated that the institution had emigrated boys and girls for numerous Boards of Guardians and if the Runcorn Guardians desired to emigrate such of their charges as were suitable for colonial life the institution would be pleased to place them in dnada. Where workhouses were overcrowded this was an advantageous way of meeting the difficulty, while for the children's own pros- pects emigration under careful auspices was infinitely betar physically, morally and socially than remaining in England. NEW NURSES. The Master reported that with the consent of the Medical Officer he had engaged Nurse Ada Hitchmough, of Runcorn, and Nurse Flora Jones, of Birkenhead. Nurse Duncan and Nurse Muir had left. The appointments were confirmed. APPLICATION FOR INCREASE OF SALARY. Miss E. Hayes, female industrial trainer, applied for an increase of salary. Her present salary is £25 per annum. She stated that she was appointed at this salary and had completed four and a half years' service. The matter was referred to the House Committee. TENDER ACCEPTED. The Board accepted the tender of Messrs. Kydd, of Frodsham, for the supply of butter and eggs to the House during the next six months. THE RECENT POOR LAW CONFERENCE. BOARDING OUT AND OTHER IMPORTANT QUESTIONS. Mr. T. Wood, who was unable to be _present, wrote giving some Personal impressions of the Poor Law Con- ference. He expressed his warm sympathy with the address given by the president of the Local Government Boardwhich strongly advocated the taking of the children out of the workhouses and pointed out the mistake of looking at the matter from a monetary point of view. The writer contended that this confirmed his statements made at a meeting of the Board of Guardians that children who were brought up in the Workhouse looked upon it as their home and saw no disgrace in returning to it in after years. A paper pointing out the advantages of training ships impressed Mr. Wood, but he pointed out that all boys were not adapted to becoming sailors. A paper was also read on the combination of Unions for the purpose of classification, and which advocated the building of Cottage Homes for the children. Mr. Wood concluded by expressing the hope that steps would be taken in the direction advocated by the President of the Local Government Board. - -</p>
        <p>The report of the delegates to the Conference (Messrs, S. Woodward,T. Wood and the Clerk) was pre- sented. The Conference was presided over by Mr. W. W. Long, president of the Local GoverAment Board. Mr. Geoffrey Drage read a paper on the advantages of training ships, in which he strongly advocated the sending of all poor law boys,who were willing to go,to such ships. Mr. H. P. Cleaver, clerk to the West Derby Union, read a paper on the "Combination of Unions for the purpose of the classification of the indoor poor," in which he advocated the removal of the imbecile, epileptic and feeble-minded and the children from work- houses and the grouping of the Unions for their better care. Miss Brodie Hall, of the Eastbourne Union, read a paper on" The case paper or history sheet system." She strongly adovcated its use and stated that by its adoption, the Bethnal Green Union and Exeter Union had effected considerable saving. The delegates advised the consideration of the adop- tion of the system. cind recommended the appointment of a small committee to go into the matter. Mr. Woodward presented an interesting report on the conference, and dealt at some length with the question of the boarding out of children. It had been suggested in that room, that if the children were removed, the Workhouse would be a deadly dull place. That objec- tion he considared to be sentimental and the Board ought only to view the matter in the best interests of the children. .</p>
        <p>Several members expressed complete satisfaction with the delegates' reports, but Mr. Edwards complained that nothing tangible accrued from the conference. The reports were adopted. ATTENDANCES OF MEMBERS. The Clerk submitted the following returns of attend- ances of members during the year, the maximum being 26 :--Messrs. J. L. Jones (out of a possible 12), 12, Roylance 19, Greenway 3, Bolshaw 15, Knowles 6, Talbot, 1, Clarke 25,McIntyre 12, Stokes 19,Whitlow s,Darling- ton 22,Baxter 19, Linaker 14, Moseley-Williams 14,Lewis 21,Bradford 3, Wright 25, Mawdsley 12, Collier 11, Wood- ward 25, Ellison 0, Pilling 24, Houghton 5, Bretherton 12, Edwards 21, Nickson 6, Baguley 4, Spruce 18, Taylor 10, Lea 18, Wood 23, Edwards 12, Reynolds 0, Norman 22, Eaton 3, Davenport 0, Billington 12, Wallington 2, Mounfield 20, rurner 4,Chadwick 7, Guest 11, Antwis 15, Bev. Dr. Gastaldi 5, Admiral J. P. Jones Parry 11.</p>
        <p>FINANCIAL. Mr. Woodward reported that the expenditure on the recent Guardians elections was £36 us. sd. The balance carried forward last month amounted to X 2,358145. 5d.. the receipts to k 300 6s. 5d., and thepayments to £1,289 18.. 3d., leaving a eredit balance of £1,369 2s. 7d. The report was adopted. MR. MCINTYRE'S RETIREMENT. Mr. Norman alluded to the retirement of Mr. W. Mclntyre who with one exceptionewas the oldest mem- ber of the board, having been connected with it about forty years. He moved that the board express its regret at Mr. Mclntyre's retirement, and its best wishes for his future happiness. Mr. Woodward seconded and also alluded to the re- tirement of another valued and respected member, Mr. Moseley Williams. It was decided that the Clerk should write to Messrs. Mclntyre and Williams in the terrus suggested by Mr. Norman. Appreciative reference was also made to the services rendered by Messrs. Darlington, Wood and Edwards, who also retire from the Board. A hearty vote of thanks to the Chairman and vice- chairman concluded the meeting. •Inefiaba.A.veamasusam-</p>
