        <p>THE MISSION TO HUSBAND'S expedition THIBET. is still " wropt in</p>
        <p>myst'ry." Explana- tions in Parliament before the adjournment failed to make it clear why Lord Cußzom deemed it expedient to force a "commercial treaty" upon Thibet at the point of the bayonet. All that is positively known about the opinion of the Thi- betans is that they do not want to have any sort of relations with the Indian Government. China is nominally their suzerian, but they have no great relish for interference even from China. The Chinese envoy has explained to Colonel YOUNG- HUSBAND that he could not hasten to meet the British Mission because the Lamas had rebsed him transport. This may be a diplomatic gloss upon his own unwill- ingness to make Colonel YOUNGHUSBAND'S acquaintance. It is pointed out that the envoy is of a cliff:trent mind since the fighting; but it. can- not be supposed that the loss of some hundreds of IThibetan lives has secured for us a cordial welcome. I Hitherto Thibet has been pretty nearly the only country in Asia secure against European intrusion. Russia, it is co-nplained, has sent an agent there but she has not sent an expedition. If her agent has done any harm, which is extremely proble- matical, it is at least remarkable that the mischief which one Russian can contrive takes a whole British expedition to ando. Even then there is no certainty that it is undone. It may be argued that the recent conflict was not provoked by us; but we must take the responsibility for what is tantamount to an invasion of Thibet. Is our influence there, associated, as it will be, with blood-stained memories, likely to prevail over the Russian influ- ence which has shed no blood ? Thibet,no doubt, is of more concern to us than it is to Russia, for it is close to the Indian frontier, and 1,000 miles from the nearest Russian territory. But this makes all the more signal the failure of the diplomacy which has to effect by force what Russia can apparently do by persuasion. The expedition does not appear to have been organised with much forethought. Something should be known in India about the climate of Thibet, but the military authorities did not reflect that at a height of 15,000 feet above the sea, oil ceases to act as a lubricant, and water will not boil in an open vessel. " Our men ought to have had pure glycerine," says a scientific correspondent of the " Times," " to lubricate the locks of their .rifles and Maxims. They ought to have had cook- ' ng-pots with air-tight lids, furnished with simple afety-valves blowing off at a pressure of 151 b. on the square inch. Then they would have had no trouble either with rifles or cooking." It may be objected that glycerine in the necessary quantities was not availahle. "Bit it appears that kerosene is carried by the expedition, and it seems a pity that there is no one to suggest that in the cir- cumstances it would be greatly preferable to oil as a lubricant." These are matters which " any tyro in phys!cal science" could have made clear to the military authorities, who are quite content to act on the assumption that water can be just as easily boiled on a snow peak as at the bottom of a valley. The correspondent of the Times is severe upon this illustration of "the appalling ignorance with which the affairs of this nation are conducted." The present Government has accustomed us to that. S 3 has the War Office. But we did cherish the belief that in India things were different. Indian officers must have had frequent occasion to adapt their cooking arrange- ments to the temperature of the Himalayas. What is the use of having a fine range of mountains if</p>
        <p>you learn nothing from them? Do the simple</p>
        <p>elements of chemistry form no part of an Indian officer's studies? Parhapi the real explanation of Lord Cuazorfs policy is that he deplored the appalling ign )ratice " of hia military advi.ers, and thought a little expedition to Tnibet might teach them something.</p>
        <p>The Town Council will meet on Tuesday f4r the presentation of the annual budget and the fixing of the rates for the ensuin4 year. la all probability a total call upon the ratep tyors of 7s. 101. in the pound will be sanctioned, thus in tkin4 an increase of 6d. on the rates for the past twelre months. The increase really cones on the ltnorovement Rate, which is being raised fro la 34. to 3s. 61., but there are fluctuAtions in the Poor Rite and Sswers Rate which balancs etch other. The former is being raised from 4s. to 45.. Id., while the Sewers Rate decreases from 4d. to 2d., the net increase being, as above stated, 6d. in the pound. A glance at the estimates of expenditure shows an increase all round, the various departments all finding that they will require a little more m may than formerly to carry out the work entrusted to them. Still we do not think there is any tendency to ex- travagance,and the F mance Committee has plainly been keeping a sharp aye on the items which the different Committeeshave submitted, for a nuinb,ir amounting to £1,878 have been struck out. The total expenditure on the Borough R-tte account is put down at £43,900 194. sd, with £3,362 124.101 due to the bankers, making in all £47,263 12s. 3d. Receipts amounting to £9,749 are allowed for, leaving £37,514 12s. 31. as toe call upon the rate, against £29,467 14 93. for last year. On behalf of the Education Authority there is put down £10,407 19s. 51 agtinst £9,290. twelve months ago. The Improveinent Rate is has sd on a total of £35,128 is. 5d., against £29,654 6:. 41. a year ago, and the S ,wers Rate £1.777 104. aottist 22,609.165. id. it is, we suppose, too much to hope for lighter rates just yet, but with the call only 2d. short of Bs. in the pound there is the most emphatic n .el for the practics of every possible economy compatible with efficiency.</p>
        <p>Were it not for the seriousness of the issue, the ramifications of what is generally known as "The Slack Coutract Scandal " would be worthy of a place in comic opera. The examination of officials still continues, awl the latest revelation by the Engineer at Winwick is that " mimes the wird " was the nature of his instructions from the Water Engine,r. When a.)ked for an explanation, Mr. Greenway tells us that this instruction applied merely to information respecting the scarcity of water. The Committee w:11 perhaps have their own opinion as to 11,)%v far the Engineer at Winwick was to remain" mum." In any case it is suffici- ently serious that the members of the Committee when making a visit to Winwick were tn be pur- posely kept in the dark as to the state of the water supply, esp catty see n 4 that the supply has on several occasions very nearly failed to ;meet the demand. It becomes more and more apparent that thei management of the Water Department has been:far from satisfactory and we sincerely hope thau the unpleasant revelations of the past few months will have the effect of making the Water Committee and its chief officials see the error of their ways. There must ;be no such cloaking of the affairs of a public department as is indicated by "mum's the word."</p>
        <p>Our columns have on several occasions,recorded the fact that Warrington's death rate was the highest in the Kingdom for that particular week. Why Warrington should have taken to beating records in this, unpleasant direction we do not know, hut it has been freely suggested that much of the infectious disease which has been prevalent in the town for the .last twelve months is due to the insanitary condition of streets and passages. This is a serious reflection on both the Health,and Paving and Sewerage Committees, and we ara glad to note that the Health Committee, at an rate, is not going to ignore the criticism, just or unjust as it may be. The Committee has resolved "That the Medical Officer of Health be instructed to prepare a statement showing the number of cases of infectious disease during the past 12 months, with the name of the street where the patient resided, and also whether the street and passage adjoining the house of the patient were paved." A return of this kind will show at once whether the charge laid at the door more particularly of the Paving and Sewerage Committee is founded upon fact. If the particulars prove that there is an actual and obvious relationship between the badly-paved streets and infectious disease, then the committees concerned will have a serious responsibility placed upon them. Their capacity, too, for coping with the difficulty will be seriously questioned, but this will not matter if it results in any extension or re-organisa- tion of the Departments concerned. We have had the opinion for some time that the growth of the town is overwhelming trio Paving and Sewer- age Committee. If this is the case it is high time the Council and the public were made acquainted with the true position of affairs.</p>
        <p>The M3yor drew rather a do'eful p'cture of the unhappy lot of the publican at the annual banquet in connection with the Warrington and District Licensed Victuallers' Association. To judge by the well-to-do app?arance of the gentlemen gathered round the festive board they seem to have prospered exceedingly, despite all the drawbacks which beset them and their calling, ilowever oppressive the licensing laws may be elsewhere, in Warrington at least, publicans cannot complain of unfair treatment at the hands of the local Bench. We have heard Warrington described as "a publican's paradise,'' but this may perhaps be a slight exaggeration. Nevertheless it is seldom that we hear of a publican being summoned for permitting drunkenness and yet it is clear from the sights which we constantly see in the !:public streets, that a great deal of drunkenness takes place. Then in regard to the suppression of unnecessary licences, the reduction in Warrington has been infinitesimal. On the other hand licenees have been freely transferred to new and populous districts, and enlargements of existing premises allowed without comment. No wonder the members of "The Trade" are benevolently inclined towards the Warrington bench. It is so amenable to persuasion at times that the brewer and the publican have cause to rejoice greatly, and when they are throwing their caps up it is high time for the temperance p ,rty to take its coat off.</p>
        <p>Captain G. R. Crosfield has no easy task before him in his endeavour to fix public interest locally of the proposals of the National Service League but we do not suppose he will be deterred in thb sfight- est by th 3 difficulties he has to encounter. On the contrary, the unsympathetic reception which he often meets mith only seems to stir Mr. Crosfield to greater qndeavours. Our readers will probably recollect the discussion in this. column batween Captain Crosfield and ourselves on the subjlet of compulsory service as advocated by the I.Hagne. At that time we were in strong opposition to Mr. Crosfield, and we are yet unconvinced of the neces- sity for general military training—on compulsory lines ! However, the euhject is one which seems bound to take a front place in national politics ere loner, and it behoves everybody to consider carefully what defensive measures are necessary for the nation's security. The local branch of the National Service League, of which Captain Cros- field is hon. secretary, has organised a public meeting in Parr Hall for next Friday evening. Lord Newton and Mr. George Shee, M.A.., will be the principal speakers and we hope the people of Warrington will attend in large numbers and give an attentive hearing to the arguments of the compulsory service advocates..</p>
        <p>Our old friend Mr. R. A. Naylor is a sceptic. At least he is sceptical as to the interest the Wets% people take in the Education Act, and he has this week unburdened himself to the " Standard as follows: "I have visited some five thousand houses in Carnarvon boroughs and -I have been astonished to flEd how little interest the electors take in the Education question, not one in twenty having mentioned the matter to me." Either the Carnarvon electors are a benighted body—which</p>
        <p>one can hardly credit seeing that they returned Mr. Lloyd George to Parliament—or else they were laughing up their sleeves when Mr. Naylor called. The fact that the Education Act is a dead !otter in the Principality is far more elo- quent and reliable testimony to the" interest" of the Welsh plople in Education than any Mr Naylor can give. Tney won't have the Bill and in all probability they won't have Mr. Naylor when the time comes. Poor man! But he will doubt- less be disillusioned some day.</p>
        <p>This week vestry meetings have occupied a large share ot public attention. In Warrington all the meeting., with one notable exception, have peen of a harmonious character. Tne exception is the vestry rneJLing of tile Warrington Parish Church. The mother church has shown suJh a good example in the past that the innovation this year is all the more regrettable. Differences arose on the vexed oue.tion of church ritual, and had reference more particularly to the recent inexoduction of altar lights by the Rector. Mr. F. G. Taylor voiced the opmion of auti-Ritualists in a dispassionate and well-reasoned sp,:ecli. His chief points are that the Rector has m tda the inno- vation without consulting the parishioners and in contravention of a promise made them than he would not do anything which would be con- trary to their wishes. Mr. Taylor also alludes to tie fact thai theßector has nut madean application for a faculty to introduce the ornaments. On the other hand, the Rector affirms that he has never consulted the ptrishioners on matters of doctrine or ritual and that there is no necessity to obtain a faulty. These are matters of ecclesiastical polity upon which there may be differences of opinion and perhaps the Archdeacon might be induced to express h s opinion upon them The Rector refused to take the sense of the meet ing on his action, and in doing so he was acting strictly within his legal rights. But it was evi- dent from the tone of the meeting that the altar lights are objectionable to a large section of .the parishioners, and it is questionable whether the object in this instance is "worth the candle". To the High Ohurchtnati, the two lightson the altar have a solemn signification, but there is no evidence that their absence from the Parish church for ages past has been a hindrance to devotion. There is distinct evidence that the introduction of these lights is objectionable to many of the par- ishioners and we should hardly deem it wise on the Rector's part to persist in the innovation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>A Handsome Easter Offering. The amount contributed by the congregation of Lymm Parish Church as an Easter offering to the Rector (Rev. Cl'._B. Thurston) on Sunday was £97.</p>
        <p>UNDER the CLOCK for GOOD BOOTS.—Advt. Ironworkers' Distress Fund. The Committee of the Ironworkers Distress Fund desire to thank all those friends who have so kindly' responded to their appeal for assistance during the present depression of the iron trade in the borough. They are also pleased to say that over two hundrel tickets for relief have been distributed to the most deserving families many of whim were on the verge of starvation. A. Retailed list of subscriptions will be published as early ae possible.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT. UNDER the CLOCK for BiseT ROCTS in LANCASHIRE.—AdVt. A Councillor's Conscientious Objection.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Curt on Saturday, Coun- cillor Fred Stott made application for a certificate eX- empting his child from vaccination.—He said big objection was that he thought vaccination would be injurious to the health of the child.—The Clerk: WhY have you a conscientious objection ?—Councillor Stott: Because I don't believe in vaccination at all. I don't believe it would be any use to the child, but that the health of the child would suffer in consequence. Applicant added that he had seen the sufferings other children had undergone through vaccination, and he did not think it was aii9 advantage.—Mr. Geddes : Do you care about the sufferings of other people ?—Councillor Stott said that he did not agree that non-vaccination would result in suffering to others.—The application w 34 granted.</p>
        <p>ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS ATINERAL WATERS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Tea and Concert at Sankey Bridges. The annual congregational tea and concert to 014: place at Sankey Bridges Wesleyan School on AlondaY• About 90 sat down to an excellent tea which was pro- vided by the congregation. After tea an enjoyable entertainment was given. Songs were rendered by the Misses C. Jackson, L. Hardy, L. Johnson, 31. Webb and Messrs. P. Jackson and G. Hackney. I\ll.. Q. Jones contributed a recitation. Violin solos were given by Miss Dolly Lawton. Pianoforte solos and duets were also contributed by the Bros. Hill. :qr. Rowland Hill ably accompanied. The Rev.R.Wilkiee Rees presided and gave an excellent address. During the evening Messrs. Wadsworth and J. Lawton gave their reports for the past year. The debt fund which was started at Easter 1903, has reached the magnis" cent sum of £llB. A most successful concert Noe brought to a close by the singing of the Doxology.</p>
        <p>FIRE GRATES of every description.—J. S. GAR- LAND, Furnishing , Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street,Warringion.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Choir Social.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening the choir of the Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church held their annual social, when about 50 persons sat down to an excellent supper, kindly prepared by Mesdames Mansell,Parke9, Brown and Shaw. A. short musical programme vres arranged, the following taking part :—Miss Wilson sang "Violets," Miss Alice Griffiths "The Japanese Love Song," Miss Lavinia Hewitt A Selected Solo, Miss Elsie Spruce "Killarney." Mr. Walter Perri sang "The Deathless Army," Master Bertie Sprnee„ sang a humorous song" The Ballymony Conversazione.„ Messrs. Fred Podmore and Perry accompanied tur also contributed a pianoforte duet which was much ari preciated. Mr. Albert Spruce, choirmaster, organise the proceedings. Games occupied the rest of tbe evening, and the singing of " A.uld Lang Syne " te minated one of the most successful socials ever hew in the schoolroom.</p>
        <p>FOR CLASS AND CHINA of every description trY J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street. Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <p>, • • • ,</p>
        <p>Wfor CUNTICDTGHA.M'S HIGH-CLASS MINERA WATERS.—Advt. Seed Merchants' Unsuccessful Action.</p>
        <p>k</p>
        <p>toting Events. The annual sale of work in connection with St. Paul's Church will I4e held in the Kendrick-street schools on Monday. Mrs. John White will perform the opening ceremony. Lord Newton and Mr. Shee, M.A. (secretary) will address a public meeting under the auspices of the -National Service Leag-ue in the Parr Hall on Friday -e vening. Warrington Cricket Club play their opening match on Saturday next, when Sefton will be the visitors. Lyrnin May Queen Festival will be held on Whit Thursday, May 26th.</p>
        <p>BASSDIETTES AND MAIL CARTS in latest art Shades.—j. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. RP. R. A. Naylor and Education in Wales. Mr. R. A. Naylor, Unionist candidate for Carnarvon, Writes to the "Standard"' :—I have visited some five thousand houses in the Carnarvon Boroughs, and I 'have been astonished to find how little real interest the electors take in the education agitation, not one in 'twenty having mentioned the matter to me. The whole lurapus on the education question seems to be the .Work of party political wirepullers, and in my opinion if the people in Wales had been left to themselves the -Act would have been cheerfully accepted, and worked smoothly to the great advantage of education in the Principality. _LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS and SHOES, UNDER</p>
        <p>the CLOCK. BRID GE-STREET.—Ad7t. VP. R. A. Naylor and Welsh Love of Music. Presiding oL Alonday evening at the annual eistedd- fod at Canarvon, 11r. R. A. Naylor observed that he been accustomed to live in the atmosphere of "music, and he hoped that the Welsh nation would zever lose its love for real good musical endeavour. Ravios recently given permission to a leading inusician to set some words of his to music, the result liad been a beautiful composition. He was authorised `to offer to the committee of the next year's festival, if tIITY accepted, the song as a test piece, and three Prizes of five guineas, three guineas, and'one guinea respectively. (Loud cheers.)</p>
        <p>Local Eisteddfod Successes. The Lyric Quartette Party (Hisses A. Woldland and L. Fairhurst, and Messrs. N. Cook and P. J. 'George) carried off the first prize at A.sh.ton-in- llakerfield eisteddfod on Good Friday: The test Piece was "Tell me, Flora" (Hadley Watkins), and lhe adjudicator, Mr. David Jenkins, Mus. Bac., Aberystwyth; spike very highly of their Tendering. At Rhyl Eisteddfod on Monday Messrs. Crosfi.eld and Sons' Band obtained the second prize in the quick step contest. The War- rington Borough Band quartette was awarded the prize for the best cornet selection. At the Crown 'Eisteddfod, at Chester, the chief event was the choral competition for male voice choirs, the prize being With a silver crown for the conductor. The following were the awards :—Southport Vocal Union Male Prize Choir, 56 out of 60; Wigan Harmonic Male ,17°ice Choir, 55; Warrington Apollo Male Choir (Mr. Berrey), 47: Moelwyn Male Voice Choir, 45; Peumaenmawr Male Voice Choir, 40; Wedgwood Male Voice Choir, 40. The prize of £2O, with silver crown for conductor, was therefore awarded to outhport.</p>
        <p>BaCE BOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER THE CLOCK.—.Vivt EPitish Women's Temperance Association. A meeting of the above society was held on Tuesday afternoon in the Church Parlour, Bold-street.----Mrs. Garnett presided, and after leading in prayer was very helpful in reading over and discussing business papers.—The Rev. J. L. Milne delivered a short -address, in which he affirmed that temperance workers had great need of patient perseverance and gentle 'determination ; their opponents were not all bad nor did they wish to hinder the good work of rescuing thaperishing. With them it was a means of earning "then. daily bread, and many of them have been so vavironed with the sights and sounds that accompany laily life in a public-house that they see no harm in -dealing out intoxicants to starving humanity. The 'rev. gentleman said that the best thing to do was to -pray ior these who were engaged in the trade. As a practical suggestion he said a good work might be one amongst the children in striving to train their 7°.ang minds against the evil results of alcohol. If all the British women were engaged in Sunday school •ncl Band of Hope work, what a power for good would te felt in our town. a M. ASHTON'S TAPE TONIC is the KING OF Toxic S.--03. oANKEV-STRERT, WArrinatOn.--Advt.</p>
        <p>-the Ragged School Soup Kitchen. The soup kitchen has been open again during the 'Week, Mr. Skelland and his band of workers being ,kept busy .by a crowd of applicants more numerous ‘4att ever. A.bout 409 men, women and children have teen served daily with soup and bread. They come Itnna all parts of the town, some over a mile away, for *what is in miny elses their only meal hr the day. )3ut for a timely contribution *from. the, Warring- ton Independent Labour Party the workers would have been compelled to close the -souP kitchen to-day. Theo are now en- abled to continue at least for another week. The, donors this week are ;—(Money), Warrington I.L.P. £6 (instalment from proceeds of concert at Parr Hall); Harrison -£1 (2nk donation). (Meat and bread), llessrs. G. Bowcock," Benjamin Cross, Singleton W. P. Stringer. Fifty girls' jackets, paid for by a Sympathetic friend, have also been given out. The Committee and workers tender their heartfelt thanks 'to all who have aided them in their efforts to provide 'a daily meal for hundreds of deserving people during the present distress. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING 00 TONICS.-63, SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—.Adv. 'Wycliffe Cockhedge Mission.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday evening, a grand concert was given an. the Cockhedge Congregational Mission room to a large and appreciative audience, The meeting was presided over by the Rev. H. Wall,and the programme 'Which was a lengthy and attractive one, was carried out in a highly pleasing manner. Mr. Louis Wood gave two violin solos in his usual brilliant manner,his Tendering of" The Broken Melody " being very fine While "the Children of the city "-was sung with great 'effect by Hiss Alice Bridge. " 0 Dry Those Tears," Sung by Miss Fairhurst, and " Love The Pedlar' Sting by Miss Collin, received well-merited applause. :qr. J. Rabbitt rendered in a most telling manner 4' Mona" and " Because " for both of which an encore 'Was demanded. Mr. W. H. Wallington sang "The 13°.Ys of Limerick Town" and "The Queen of Connemara "iii a very pleasinr, manner. The humor- ous. element was supplied by Mr. A. 'Grounds who delighted his audience by his intensely . amusing *Musical sketches. A hitherto unknown conjuror, Mr Ernest Stanley, astonished the meeting by his sleigh of 'land tricks: The whole proceedings passed off in highly satisfactory manner and may be said to have. °een a distinct success. The pianoforte was kindly lent by Hr. W. H. Bailey.</p>
        <p>....At the Warrington County Court on Thursday. 4tessrs. Gartons, Limited, seed merchants, Warring- sued Frank Palethorpe, farmer, Peterborough, .'er £ll for seeds supplied.—Defendant, who was vepresepted by Mr. H.J.Longton,admitted ordering the seed, but said it proved to be valueless and would not 'germinate. He had, he alleged, sustained a loss of '.1() or £5O in consequence. The firm supplied hial with some olt seed as compensation, but no time afterwards was surprised to ",ceive a bill in respct of the first order of the Red ttng wheat seed.—ln crass-eamination by Mr. „rowne, Aliho appeared for the plaintiffs' he admitted wohlt he did not return the ten empty sacks belonging /0 the firm. He dressed the wheat before sawing it, with tar and vitriol. That was his usual practice.— ~ales Heves, a large 'farmer in the Peterborough .tt'strict, said he saw Mr. Palethorpe's wheat growing and the crop was a failure. He (witness) also sowed .B.l'me of the Red King wheat. supplied by the (lefendants, and that was a failure also. receiv?d five sacks of oats as corn- Ipensation and he was not charged for the .wheat.—Mr. Pearson gave evidence on -behalf of the )3laintiffs, and said the firm sold 5,000 or 6,000 sacks cif this wheat, and Mr. Palethorpe's was the only '"raplaint received respecting it. Mr. Heyes's was other stock of the sam species of wheat. -unYers dressed the wheat at their own risk. The hats were, sent ex gr-atio because Messrs. Garton's lau no liability in the matter.—Mr. Browne applied or the case to be adjourned in order that he might ex.pert vidence.—His Honour did not think it 'worth while to do so. There was no allegation Zgainst the firm. Their reputation stood too high to affected by the failure of a small portion of their l'rek to come up to merchantable quality. Such a 1144 might happen to the very best firm in the world. non-suited the plaintiffs viith costs.</p>
        <p>Burning Accident. Annie Cann, aged seven years, residing with her parents in Clara-street, was admitted into the infirm- ary on Thursday afternoon Offering from severe burns\on the arms, neck, and face. She lies in a serious condition.</p>
        <p>P.S.a. Meeting. The monthly open meeting of the Buckley-street P.S.A. Society was held on Sunday afternoon. Mr. W. Beswick presided over a large attendance, and gave an interesting address. The speaker was Mr. J. Stringer (of Leigh), who took as his subject "Calvary." , The soloists were Miss L. Plinston, who gave a pleasing rendering of " Calvary," and Mr. James Clayton, who sang "The G-ate Of the West." Miss M. Plinston, A.L.C.M., was the accompanist. Mr. N. Shaw read the lesson. There lwas a large con- gregation.</p>
        <p>Athletic Successes. I There were several local successes at the athletic sports held last weekend. At the Liverpool Harriers meeting, R. Edge, of Warrington A.C. came in second in the 100 yards sprint ; C. Berry 'won the third prize in the half - mile bicycle (scratch) race ; and C. J. Straw was fourth in the four miles handicap. At the New Brighton Tower sports, H. Mountield secured the third prize in the mile bicycle race. On Monday at the Wigan Racing and Athletic Club sports, John Riley, Sankey Harriers (130), and C. Dagnall, War- rington A.C. (159) secured first and second prizes respectively in the mile flat. -L. Edwardson Sankey Harriers (17) was second in the 220 yds., and W- I Price, of Warrington, third in the mile walking race.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>Kirkconnel—Bullivant.</p>
        <p>A marriage of local interest was s3lemuized at the Church of St. Andrew, Cotterstock, Northamptonshire,. on Tuesday, the contracting parties being Mr. Harold Kirkconnel, solicitor, Warrington, son of the late Mr. Edward Kirkconnel, of the Mount, Stockton Heath,and Miss Ethel Bullivant, elder daughter of Mr. J, W. Bullivant, of Cotterstock. The Rev. F. Buttaushaw, n„X., vicar of Cotterstock, conducted the ser- vice, which was fully choral. The bride TVAS attired in a beautiful white crepe-de-chine dress, Viral-fled with chiffon and silk fringe. w )re her mother's veil, fastened with pearls, her only ornament being 4a pearl and Virg:noise necklet, the gift of the bridegroom. Miss F. Ballivarat (sister of the bride), and Miss M. Kirkconnel (sister of the bridegroom). were the bridesmaids and their dresses were of green cr3 o Jli u. They wore black picture hats and carried bouqaets of daffodils. Their ornaments were pearl necklets, the gifts of the bridegroom. 'Mr. W. Kirkconnel, consin of the bridegroom, discharged the duties of best man. Numerous presents were received, including one from each member of the staff of Messrs. Robert Davies and Company, one from. the olce staff, and another from the Stockton Heath Hockey Club.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>Worthington —Dickenson</head>
        <p>Worthington —Dickenson</p>
        <p>At St. Thomas's Cnurch, Stockton Heath, on Wad- nesday, the marriage was solemnized of Miss E nily Worthington, youngest daughter of the Etsv. W. IL Worthington, The Mount, Stockton Haath,and formerly of Birkdale, and Mr. Edgar G. Dickenson. manufacturer of Warwick. The officiating clergy were the Revs. W. R. Worthington, H. V. Pigot and C. A. Evans. Toe bride was charmingly attired in a dress of w'aite duchesse satin, trimmed with lace, with court tra,in. She wore a white tulle veil and wreath of orange blossom, and carried a lovely bouquet of white roses and lilies of the valley, the gift of the bridegroom. The bridesmaids were Miss Jones (daughter of the Rev. J. R. Jones, Sankey), and Miss Coulton (daughter of the late Rev. G. ('oulton, of Livvrpool), and were tastefully gowned in pink voile dresses, with large black velvet sashes, and black hats. They carried choice bouquets of roses and wore gold curb bracelets, the gifts of the bridegroom. Mr. Pickton of Southport, discharged the duties of best man. The reception was held at the Mount, and later in the day the newly married couple left for London, where the honeymoon is being spent. The bride's travelling dress was a navy blue costume, with pale grey feather stole, and biscuit coloured hat, trimmed with pink. Nnmeroas presents were received. Bate—Jowett.</p>
        <p>At Bold-street Wesleyan Church on Saturday the wedding took place of Miss B. Jowett, dang'eter of Nlr. Thomas Jowett, 119, Marshhouse-lane. and Mr. John Henry Bate, son of Mr. Fred Bate, Warrington. The Rev. W. C. J. Neville officiated. The bride wore a pretty crepe-de-chine dress, trimmed with white silk and lace, The bridesmaids were the Misse; Lydia an! Evelyn 'Jowett (sisters of the bride) and the Misses Louisa and Gertrude Smith. Mr. Llewellyn Bate, brother of the bridegroom, discharged the duties of best man. Mr. W. Jowett presided at the Oman. The reception washeld in 860 k-street Mission. Numerous presents were received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>83R9E11 BUMEF. A Sixpenny Advance in the Rates,</head>
        <p>THE 83R9E11 BUMEF. A Sixpenny Advance in the Rates,</p>
        <p>The Warrington Corporation Budget will be presented tat the meeting of the Town Council on Tuesday even- ing. The estimates have been prepared already and if they are sanctioned, the total rates of the Borough vill be 7s. 10[1. in the .£ as against 7s. 4d. in the £ last year. This is made up as follows :—lmpro7enient rate 3s. 6d.. poor rate 4s. 2d. and the sewers rate 2d. The chief increase is in the improvement rate, which was 3s. last year. The sewers rate has dropped 2d. whilst the poor rate has increased by a like amount. Taa following is a sum na.:3,- of the estimates, and comparison is given of the rate for the previous year.— April, 1903. April, 1901. s. d. £ s. d. Borough Rate Improvement Rate. 29,651 6 4 ... 35,128 1 5 General Sewers Rate</p>
        <p>61,731 17 2 74,420 3 8 The Finance Committee have carefully revised the estimates, and recommend reductions involving a saving of £1,878. In regard to the Watch Committee's esti- mates, the Finance Committee recommend a reduction of £320, the amount included for fotir additional con- stables ; in the General Purposes Committee's estimates a reduction of £9.58 was recommended for accrued interest on purchase money for land at Howley and branch libraries. A proposal of the Park committee to erect a new front to the aviary at a cost of ,C3O is e tr- marked, as is also the proposal of the Gymnasium Cam - mittee to provide a shower bath at a cost of £5O. A reduction of £5O) is also re3om nea led in the Finince Committee's estimates.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF</head>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>ENDING APRIi, 2nd, 1904.</p>
        <p>During the week, the following births and deaths were registered :—Warrington Registration District births, 48 • deaths, 33. The' excess of births over deaths was 15. The birth-rate was equal to 36.5. The death-rate from all causes was equal to 25.9 per 1,000, against 23.9 the average of the previous 4 weeks and against 11.6 the corresponding period of last year. The death-rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 9.lcper 1,000, against 3.7 the average rate of the previous 4 weeks and against nil the correspond- ing period of 1903. The ages of mortality were :</p>
        <p>0-1 5-15 15-25 25-35 GS—a. • Total 12 9 3 • 5 4 33</p>
        <p>The deaths included 1 from measles, 1 from scarlet fever 8 whooping cough, 2 from diarrhcea, 2 from tub- ercular diseases, 5 from lung diseases, 14 from all other causes.</p>
        <p>The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during fi,!e wee:: :—Scarlet fever 18, erysipe- las 1 caee. .</p>
        <p>Total number of patients in hospital week coaling 2nd April, 116. METEOROLOGY.—The mean temperature was 41 Fah., and therefore 2 degrees lower than the preceding week. The maximum temperature 52 F., was reoorded on he 27th inst, and the minimum temperature 32 F., on tke 29th _hist _</p>
        <p>The rainfall measured .83 inch. The general direc- tion was N.E.</p>
        <p>J. GUEST GOR:NALL. Medical Officer of Health.</p>
