      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, April 9, 1904</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 9, 1904</p>
        <p>one which theycould reasonably expect, they must do their best to support the law. (Hear, hear.) If the offer was simply a snare and a delusion, well, they had shown what they could do in times gone by, and he thought they would be able to do it again. (Applause.) The necessity for protection still exitted. It was all very well for their opponents to say "Well, if you take the figures throughout the country, the number of licences would amount to one per 100 or one per 200." That was not the way to look at it. They should look at the number which existed in the area which is being dealt with by the licensing magistrates in the different towns, lathe city from which he camel the property in licenses relinquished was 25 per 100, so that when they came to relinquish inn given area a quarter of the total number of licenses, it became a serious ques- tion. It was said that they had been voluntarily resigned. Well they knew what it was. They had heard of the Eastern Pacha who said he did not levyl any duties. He did not expect any great amount of ' taxation from his subject, but if a certain amount was not delivered into his exchequer by a certain date there would be trouble (Laughter.) The licensing bench said almost the same thing. They said if they did not relinquish go many licenses in that district there would be trouble for them. He did not blame traders resigning licenses if they could come to an arrangement whereby the better claw.; of property might be saved, but at the same time every right-thinking man must admit.that the pressure placed upon them to give up property was not English at all. It was not the time to say too much. They were anxiously waiting for the opening of Parlia- ment. Mr. Balfour had, said he would introduce the Bill on the first or second day after the opening of Parlia- ment, and they must quietly wait the result of the introduction of his bill, because they would perhaps be offering arguments to their opponents beforehand if they said much about it. They must be prepared for the biggest fight they had had during the last twenty- five years. They had a big fight when they defeated Sir William Harcourt's Local Option Bill. They showed their strength then and they had then the public opinion behind them. He felt certain they would rise successfully, but they must fight loyally together. If they did he had no doubt that when he came on another occasion like that he would be able to congratulate them upon having got into a safe harbour with some vested interest so that they could carry on their busi- ness peaceably and quietly. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>ALDERAIAN FRANCO NIB AND "INJUSTICES" TO PUBLICANS.</p>
        <p>Sir GILBERT GREEN'iLL, Bart., proposed "The County Borough Magistrates." He thought that with regard to the licensing justices he could say that as a licens- ing authority they had met the licensed victuallers in a very fair and reasonable spirit. (Applause.) He thought that if the magistrates throughout the country were to use the same discretion as they did in Warrington there would be a good deal less friction than existed at the present time. _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>The MAYOR, in reply, remarked that Alderman Fran- comb had had more experience than himself on the Bench because he (the Mayor) was only magistrate by. the courtesy of his fellow townsmen who had had confidence in him to place him in the position of Mayor of the Borough. Alderman FRA.NCOMB also replied, and said he was rapidly becoming an old member of the Bench, as he had been a magistrate for eighteen years, and most of the time he had been on the licensing bench. He must say that after hearing what their. friend Mr. Isaac Turner had said about Liverpool they were a happy family in Warrington. He never saw anything like tyranny exhibi- ted, and never saw anything unreasonable. They had also got a very respectable body of licensed victuallers in the town, and in g ing to the Warrington Bench week aQer week, and year after year, they scarcely ever had even the most trivial offence against the licensing laws. As long as a body of men would conduct such a difficult business as they were doing in Warrington, there need be no fear of the bench. There were very funny things in the licensing laws. For instance the Mayor had for 21 years been doing zealous and useful work for the town, but because he happened to be con- nected with the brewing interest he was not considered a fit and proper person to be a magistrate. (Shame.) A teetotaler could be made a magistrate and be put on the licensing committee, and could do what injury he liked against publicans. (Hear, hear.) That was thoroughly un-English and thoroughly unfair. Some years ago a reso- lution was passed by the magistrates that when a publican was to be brought before the Bench a special notice must be sent to all the magistrates, so that if a publican committed the most trivial offence it took the whole Bench of magistrates to try it, but if there were any serious offence committed by anyone else there were only one or two magistrates. (Shame). That was unfair—(hear, hear)—but it defeated its own object, as there were always moderate men and they came down to protect the poor publican against those who wished to do him an injury. (Applause). He had the greatest possible respect for the people connected with the trade as they were law-abiding and he did not think that any of them need fear the magistrates or the police or anybody else. (Applause). TEIE MAYOR AND " TRADE " GRIEVANCES. The MAYOR proposed" The Warrington and District Licensed Victuallers' Association " and alluded in felicitous terms to the long and valuable services ren- dered by the president, Councillor John Evans. He (the Mayor) had been fighting the battles of the trade ever since he could remember. He once had to talk the Town Council out when they tried to pass Sunday closing. The Licensed Victuallers' Association was one of the best things they could possibly have in a place like Warrington. Singlehanded they could do nothing but collectively they could do a great deal. They could protect themselves from what he called tinkering legislation and the erratic ideas of anyone who wished to interfere with their trade. They had to be models of men to carry on their business. If ever there was a time when the trade should combine and let the House of Commons know distinctly that it was time they were not inter- fered with and annoyed, it was the present. Not only that, but from some legislation they obtained no satis- faction because different benches put different con- structions upon it. They could not carry on a busi- ness according to the law and at the same time make it pay if they were to be constantly annoyed by not knowing how that business was to be conducted. One began to think that publicans were the only wrong doers in society. Legislation, he contended, ought to definitely define how 'a man eould carry on his business so as to be free from inter- ference. Continuing, the Mayor referred to the sup- pression of houses in the demolition :areas. The practice, he contended, was not English. It was not fair or honest, and it was not justice for the simple reason that they were robbing one man to tryand benefit another. If the Government did not know what to do in regard to compensation, the best thing to do would be to allow the matter to stand over for five years and prevent any benches of magistrates from suppressing licences until a satisfactory scheme had been drawn up. The magistrates were there to dis- pense justice, but was it justice to suppress licences on the ground that they were not required, and refuse to grant compensation? He did not consider that was honest. The licensing legislation, he contended, was simply class legislation. The Liverpool magistrates never suppressed the hotels which the magistrates went to' They suppressed the public-houses where the working- man held his club. They made frivolous objections of all descriptions, the reason being that some magistrates looked at the matter entirely from the teetotal point ofilview, and forgot that the trade had to be !carried on collectively, and that the licensees had to make their living by it. This class legislation tended to one thing —interference with the working classes. The working classes should put theirshoulders to the wheel and say distinctly "We have been interfered with enough and from what he could gather he thought they were beginning to find that out, and that they would have no trouble in influencing electors iu the future. The licensed victuallers and the brewers must combine and present a united phalanx against the men who would interfere with the carrying on of their business accord- ing to the law, and made frivolous objections which placed the publicans in the position that to-day they might be earning their livings and to-morrow they might be in the workhouse. They could only put a stop to that kind of thing by supporting their district associations. (Appliiuse.) Councillor JORN EVANS responded and said it would be thirty years this month since they put him in office in that association. (Applause.) He was very pleased to say that only a very small percentage of licensed victuallers were not rnem‘)ers of the association. THE CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOSEPH SHILLCOCK proposed the health of the Chairman and referred to his work as a member of the Town Council and to the important part he had played in various municipal undertakings. They were very proud that one of their trade had been found fitting to fulfil the highest honour that the Council could confer upon hint? Whatever might be said by carping critics, he felt sure that the ()face of Mayor would lose none of its lustre through being entrusted to Alderman Bolton, (Applause). In reply the MA.Yort said he had tried to serve the town as well as he could ; and he had done his work conscientiously. He thought the trade were a" badly used lot,"and that they had been unfairly dealt with, and he hoped that in the future theywould pull together for the advancement of that association. (Applause.) The health of Sir Gilbert Greenall having been en- thusiastically honoured, a successful gathering was brought to a close by the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
        <p>During the evening an enjoyable musical programme was submitted. Mr. T. Humphrey, Mr. J. Parkinson, Mr. J. H. Draper and Mr. B. Poole each gave songs in very creditable style, and the voices of the party blended effectively in the quartette " As the moments roll." The trios " Life's a bumper and " A little farm well filled," were well rendered by Messrs. Draper, Parkinson and Humphrey.</p>
        <p>LORD SIMSBURY'S Br-cvcr.E.—Lcal wheelers will, no doubt, be interested to know that during the last few day's the Marquis of Salisbury has purahased Premier bicycle fror.n. the Premier Cycle Co., Limited Coventry. This in. Itself is a tribute to the .vide popu- larity of the machine, the local agent for which is Mr. J. S:Garland.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>LICENSED VICTUALLERS' BANQUET. SPEECHES ON TRADE QUESTIONS. The Mayor and the Publican's Grievances.</head>
        <p>LICENSED VICTUALLERS' BANQUET. SPEECHES ON TRADE QUESTIONS. The Mayor and the Publican's Grievances.</p>
        <p>THE CONFISCATION OF LICENCES.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening the members of the Warring- ton and District Licensed Victuallers' Association met in the spacious assembly room at the Lion Hotel, for their third annual banquet. The company num- bered over 80 and was presided over by the Mayor (Alderman Bolton). Amongst those present were Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., Alderman Francomb, Coun- cillor Isaac Turner (Liverpool), Mr. C. W. Swift (chairman Of the Liverpool Licensed Victuallers' Association), with Mr. James !Tones (vice- chairman) and Mr. W. Gill (secretary), Councillor John Ingle, Councillor Gregory and Councillor H. H. Heap (Manchester), Councillor Hind (Salford), Mr. Aston and Mr. T. Thorley (St. Helens), Mr. NV. West- head and Mr. John Llewellyn (Wigan), Mr. H. Rowley (Oldham), Captain Crosfield, Captain Potter, Messrs. J. Fulton Smith, W. B. Roberts, E. Parry, IL W. Rowson, J. H. Clarke, F. Hou ard, Councillor Rogers, Councillor John Evans (president of the assooiation), Mr. J. Shillcock (vice-president), Mr. R. Shacklady (hon. treasurer), Mr. T. Walsh (secretary), and Messrs. John Sampson, T. Massey, W. Dillon, Fairfield Turner, Peers Brimelow, and T. Partridge (members of the committee). Aplogies.for non-attendance had been received from Mr. R. Pierpoint, M.P., Colonel W. Hall Walker, M.P., Mr. J. G. Groves, M.P., Mr. J. Reid Walker, Mr. A. H. Crosfield, Mr. T. S. Down, Mr. Roger C. ,Parr, Colonel Ridgway, the Rev. Father Wilson, Messrs. James Monro, Henry Greenall, J. J. Whitley, William Owen, Joseph Robinson, W. Howarth (Rochdale), and Ford (Manchester). An excellent dinner was served, Mr. J. Lee catering in his best style. Afterwards a toast list was gone through, Mr. R. Shacklacly acting as toastmaster. riE LOYAL TOASTS.</p>
        <p>The MXY37t propoiel "roe King." He said his Majesty was a'3out as gool a King as we could possibly have. He was the right man in the right place. We neverheard much of republicanism and that kind of thing in the country now. They were undor the impression a little time ago that if we had a republican at the head o f affairs we should have a very elegant sort of gentleman, bat he thought that if Ar 3 now became la republic and hal to- find a president, our present King would be selected. (Hear, hear.) He was a model King and a pattern tOlall the other nations on the face of the globe. (Applause.) The toast was accorded musical honours. _ _</p>
        <p>The company also drank the health of "Queen Alexandra, Prince and Princess of Wales and the rest of the Royal family," on the proposition of the MAYOR. THE IMPERIAL FORCES. CAPTAIN CR3SFIELD AND THE COAPULSORY SERVICF, QUNSTION.</p>
        <p>In the absence of Mr. Arthur Browne, the toast of the "Army and. Navy aad Auxiliary Forces" was pro- posed by Mr. WILIAA.M GILL (secretary of the Licensed Victuallers' Association, and formerly of Warrington). Ha said he was asked to propose the toast because he happened to have SOIII9 coanectioa with the army and navy. After the passing of the Bill which led to Free Trade in 1813, his father went to enlist in the Life Guards, but he didn't enlist. In that way he (Nlr. Gill) was connected with the army. (Laughter.) Then in the year 60 something he ran away from school at Beverley and got to Hall, and went on board a ship to ask if they wanted a boy. A violent man kicked him off the gangway. That was his only connection with the navy. ( Loud laughter.) He had very pleasant memories of Warrington and its Volunteers. He had seen them many times 20 years ago drilling on the old green oppo- site the Town Hall, where the . Post o:fice and streets were now. %V arrington always • produced a good regi- ment. (Hear, hear.) He was pleased ta know that they kept it up naw,aad he was proud to find. that Warrington during the B3or War seat out he should think as many men per head of the population as any town in the Kingdom. (Applause.) Captain G. R. Caosrar,D responded. They were all be said, justly proud of our imperial forces. But in their enthusiasm let not the fact that the navy was the finest and largest that the world had ever seen, let not the theuzhtuf the valour of our soldiers whose device was honoar and whose support was patriotism, let not the devotion of certain volunteers, let not all these things blind them to the fact that those gallant forces were weak compared with those of other countries— (lear,hear)—because they had got that backing and that backbone of all others—a nation which was trained and organized. Our navy was spread all over the world and our army had its hands full. Surely it was the duty, nay it was the privilege of every man who called him- self Briton, to fill a) the gap which was in his armour by training and learning now to defend his , country in the hour of need. The training required was not a bag one. Gallant little Switzerland had shown us what could be done with short service. The National Service League, of which he was proud to be a member, advocated that every young man when he came to the age of 18 years should serve his country for 100 days either in camp in the country, or else on board a training ship. Thus, and thus only, would we get that reserve of men which we needed and which was of such pressing importance with our navy. Thus, and thus only, would we get that • backing, that great second line of defence which would indeed make a raid or invasion an idle dream. When national service avaN the law of this country, then, and not till then, would anyone who responded to that toast be able to say Yes. We are an imperial force. Wo hope for peace but if war should come, then .we should go and fight with no fear as to the result, because we shall know that there is behind us that best backbone of any other—a nation well trained and organized." (Applause)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>HOUSES OF PARLIANIENT.</head>
        <p>THE HOUSES OF PARLIANIENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. B. R33ERTS proposed " The Houses of Par- liament." He alluded ta the Licensing Bill, which they hoped to welcome on the 10th inst. He hoped it might be acceptable in its compensation and -administrative clauses to them as a trade which only demanded justice and protection. (Applause.) Among the numerous bills which were before the House during the coming session,representing every form and shade of politics,he felt perfectly certain that they could place every con- fidence in the action of their respected member—Mr. Robert Pierpoint—(applause)--And they could leave it in his hands ta support and v‘ate for those various measures which were occupying so seriously and so strenuously the attention of the present Government. Owing to the absence of Mr. Pierpolnt. no reply was made to the toast. THE TERRIBLE WORD " DISCRETION "</p>
        <p>J. FULTON SMITH proposed "The National Defence League." He said that defence was in the air Just now. They had had an eloquent appeal by Captain Crosfield as to the national defence of their hearths and homes, and now they were asked to consider another defence which came much closer to them, the defence of their trade. (Applause). It had been tyrannised over, it had been hampered in ways unaccountable and in a way they considered to be most unwarranted and must unjustifiable. Their trade had to be conducted under a great deal of petty interference and by that terrible word " discretion " which was wielded by people who did not know what discretion meant. (Hear,hear, and applause). He thought that what was wanted in the trade generally was more loyalty to each other. The Chancellor of the Exchequer had. a deficit of five millions and this must come out of something. He did not know what some people would think if they had to pay three or four times the priee for things as they would have to do but for the revenue derived from the trade. In their defence thero was one point which appeared to him required attention. That was that the distributing portion of the trade was not represented in Parliament. The teachers had a practical teacher in Parliament and there were other institutions repre- sented. It would be a good thin,' if there was a man there who had been brought up in the trade and knew the dangers and difficulties which had to be provided agaihst. Such a man would be invaluable to them as a trade. (Hear, hear.) Councillor Isaac TURNER, of Liverpool, (secretary of the National Defence League) replied, and said it gave him the greatest pleasure because he knew that for so manyiyears,eyer since its inaugu ration,theyhad been good and loyal supporters of the national organisation in Warrington. Not only had they supported the national defence of trade, but they had also kept their own farm well titled and had kept their own house well looked after, because the president told him that so far as the Trade in Warrington was concerned they could thoroughly rely upon the good sense and sound discre- tion of those who governed them. (Laughter.) With regard to the question raised by Mr. Roberts with respect to the trade at the Drasent time, the advice they had to give Some time ago to sit tight was the advice they had to offer to-day. Those gentlemen on their executive who had been to Mr. Balfour to hear his memorable speech given in all sincerity, must still sit in confident expectation that the promises given with such good faith would be fulfilled. (Applause.) It would be impossible to „think otherwise when the Premier of this great country would voluntarily pledge himself to certain legislation of an honest and proper character. (Applause), They had the promise of the introduction of legislation which could protect their property from confiscation. (Applause). They had seen a test question raised in the House as to the feelings of the majority of the members on the question of compensation, and they had a majority of two to one that compensation ought to be given to lawabiding citizens whentheir livelihood was taken away from them without cause of complaint. They had got to do nothing but sit quietly and wait for what the law would do for them. If the offer wit:.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>FLYING VISIT TO PONTEFRACT.</head>
        <p>A FLYING VISIT TO PONTEFRACT.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON GAIN A WELCOME VICTORY.</p>
        <p>Strange as it may seem, the most popular excursion from Warrington during the recent holiday season was not to some favourite seaside resort, or the scene of rustic beauty, but to the small manufacturing town of Pontefract in the shire of broad acres. This seem- ingly had taSte was not dictated by any desire on the part of Warringtonians to see the land from whence comes liquorice, Pomfret cakes and confectionery of a like order, but because the Pontefract Football Club had had the great good fortune to be drawn against Warrington in the Northern Union Cup Competition and to have the option of meeting the representatives of the Warrington Club at Pontefract,or as the town is colloquially called,Pomfret. The Yorkshiremen eagerly seized the opportunity of entertaining the wearers of the primrose and blue, and accordingly the journey was made from Warrington on Saturday. The team journeyed by an ordinary train via Manchester, but their followers, to the number of about 1,000, patron- ised the two excursions which were run.</p>
        <p>10.30 a.m. found me standing outside Areley Station among a knot of football enthusiasts. They are easily distinguishable by the favours they wear. If there is not a button-hole display of primrose and blue, in all probability there will be some device resembling a fish, or some word of command imprinted on an ivy- leaf, addressed to that finny creature to" play up." One individual showed his partiality for that comes- tible by displaying a red herring pinned on his cap ! Amongst such red-hot enthusiasts, he would be a bold man AP dared even to discant on the possibilities of Pontefract getting the better of the fray. Pontefract, in the eyes of many of these supporters of the War- rington club was a beaten team the moment they stepped on the field. Events did not turn out quite so rosy, as we shall see later on. The excursion train which steamed out of Arpley Station at 10.40 a.m. was a long one, a feature of it being the number of saloons which had been engaged. For an additional sixpeace per head a party can enjoy the luxury of a saloon, and on a long journey such as that of Saturday's, the additional sixperrtS is well spent on a saloon. I was fortunate enough to travel with Mr. Thomas Petuberton.'s party of checkers, whose duty it was on arrival, to check the takings at the entrance gates to 'the football enclosure. Such a party would not have been necessary if the field had been equipped with an adequate number of turnstiles, but when tickets are sold through numberless pigeon-holes and there are two or three entrances unprovided with 'turnstiles, it is more satisfactory to the two clubs who are ping to share the spoil, if they are both represented at the seat of custom. The railway journey thither was accomplished with but few stoppages, and the London and North-Western Railway Company won golden opinions for their expeditious running. One reason assigned for it was that the company was desirous of getting into the good graces of the club's supporters in view of the semi-final excursions which will be required a week hence. A brief stoppage at Hudders- field was sufficent to call to recollection that soaking wet cup-tie c:ay, a few years ago when Warrington's hopes were buried at Fartown. But to-day the weather was more propitious. The lowering clouds were left behind in the Manchester districts, and when the heights of Pontefract were reached, there was promise of a fine day, and fortu- nately for Warringtoa this prognostication wai justified. 'Singular to relate, the town of Pontefract, despite its 20,000 inhabitants has not a railway sta- tion Jearing its name. The excursionists by the London and North Western Railway system disen- trained at Tanshelf whilst those who travelled by the Cheshire Lines Committee g -4 off at Baghill. Neither stations are very far away from the town, and both I trains arrived long before the time of the kick-off. Opportunity was taken to see the sights of the town. It is prettily situated, surrounded by hills, and lies near the confluence of the Aire and Calder. It is a town with a history. There still exists the remains of an old Norman castle which was the scene of the murder of Richard 11. and other atrocities, and it is credited with being the last garrison to hold out for Charles I. In 1649 it was -dismantled, and to-day the visitor sees nothing more than an interesting ruin. The purpose of visiting Pontefract was not,hovvever of the aesthetic kind, and it was necessary without much delay, to find out the scene of the impend- ing conflict. The enclosure bears the proud title of Park Avenue, but it is not as grand as its name suggests. To one accustomed to such an excellent enclosure as that of Wilderspool, it seems paltry. The playing patch is diminutive in size, and at one endi there is a nasty slope which must be discon- certing to players unaccustomed to it. The stands are curiosities in their way. Admittance to the only covered stand on the field was modestly fixed at half- a-crown. It was approached by a ladder and covered the dressing rooms. Perched up in this limited area were several of the elite of tho neighbourhood, and their surroundings resembled to some extent, a menagerie cage. There were two uncovered stands, for which an addi- tional shilling was demanded, and willingly paid by many who at Wilderspoal have the same privilege, without extra charge. But cup ties are cup ties and treasurers are inexorable when the gate has to be divided. Mr. Thomas Pemberton was busily engaged with the officials of the home club in checking the takings and seeing that every entrance was covered. It is at such times as these, that one realises more fully than ever, the good services which Mr. Pember- ton quietly and unostentatiously renders to the War- rington Club. As the time neared for the commencement of hostili- ties, spectators gathered together in their thousands They came from all quarters and in every kind of conveyance. Brakes, waggonettes, gigs, and motor cars rounded the adjacent hills and dumped their occupants at the gates of Park Avenue. The supporters of the Pontefract club wore blue and white favours, and ivy leaves bearing the words" Play up Pomfret." Castleford,where memories still linger of Warrington's triumph over their club in a former cup competition, sent a quota of spectators, whilst Batley, still smarting under defeat at the hands of Pontefract, sent men to cheer on Warrington as a kind of revenge upon the liquorice men. There was 4 no band of music to while away the time of waiting but a diversion was caused by some Warrington men, who shall be nameless. Two of them set themseltres the task of running across the field. The race started well but before they had gane far, first one and then the other stumbled in an undignified way, and one of them left the field with his shoulder injured. A smart Pomfret lad, who had been selling programmes mean- while, went to the scene of the contest and had the felicity of picking up a number of silver coins and various other articles which had fallen from the 1 pockets of the Warrington wobblers. Another War- rington enthusiast here interposed and Cut but a sorry figure in his attitude towards the Pomfret lad. It was a relief to the Warrington spectators when this little episode was over. A loud cheer signalised the entry of the home team into the arena. They wore jerseys with narrow per- ! pendicular stripes of blue and white, and were of that ! robust, heavy type, characteristic of Yorkshire' Clubs. The hearty cheer which greeted the appearance of the 'Warrington men was evidence of their popularity. Fish was the cynosure of all Yorkshire eyes, his fame having preceded him. Even the school lads had chalked his name on almost every available board in the district, and as this was his first appearance on Pontefract turf, Fish was as great a draw as the War- rington team itself. .. , It is not nay intention here to enter into details of the play. Those will be found in another column. Suffice it to say that it was a typical cup-tie encounter. The Yorkshiremen had the wind behind them in the first half and their forwards went off with such a bound that I hardly thought they would be able to maintain it to the end. But they did. They held the upper hand until well on in the second half, when the Pontefract captain made the fatal mistake of taking one man out of the pack and placing him in the three-quarter line. The object in view was the obvious one of keeping the Warrington backs out, but the manceuvre had the exactly contrary effect. Having the upperhand in the scrum, the Warrington backs got the opportunities they had been waiting for all afternoon, and they soon gave a taste of their quality. When once under weigh, the Pontefract quintette was not in the running, and both Harris and Fish succeeded in crossing the coveted line,and giving Warrington the victory. Until the home captain made this mistake, 1 't looked as though there would have to be another trial of strength at Wilderspool. Both teams had scored 4 points each, Fish having rubbed off the</p>
        <p>ftrrears which accumulated in the first half, by two ' magnificent place kicks. When Warrington turned round with the wind in their favour, it was thought that they would very speedily gain the lead. But to the dismay of the Warrington spectators, time passed on without a single point being scored.. The Pomfret men were delighted and there were loud cries of "Play up Ponty !" There was no such hilarity amongst the Warringtomans. I have it on the authority of a former official of the Warrington Club, whose veracity I cannot question, that he heard his companion's knees shaking, and that there was an evident tremour in his voice at this stage of the game. Every well-wisher of the Warrington cbib was hoping against hope that the Warrington backs would get going and pull the game out of the fire. But no ! It was a long while in coaling, and then only as the result of mistaken judg- ment on the part of the home captain. Bate and Davies seemed unable to do anything right when they did occasionally get possession, and penalising,knock- ing on and dallying with the ball seemed to be the order of the day. Meanwhile four brilliant backs were standing shivering behind. Fish, full of resource as ever,determined to resort to.place kicking as a means of getting on level terms with the Pomfretonians and the two goals which he kicked must rank among his finest efforts of the season.</p>
        <p>From this stage of the game to the close, the War- rington men never looked • back. A passing move- ment between the halves and the, three-quarters resulted in Harris bounding over the line amid cheers from the Warringtonians, which would com- pare favourably with the best 'of " Wilderspool whis- pers. The try was the result of what was practically the first combined movement of the afternoon, and was an eye-opener for the Yorkshiremen, who had relied solely on rushing, bustling and punting as cup- tie adjuncts. Pontefract were given one more chance to draw level, and they nearly accomplished it. Dickenson in trying to relieve Hallam of a catch, dropped the ball in front of the Warrington posts, and from the ensuing scrimmage a weak kick by Davies towards the touch line, gave Connell possession. He reached the line, but in grounding, fell into touch in goal. This was Pontefract's last chance, and the closing movement of the day filled the home team's cup of sorrow to the brim. It came about in this way. Bate got 'away from the scrimmage in the Pontefract 25, and after bluffing his oppment, he transferied to Fish, who, with little ground to move about in, got past the full back, and scored near the corner. The spectators were now leaving the ground and when the goal kick had failed, referee McCutcheon blew his whistle for "no-time."</p>
        <p>The victory of Warrington was the more appreci- ated as it had been so long delayed, and the happiness of the Warringtonians was depicted on their faces. The old-fashioned streets and the adjoining railway sta- tions resounded with the shouts and cheers of War- rington men. Almost in every hand there was held aloft a bunch of liquorice roots, in the sale of which a heavy trusiness was done, whilst some of the more demonstrative, pinne,d funeral cards of the Pontefract club in their caps. When the results of the other cup tie Matches became known, the remark was heard "Why we are the only Lancashire club left. And yet they say we are no good !" That is flit of opinion of J. H.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>DEATH OF MI JAMES TINSLEY, J.P.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MI JAMES TINSLEY, J.P.</p>
        <p>Funaral at Grappanhal</p>
        <p>Mr. James Tinsley, J.P., died on Sunday evening at Bishopswood Grange, Herefordshire, the residence of his youngest daughter, Mrs. E. G. Davies. Mr. Tinsley was taken ill while at breakfast on Sunday morning, and passed peacefully away the same evening. He was 74 years of age. Por over 50 yeArs ha had been associated with the firm of Messrs. John and James Tinsley, tanners, Mersey-street, a firm which he founded in conjunction with his brother.. He was never prominently associated with public affairs in Warrington, but for many years he was a trustee of the Blue Coat School, and also of the Warrington Savings Bank. He also faithfully dis- charged the duties of County magistrate for Lancashire and Cheshire. in politics he was a Conservative, but he was never a prominent politician. The Church of England had a devoted member in Mr. Tinsley, and so far back as 1874 he was serving as warden of St. Thomas's, Stockton Heath. On removing to Daresbury he attended all Saints' Church, and took an active interest in its work.</p>
        <p>The body was brought to Fro dsham from Herefordshire by rail, and thence conveyed to Daresbury Church on Tuesday evening. The bearers were: Messrs. G. Hayward, J. Withenshaw, T. IVitter, F. Pollard, r. Houghton, J. Gleave, George Wright, C. Phillips and Sergeant Booth A short and impressive service was held in the church, by the Rev. F. Clifton-Smith, and attended by the im- mediate relatives and villagers. The body rested in the sanctuary all night and was guarded by watchers. On the following morning the remains were conveyed to Grappenhall Church and interred at noon in the family vault there. The cofan was boine to the grave by some of the oldest of the deceased gentleman's workpeople, namely Messrs. John Waring, Thomas Burke, John Burke, J. Poyner, J. Underwood and J. Gleave. The funeral service was impressively conducted by the Rev. Canon Willis (Rector of Warrington). Rev. Richard, Greenall (Rector of Grappenhall) and the Rev. F. Clifton-Smith (vicar of Daresbury). There were also present the Rev. H. V. Pigot (vicar of St. Thomas's), and the Rev. Edward Charley (vicar of race). The relatives B,nd-friends present were : Mr. Frank Tinsley, Mr. James Tinsley, Mr. Charles H. Tinsley, Mr. Hugh Tinsley (sons), Mr. and Mrs. Vero B. Davies (daughter and son-in-law),Mr.and Mrs.E. Ga.skell Davies(daughter and son-in-law) Mr. Claude and Mr. Vincent Davies (grandsons), kr. Walter Tinsley (nephew), Mr. and Mrs. William Long, Air. Walter Shipman, Mr. William Tinsley, Miss Harrison and Miss Grecian') (Knutsford), Mr. A. Thomas J.P. (Frodsham), Mr. Charles White, J.P., Sir Gilbert Greenall Bart, Dr. Parker (Stockton Heath), Mr. and Mrs. John Hutchings Mr. John Hankinson Mr. A. Ashton (Sandiway), Mr. Percy Mortimer (irodsham), Mr. John Whitley, Mr. William Nelson, Mr. Henry Greenall, Mr. John Bleckley J.P., Mr. W. H. Bleckley J.P., Mr. J. E. Seeker, Colonel Ridgway J.P. Mr. William Waters, Messrs. Leyland (Grappenhall), Mr. Thomas Darlington, Mr. Thomas Warburton, Mr. and Mrs. Edwards, Mrs. Dawson, Mr. F. Spence, Mr. J. Fairhurst, Mr. W. Miller, Mr. J. Leigh. Wreaths had been sent by the following :—Sir Gilbert and Lady Greetiall, Mr. Leonard G. Davies, Mr. and Mrs. A. Ashton, Mr. John Hankinson, servants at Bishopswood Gra.nge,Mr. andWrs. Henry Greenall. Mr. and Mrs. C. Reynolds, Mr. and Mrs. John White, Miss Woods, Mr. and Mrs. Muskett and family, Rev. F. Clifton Smith, Mr. and Mrs. H. P. Mortimer Mr. and Mrs. Thomas, Mr. and Mrs. E. M. and Mr. S. Crosfleld. Mrs. Hodgson, Mrs. T. Litton, servants at Daresbur Hall, Mr. and Mrs. Wm. Long, "Frank, Charles, James and Jack," gardeners andlservants at Birchdale," Claude and Vin," Nurse M. Jones, and one from the family. . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>MAGISTRATE'S TRIBUTE.</head>
        <p>A MAGISTRATE'S TRIBUTE.</p>
        <p>On taking the chair at the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednesday, Mr. W. H. Bleckly said that before beginning the ordinary business of the Court, he would like to express on behalf of himself and every county gentleman of the Warrington County Petty Sessional Court, their extreme regret at the sudden death of their late valued colleague, Mr. James Tinsley. The deceased gentleman was one of the senior members of the Bench, having entered upon it some twenty years ago. He was always thoroughly painstaking and con- scientious in the work he did in the Court, and during the whole of that time he held the conftdence and esteem of all those who had the privilege of working with him. His loss would be very keenly felt. REFERENCE AT ST. THOMAS'S VESTRY MEETING.</p>
        <p>At St. Thomas's vestry meeting on Tuesday, Mr., T. R. Westbrook referred to the death of Mr. James Tinsley, and moved the following resolution :-6" That this meeting of the vestry of St. Thomas's hears with deep regret of the death of James Tinsley, Esq., of Daresbury Hall, and desires to express its sympathy. with the family in such loss,and records itsappreciation of his services at St. Thomas's Church in the past." He (Mr. Westbrook) had very early recollections of Mr. Tinsley and his wife and always found them good staunch churchpeople. (Hear, hear). Mr. Tinsley took a great interest in that church and so far back as 1874 acted as a churchwarden. Years before that he was a seat holder. He thought it would be only a ftriblite to his memory to express their regret at his death.(Hear,hear). Mr. PEA.RSCii said that as a very old resident in the parish it would not be inappropriate that he should second the resolution. Certainly it did stand out pro- minently in Mr. Tinsley's character that he was a strong Churchman, and his death would be a very great loss to the church at large, and no doubt to his own parish in particular. (Hear, hear). The motion was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>1 THE TRAMWAYS AND WIDENING OF OLD LONDON- ROAD.—Preparatory to commencing laying down the I new tram lines by the Warrington Corporation along 1 Old London-road to Victoria-square,workmen have this week been engaged pulling down the houses opposite the Mulberry Tree Inn and known as Beaconsfield- terrace. When this work has been completed and the road properly laid out, a great public improvement in Stockton Heath will have been effected, as the road known as Mulberry Tree corner at that spot, owing to lits narrowness, has been the scene of many accidents, iboth to pedestrians and vehicular traffic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>Well done, Warrington ! In the semi-finals again and with some prospect of securing the Cup. Whether or no, the team has done well to get so far in the competition, and it will not be ousted easily. Every match has been won by the super-excellence of the backs, who have atoned for the lack of weight in the scrummage. Still it is rough on Warrington to be thus handicapped, and all the more credit to the whole team for the perseverance which has brought success. Each of the teams now remainin, in the Competition can whack Warrington forward, at that does not say they will secure the verdict, and truth to tell, there is not much fear locally, whoever the opponents may be.</p>
        <p>Unfortunately I was unable to get to Pontefract on Saturday, but Ovalist the Second made the journey and apparently brought Warrington luck. It was only in the last quarter that the game was won, and there was considerable relief in the minds of the visiting supporters when Harris notched the try which gave Warrington the lead. The liquorice men con- ti•olled the serums with such affect that they looked like making a draw at least. When the score was seven points to four, Warrington had a narrow squeak for the Pontefracit men all but gained a try. Just on time, Fish capped the proceedings by a clever run down touch which produced another try and settled the issue of the match beyond all question.</p>
        <p>Though they were Playing second fiddle in the scrums all through, the Warrington forwards deservb praise for their smartness in the open and for their determination and pluck. They never gave up, though they got a severe gruelling in the first half, when Pontefract had the wind behind them, and eventually their superior stamina pulled them through. The homesters hardly made the best use of their chances in the first half or they might have secured a more solid advantage than two penalty goals. These were soon wiped off byTish on changing ends, one kick being from nearly half-way, and the other from the touch-line. It was of course, Pontefract's best policy to keep the ball close, but all the same when they had the advantage of the wind they might have risked opening the (Tame out in order to secure a try if possible. However, it is easy to advise after the match. Warrington expected to win, and they did so, superior speed and finish' telling in the end.</p>
        <p>The Warrington half-backs alone failed to dis- tinguish themselves. Davies was not at his best, and Bate was quite at his worst, the slowness in getting the ball away at times beina positively painful. Somehow, Bate does not seem able to rid himself of the habit of dallying with the ball when he has got it, and again he fails to make headway on his own. I thought at one time hl would have made a fine half back, but he falls far short of " Smack's " standard. The three-quarters were all in good trim, and at full back Hallam was safe as the Bank of England. The least unsteadiness on his part in the first half would have lost Warrington the match, but we have learnt to rely on Hallam, and he rarely tail.</p>
        <p>The VireA Lancashire Cup is expected to find a resting-place in Warrington this season, wherever the NTorthern Union trophy my ultimately go, They would look well together, but I am not going to count chickens. By defeating St. St. Helens sc handsomely on Monday, War- rington took the lead in the South West Lancashire Competition and have a match in hand over their only rivals, Wigan. The whole team was in swinging form, and suffered little from the absence of Dicken- son and Bate. In fact Hockenhall was far superior to the latter as Davies' partner. Nineteen points to three was the final score, and all Warrington's points accrued from the backs, Fish %himself aggregating 2 goals, 2 tries. He and Isherwood did splendid work on the left wing, one series of exchanges being re- miniscent of the pair at their best. St. Helens were 'made to look rathet•small thouga they are candidates for the first division.</p>
        <p>The South West Lancashire table up-to-date is as follows :</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. For. Agst. Pts. Warrington Wigan *St. Helens 5 ... 2 ... 2 ... 1 22-10 ... 10 Runcorn .Widnes . 11!..eig,h 8 ... 1 ... 4 ... 2 22--16 ... 4 *St. Helens matches, not being home and away en- gagements, each count 4 points.</p>
        <p>-- On Tuesday, the Northern Union "International " came off at Wigan, but the English side being short of Wilson and Hog.- could only manage to scrape up 3 points against 9'credited to the Other Nationalities. Fish obtained the try for the English side, he and Lomas being the two stars. Dickenson filled Wilson's place creditably.</p>
        <p>lain expecting WarringtOn to avenge the defeat at Leeds, to-day. The Headingley men have already said good-bye to the Cup, and if the Wilderspool visit proves fatal they will have to wait another season for the League trophy. Warrington will be represented by Hallam, full back ; Fish, Isherwood, Dickenson, and Harris threequarters ; Davies and Hockenhall, half backs; Boardman, Morrison, Lunt, Jolly, Naylor, Edmondson, Swift, Cook, or Thomas forwards.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday the postponed match with Huddersfield will be played at Farton-n.</p>
        <p>During the four matches he took part in at Easter, Fish scored 0 goals, 4 tries—not a bad record for the most closely-watched player in the Union.</p>
        <p>- • Bradford made no mistake in squelching Brough- On Rangers at the third attempt. The match took place at Leeds yesterday, when the Rangers were vanquished by 15 points to nil. This does not look well for Warrington's chances in the semi-final, but we have beaten Bradford before in a cup-tie, and I fancy we can do so again. The Warrington-Bradford match will be ployed at Broughton next Saturday, aud the sympathies of the crowd at any rate should be with the plucky Warringtonians. I don't think any- thing would please the primrose and blues more than to put paid to the Bradford account. I hope they may have the good fortune to do so.</p>
        <p>Broughton Rangers were reported to the League Committee by Hull Kingston Rovers for breach of engagement ,on April ith, and were fined :£25, the question of damages being adjourned until a future meeting. The committeelrdered that the match Anus t be played on April 15th.</p>
        <p>A match at Ossett on Tuesday against Kinsley was remarkable for the huge score piled on by the home team, and also for the goal-kicking abilities displayed by Aldred, who landed 20 goals from as many tries, and dropped a goal from the field of play. The first half resulfed in 7 goals and 7 tries falling to the lot of the home team,. but this is only a trifle when compared with the second portion, when 14 goals and 13 tries were notched. The try-getters were Gath (7), Aldred (6), J. Glover (2), J. Clarkson (2), Brady, Fenton and Dews. Aldred's 20 goals out of 20 tries completely eclipses Albert Goldthorpe's record of 14 goals from 15 tries, secured in a cup tie against Dudley Hill some years ago. Scere :—Ossett 21 goals, 20 tries (102 points), Kinsley nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>EASTERTIDE.—The Easter holidays passed over in rather quieter fashion than usual this year. Owing to the prevalence of measles in the district, it was thought advisable to forego the usual tea meeting at the Wesleyan school, on Good Friday. The usual morning service on Friday at St. Mary's Church was. well attended. On Saturday the Penketh Tannery prize band journeyed to Compstall to participate in a contest there, but were unsuccessful in gaining a prize. On Sunday morning early Communion was held at St. Mary's Mission, and also after matins at St. Mary's Church, a large number attending. Easter Monday was a general holiday, but those in search of enjoyment and excitement, were obliged to seek it outside the village, and ' good number made expeditions to different places. The weather on the whole has been fairly good, but the rough winds have not been conducive to holiday mak- ing. EXODUS.—OnTuesday last there departed from this village three young men, who are trying their fortunes in another land .Messrs. W. Upton, J. Dunbabin, and E. Broadbent, left to embark at Liverpool, en route for Canada. Several friends and relatives were present to see them off, and it is to be hoped their enterprise will be successful, and they carry with them the good wishes of the village, where they are well-known.</p>
