      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>SPRING CLEANING.</head>
        <p>SPRING CLEANING.</p>
        <p>An Article for Women Readers.</p>
        <p>ONCE TRIED OUR ALWAYS USED</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>PICTURE TITLES FOR PAINTERS AND PHOTOGRAPHERS.</head>
        <p>PICTURE TITLES FOR PAINTERS AND PHOTOGRAPHERS.</p>
        <p>"THE STUDIO" OFFICE LEICESTER SQUARE, LONDON, W.C. •</p>
        <p>It is generally admitted that an appropriate and : attractive title adds to the popularity of a work of art. Most artists have a genuine belief in the advantage of giving to their pictures a name which will arrest attention by its verbal picturesqueness, and yet will be sufficiently explanatory of the pictorial intention; and in this belief they devote much trouble to hunting up titles which will suit their works. It is, however, diffi- cult for a man who has not readily available a number of books cgntaining the right kind of material to find a title which has not been used over turd over again. Poetry is the most natural source of inspiration, but a busy Worker grudges the time spent in searching through many volumes,of the poets on the chance that he may hit upon a line or two adapted to his purposes; and, as an ' easier alternative, he adopts one of those hackneyed quotations which recur with tedious frequency in the pages of exhibition catalogues. The intention of the hook just published under the above title, is to save artists of all kinds from wasting their energies in often fruitless reading. It provides them with nearly three thousand poetic quotations, which have been carefully ! selected as combining a certain amount of suggestion for pictorial motives with adaptability for titles. In preparing itythe most familiar poems, which have been drawn upon over and over again, have been avoided, and the quotations chosen are chiefly those which have been unused before. The book will be found of the greatest value to painters, sculptors, photographers, and others. The many quotations it Contains not only supply titles for pictures already completed; but suggest subjects for future work. The book is so arranged that suitable titles can be found with the greatest possible ease in the shor6est possible time, and is published at the very eeasonable price of 3s. 6d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>SWALLOWED A MOUSE:</head>
        <p>SWALLOWED A MOUSE:</p>
        <p>A young man employed at Leighton Buzzard was lifting a box containing groceries into a cart when a mouse jumped from the box right down his throat. He drove to a doctor's surgery. when a strong emetic was given to him, but without effect. For several hours the Iman suffered considerable discomfort,but he is now well again. •</p>
        <p>NERVE PAINS, WINTER ILLS, CHILBLAINS, CUTS AND SORES, BRUISES, BURNS, Etc.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>Examiner Saturday, April 9 1904.</head>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday, April 9 1904.</p>
        <p>stepped back a pace or two; he was at a dis- advantage, for the light which was behind Miss Morton throwing her face so completely into fa:sclow, shone all on his, and :shewed her a pair cf startled auk eyes, a long, thin nose, a close - cropped heud, and a thin-lipped, wide month; while of her the man could make out nothing beyond a very white face set in a halo- of bronze hair on which the light shone brightly. He could see nothing of her eyes and the terror in them. Twice Lavinia tried to speak, but her voice some- how would not come at her will. At last, how- ever, by a huge effort, she mastered herself and it. "Who are you ?" she asked, abruptly, in a voice that trembled a little. The first silence broken a . feeling of resentful anger took the place in part of some of her fear. She was angry with him for frightening her so. "Who are you, and why are you here?" she asked, haughtily. Her second question was just what the man wanted. It saved him from inventing an answer to her first. An air of remorse and apology came over him. He drew himself up, and settled his shoulders as thoneh about to take a plunge off a diving board. "I—l em sorry if I have alarmed you, but is not this Mrs. Dale's flat?" Lavinia hesitated a moment before answering, her eyes fixed keenly on his face. "Yes, it is," she said at last; "but that does not explain how or why you are here. How did you get in?" The stranger gave a little light-hearted laugh. "Well," he said, "I don't wonder at your surprise; to you this must seem a most—a most burglarious proceeding—" "It does," she interposed, drily. "But "-.-and he laughed again—"l can explain." Lavinia's brain was working rapidly, but her eyes never moved from his face. Perhaps her very ignorance of London and London life, her convic- tion that one could suspect nothing too bad, or too exaggerated of the dwellers in it, helped her to a correct conclusion where a more accustomed person might have failed. She laid aside her haughty, suspicious air, and assumed one of a dignified readiness to listen and to accept explana- tions. - "Will you come inside?" she said, and stepped into the room, the man following her. "I expect the servants did not hear you," she said, nonchalantly. "No, I think they could not have," answered the stranger, gravely, but with a little nervous move- ment of the head. "I really began to think the flat was empty, and was on the point of leaving again disappointed." "If you had rung the bell," she said, innocently, "you would have found out at once that it was not empty." "I wish I had," he answered, ruefully. "Yes, it is always wisest to do so when paying a call," responded Miss Morton, severely, with a little flicker of the eyelids, which might have , mean triumph or fear. "But, you see, I thought there would be no one to--" "Oh then you knew the Dales were away?" Only the keenest eye could have seen the shade of annoyance which crossed the stranger's face. "I must explain," he answered, a trifle wearily, "I am a very, very old friend of the Dales, so great a friend that you see they leave the key of their flat with me when they go away." "Which is very wrong indeed of them," said Lavinia, tartly, "when they let their flat." "And I," he went on, ignoring her remark, "and I—run up now and then to see that all is right." "Oh!" she said. "Oh The porter told us that he did that, but I suppose you keep a sort of watchful eye on the porter?" "Yes," said the man, thoughtfully. Be was trying to understand this young woman with the pretty face, and the smile that bewildered and worried him. "It was very remiss of Mrs. Dale not tc have told you that she had let her flat, so that there would be no need to bother you to watch the porter any more for a time; or she should have told us to be prepared for an unexpected and friendly visitor. If we had only known we— we—" "Yes? You—" 1151 "We would have been better prepared." The man shot another sharp, doubtful glance at her, but she was still looking at him with bright, innocent-looking eyes, and that odd smile. "I shall scold Mrs. Dale severely when I write to her. What did you say your .name was—oh, but she is sure to know when I tell her that.the gentleman with whom she left the key of the flat has called. And you will be able to tell her the flat was safe and in good order—as far as you could see. Oh "—with sudden thought— I'nerhaps you would like to go over it--" She stopped suddenly, remembering the servant- less kitchen. The man drew a deep breath thee sounded like relief. For a moment their eyes met, and his had in them a look which bridled the girl's tongue, and made her heart throb heavily with renewed fear. As though conscious of her longing for the inter- view to be ended, he moved towards the door, while she followed him, chatting pleasantly the while of the Dales, the flat, and the charms of Chelsea. She stood by him as he put on his over- coat and crush hat, still chatting and smiling, but with one hand on the bell which sounded down in the porter's quarters. As he turned to bid her good-bye she softly pressed it, but gentle as she was the stranger heard the click and turned quickly with the same look in his eyes that had frightened her so before; it was but a gleam, in a second he had mastered himself, and smiled blandly: "You should not have troubled to ring," he said, kindly. "I can let. myself out," and with that he bowed, passed through the door, and vanished. Lavinia's call down the telephone shot the porter out of his chair like a cork from a champagne bottle. "Follow--" she cried, then her voice died away, and she slid to the floor unconscious. The porter waited patiently for the end of the sentence, then slowly and grumblingly made his way to the top fiat. Meanwhile, the well-dressed stranger walked quietly down and out of the front door, with cursing in his heart, but a civil "Good- night" to the man lumbering up from the area regions. The porter had to ring at the door-bell many times before the sounds reached Lavinia's brain. And when at length she did open the door to him, she was almost too tired and bewildered to explain; besides, what was the use, he could not be over- taken now—and, somehow, she was not sorry. Now,' too, with this solid, stolid specimen of humanity before her; all that had happened seemed so incredible; so absurd, she scarcely liked to tell of it, the whole incident appeared to her almost like a dream she had dreamt long ago, but which was haunting her waking hours. But the porter's dismay at her long Unconscious- ness, and the sight of her white face, roused her, and in a few broken sentences she.had told her story and her suspicions.. The man turned the lights on, and together they went through the rooms, making careful search. In the scullery they found Mrs. Bantry-Jones's jewellery and Mrs. Dale's plate, which had been locked up in a cupboard in one of the bedrooms, in a confused heap on the stove, partly covered by sundry articles of Mr. Dale's clothing. "Then he knew there were no servants, that I was only pretending," thought Lavinia. And, somehow, this thought troubled her more Jhan all the rest of the incident. For the rest of the evening a wicked waste of electricity went on, and Mrs. Bantry-Jones, returning just before midnight, found Lavinia and the porter sitting together in awed silence and a blaze of light. "Oh no, I am not going to let this stupid affair upset my nerves," Lavinia assured her sister, as they parted for the night. But, nevertheless, the first thing she did the next morning was to go out and engage two servants and bind them by wild promises of exorbitant reinunerathin not to quit her until Mrs. Bantry-Jones's lecturing season was over. "Now, with the F-rvants 'about the place, I shall ' not in the least mind spending an evening at home without you, dear." This was in answer to Lois's remark that she had better come with her for the future, she would feel safer. But, seeing that Mrs. Bantry-Jones had, from the moment she heard of the affair of the mysterious stranger, taken to wearing all her jewellery disposed about her person, Lavinia 'felt some little doubt on that head. In any case, though, the matter was settled for the time by the opportune arrival on the scenes of friends who had learnt of their presence in London, and for a while Miss Morton. found her chances of a lonely evening but few. Her time was so fully occupied, in fact, that she awoke one day with a pane of remorse to a realisatior. that her sister's last ecture was to be given that evening, and that she had been to none, so far. "Lois," she said, looking up with eyes laden with self-reproach, "I am going with you to-night, if you will have me, dear. I have been having too much frivolity lately, I feel I need some ballast." . Lois smiled arr.iabl:A. "Do come, darling, I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>LOVE OF LAVINIA.</head>
        <p>LOVE OF LAVINIA.</p>
        <p>By MABEL QUILLER-COUCH, Author of "A Wild Escapade," "A Talent Developed," "The Recovery of Jane Vercoe," "By the Side of the Cornish .Sea,"</p>
        <p>Mrs. Bantry-Jones had a busy season in front of her. She had been asked to speak at nearly a dozen public meeting') of one kind or another, and life seemed very full and precious to her. Colonel Bantry-Jones was away in South Africa, as he had been for two years past, and his wife's heart rejoiced—not at his absence, but that she should have discovered how to fill the aching void, and mitigate the pain of paring, by filling her life with new cares and duties.</p>
        <p>She was of the type of woman who must fill her life very full of something. For some years Colonel Bantry-Jones and his dinners, and a certain exaction which was a dominant trait in his character, had been sufficient for the purpose. Now the needs of humanity for her voice raised in its behalf took the gallant Colonel's plaee. She was a fluent speaker, and could speak well; also she was not hampered by any foolish scruples or narrow- ness as to the subjects she spoke on. Give her the main facts of any case, or any situation, and details sufficient to rouse her wrath, pride, pleasure, pity, or any other sentiment, and she could make a telling speech. "My dear," she said to her sister, Miss Lavinia Morton, one day in late October. "My dear, I have determined to spend a few months in town. I shall take the Dales' flat in Chelsea; they offered it to me. It will be so much pleasanter and better for my nerves to be close at hand than to have to take the long journey' before each meeting, and perhaps go to an hotel after. I find that very upsetting. Miss Morton, aged twenty-one, agreed joyfully. She was staying with Mrs. Bantry-Jones during the time of the latter's grass-widowhood, and was findinc, it intolerably dull; particularly now, when her sister was so constantly absorbed in preparing notes, reading up her subjects, and battling with a voluminous mass of correspondence. She knew nothing of London, but, at any rate, it would be a change, and probably more amusing than life down. in Chipham. While listening to Mrs. Bantry-Jones's speech from a platform one could have no doubt but that she was a perfect housewife, and skilled, manager, for she could give the most practical and abso- lutely sound advice on the management of the home, the nursery, servants, the parish, housing of the poor, emigration, and a host of other subjects ; but for those who sat at her feet it was as well that her life outside the lecture-room was a blank.</p>
        <p>Within two days of her arrival at the Chelsea flat the servants left in a body," as the phrase goes. There were but two of them, but they left with all the commotion of five times their number, and as she lay awake in the dim dawn of the November morning succeeding the exodus, Miss Morton wondered if life in London would be so very different after all. Hurried and wholesale depar- tures from below stairs had lost all interest for her; in fact, there had been times when her sympathies were all with those departing, when she had even envied them their freedom of choice.</p>
        <p>But, once up and dressed and exhilarated by the novelty of everything about her, the desertion did not strike such cold dismay to her heart as such events did at home. True, they knew of no übiquitous char-lady to come in and "do" for them here as was their custom when such emer- gencies arose at home; but, she reflected, there were a number of restaurants where one could get one's meals, or have them sent in from. She rather enjoyed, too, pottering about the compact little flat, and putting it neat. It was very different from the great rambling house at Chipham, where kitchen and dining-room lay so far apart, and one had to prepare every meal oneself, unless one were stoic enough to go without. Where, too, there was a never-ceasing danger of visitors bursting in oR one at all hours. _ _ _</p>
        <p>So Miss Morton rose with a fairly light heart, and got breakfast. Then she went out for a whole day's shop-gazing, and sight-seeing, while Mrs. Bantry-Jones remained at home preparing the speech she was to deliver that evening. Lavinia managed to get home in time to prepare tea for her sister, though she was almost too weary to do so.</p>
        <p>"Lois," she said, with a sigh which was not all weariness, "I am so tired, I think I will not go to the hall to-night, if you don't mind." Mrs. Bantry-Jones looked up with a bland but abstracted look on her mild face. "No, oh, no, certainly, I don't mind, dear." "I think—perhaps, too, it would be scarcely safe to leave the flat," went on Lavinia. "And—and"— with a sudden thought and simultaneous sense of relief—"it would be so wretched for you—to come back and find nothing prepared. You are always so tired after speaking." "Yes, just so, dear. And you have heard me on the subject of The Home ' before, so that you will not miss anything new." Lavinia felt a pang of remorse for the thought which, in spite of herself, flashed into her mind, and grew suddenly more than usually attentive to her elder sister in the matter of tea and cake.</p>
        <p>At 7.30 Mrs. Bantry-Jones, after being: carefully packed by Lavinia into a cab, and handed all her papers, pencils, and note-books, started for her meeting. That done, the girl with a sigh (..? relief climbed the stone stairs again, and let herself into the empty flat. She had left the electric light burning while she was downstairs, but now, her mind deeply imbued with fear of the ruinous costliness of this mode of fighting darkness, she turned it of in the hall, in fact, everywhere but in the little drawing-room which looked out at the back, and where she elected to sit. Then she took a book, drew a big chair up close to the gas fire, and after settling herself snugly down amongst the cushions, realised in a swift flash that she felt horribly lonely. She was not of a timid nature, but she was blessed with highly- strung nerves and a vivid imagination, and her mind was keenly alive to the dark, empty rooms on the other side of the door, and to her loneliness' there at the top of that big building in which she did not know a human creature by sight even. She was too unaccustomed to flats to feel the security most feel in them. However, her own unusual nervousness nerved her as much as anything could to take a sensible view of the situation, and try to pull herself together. "You have to stay here for four solid hours alone," she said to herself, "so you may as well put all nonsense out of your head, and not be foolish. As if any harm could come to you up here, in this snug little place, all locked in so safely." But she did wish she had carefully examined every door and window, and shot every available bolt as well as lock. However, it must be all right, she told herself; at any rate, nothing could induce her to go out of that room now to make a tour of inspection. She exchanged her book for another which looked more frankly frivolous and diverting, placed a box of chocolates on a stool beside her, and fixed her mind with Spartan firmness on the page open before her. After awhile she did overcome the panic which had seized her, and was able to laugh at herself for it, but had Mrs. Bantry-Jones come back just then and announced that the meeting was "off " she would have met with a warmth of welcome to which she was scarcely accustomed on such occasions.</p>
        <p>However, at last, in a fixed determination ,not to look at. the cloe.k again for quite a long time, Lavinia really did become absorbed in her book, so absorbed that when, after what was really nearly an hour, faint sounds of movements about. the place reached her ears in a dim, confused way, she, without thought and, forgetting the exodus of yesterday, put them down, if she thought of them at all, as coming from the servants, or from without, and paid no heed. But at last the sound of a heavy footstep in the hall, a footstep she did not know, brought her suddenly to a realisation of the truth that there was someone in the flat besides herself, and brought her, too, a nervous terror which made her sick with fear. For a moment she remained rooted to ;wr chair, her big brown eyes fixed, wide with fent% on the closed door. She had a feeling of being trapped ' • she thought she should yell, shriek, go inad• if that step came nearer; and the door was opened. Then, with a bound she Ivas on her feet, her horror of being shut in there. waiting for she knew not what, was greater than that of facing the danger. She flung the doom open wide, and found herself face to face witb tall young man clad in evening dre!is, if she was surprised at sight of him, probab!, no man had ever before looked more utterly con. founded by the sight of a pretty girl. For at appreciable space of time they stood staring at eack other without 'attempting to speak. The sterantli</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <p>Aril be delighted. I am speaking on the 'Reclaiming of the Criminal Classes.' Most interesting. I wish you had been at the last; in fact, I have often wished you were with me. I have come across such interesting peop're, so good for you to have met. They will be there to-night; • I shall be so glad to introduce you to them. I—l thought, dear, we might give a little farewell party, and ask as many as we can of those who have supported me, and fought with me side by side in this campaign." Mrs. Bantry-Jones loved to think of herself as a fighting champion, and martyr, in the causes she took up. Lavinia assented gladly, in her aniiety to make up for her neglect, and spent the morning writing out invitations to persons of whom, for the greater part, she scarcely knew the names. "Mrs. Stonely- Stone, Mrs. Messinger, Mr. Jones, Mr. Marsell," she read. "Is that the way •to spell Marsell, Lois? I hope he will not be offended if I have made a mistake." "Oh, no," said Lois, placidly. She had no notion how his name was spelt. "Oh, no, he is such a delightful man, and so amiable, I particularly want to introduce him to you. He looks so sad, poor fellow—quite young, and so accomplished. He has been my right-hand man just lately," and so on and so on, to deaf ears. Lavinia was busy ,vriting, her thoughts having shot far away from Mr. Marsell. She knew Lois's panegyrics of old her geese Were always swans, for a time. The audience to listen to the speeches on the "Reclahuing of the Criminal Classes " was fairly large that night. Lavinia, following her sister up the room, looked curiously at the crowd, and wondered. People must be very badly off for amusement, she thought. Mrs. Bantry-Jones introduced her to a person here and a person there, until Lavinia felt she would soon have a bowing acquaintance with the whole room, reclaimed and otherwise. "Ah there is Mr. Marsell. How do you do? So pleased to see you." Lavinia turned with just • the least feeling of interest, arising:, from the fact that this was the first name she had really heard distinctly of all those murmured before her. For the moment Lois stood between her and the Etranger she was welcoming so cordially. "I want to introduce you to my sister, Miss Morton. Now, where can she be? Lavinia! Oh, there you are! I have brought Mr. Marsell to introduce to you. It seems strange that you two should never have met until now—" She stopped suddenly, looking from one to the other wonderingly. "You --you have not, I think?" "You looked so odd, I began to wonder," she said, speaking to her sister later. "I wondered, too," answered Lavinia. At the anoment she had been the first to recover herself. "No, I think this is the first time I have had the pleasure of an introduction," she responded, in answer to her sister's curious glances. "I deeply regret that it is so," said Mr. Marsell, coolly, but he did not meet her eyes again. Lois went away and left them together, obviously bent on giving them an opportunity of improving the acquaintance. An embarrassing silence fell. Miss Morton was the first to break it. "In what capacity are you here?" she asked. Then shis could have flayed herself for the uninten- tional bad taste of her remark. The man for a second lost his hold over himself, and coloured hotly. "I—l mean—" She stammered, blushing crimson, too, over face and neck, her eyes misty with confusion. "Forgive me. I—l think—my sister said you were—were secretary—or—or some- thing." For a long moment they looked into each other's eyes. "By your olemency, yes—for the time," he said, softly. He looked almost pleadingly into the brown eyes fixed on her face, the expression of which was quite inscrutable. A light was shining down on her, and lighting up the copper-coloured hair as he had seen it once before, and, as then, her eyes were in the shadow. Lois, looking at the two, was surprised and delighted to see how absorbed they were in each other. ."Perhaps he will instill in dear Lavinia a love for the deeper and more serious interests of life," she murmured. Miss Morton at that moment had forgotten Lois, and her speech, and why they were there—every- thing, in fact, but the man before, her. With her mind's eye she saw again that incriminating heap of things piled up ready to be taken away, and it seemed incredible that the two could ever have been connected. "Oh, .how could you!" The words came almost like a thought made tangible. They escaped her without her will. The man looked at her with anxious eyes and bent. head. "Heaven knows !" he answered, softly. The chairman rapped on the table, and called the room to order, and Lois began to speak; but neither Lavinia nor her companion heard a word. "He really is mcst charming," gushed Lois, as the carriage drove off, leaving Mr. Marsell bowing on the pavement. "I really think, dear"—with a little apologetic latieh, for she knew her sister hated such remarks—"l really think it is a case of love at first sight." "It certainly was not that," said Lavinia, in a manner which puzzled Lois considerably. "I do wish you would not say such things, Lois." "I can't get rid of the feeling that you have met before," went on Lois, imperturbably. "I felt it the moment I brought him up. Had you, dear?" "In a previous state of existence, perhaps," said Lavinia, lightly. Then she yawned wearily, and professed herself very tired and hungry. Mr. Mantel' accepted Mrs. Bantry-Jones's ievita- tion to her "At Home." Throughout breakfast-time he was abstracted in his manner. The meal over, he wrote a reply to Mrs. Bantry-Jones, but did not post it. At lunch-time he wrote another, and posted it at once. Whatever their respective contents may have been Mr. Marsell arrived in good time at the flat in Chelsea; but, though the owner of a latch-key, he rang for admittance this time, and was ushered in with due ceremony by one of the new maids. Lavinia was crossing the hall as he entered, and the hot colour flew to both their faces as the same thought flashed through their Minds, but she stopped and.shook hands with him in her best manner. Mrs. Bantry-Jones was enjoying herself to the full, the dining-rooin was packed, the drawing-room overflowing with a chattering, shrieking crowd. Everyone was talking at once, and entirely, as it proved, for his or her own edification, for never an inch could a word carry beyond the lips it issued from. Fortunately for her peace of mind, she had no opportunity to notice that Mr. Marsell seemed to be not in his usual spirits. Miss Morton seemed given up heart and soul to entertaining and looking after her sister's guests. She NV:LS untiring, she gave herself no rest, being bent, in fact, upon avoiding speech with anyone. Henry Marsell, standing moodily watching her, his hungry eyes taking in every detail of her long, soft white gown, her beautiful bronze hair, her grave face, knew instinctively that she was avoiding him. Once only he felt her glance light, on him, and he looked at her suddenly, pleadingly. But she only let her eyes fall, and turned away. The evening was nearly over when at last, by good fortune, he found her alone. She had dropped, utterly weary, on a couch drawn up by the open window in the little hail; her companion, noticing her fatigue, had gone off to get her a cup of coffee. Henry Mansell watched him go, then seized his opportunity. "I am going," he said; abruptly. say good-bye?" She lifted her weary eyes to his, the tiredness 'banished for the moment by another look of which she was absolutely unconscious. "Going!" she said. "So soon" He did not answer, and she sank back .on the cushions with a sigh which might have been of fatigue, or something else. "you will be leaving here—on Saturday, he said, slowly, "and I shall never see you again. I wanted to thank you, and to ask you to say good-bye." He spoke in a monotonous, dejected voice. The tears' sprang to the girl's eyes, she was so tired, so depressed. She wanted to speak, but she did not know what to say, everything seemed too small—or • too-- He turned to her suddenly, a look in his eyes she could not meet. She put up a little shaking hand and push.d her warm hair off her forehead with an impatient, distracted gesture. "Lavinia, if things had been—been different— could it—ever have been—possible?" He bent over her as she lay back on the cushions, the passion in his eyes compelling her to speech. Her timid eyes met his in one long look of misery, and reproachful love. it—could," she whispered through white lips. • "And—now ?" Her hands were clenched tightly, her face was white as her gown; she kept her eyes resolutely turned away from him. "It—it must be possible—to forget," she moaned rather than svoke. "Good-bye." [TILE END. I ..77 • 0"</p>
        <p>"Will you</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <p>THE WORKMAN'S HOME.</p>
        <p>WHAT HE EARNS AND WHAT HE SPENDS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>WILL PROTECTION HELP HIM?</head>
        <p>WILL PROTECTION HELP HIM?</p>
        <p>MR. CHAMBERLAIN has told us that the working man is more deeply concerned in the Fiscal controversy than any other class of society. If we include the workman's wife and his children, there can be no doubt of tho truth of that statement. Any additional burdens that may be placed upon the workers must of necessity fall heaviest upon the women and children. If through higher prices or lower wages some member of the workman's family has to have poorer food, scantier clothing, or less comfort in the home, it is, generally, not the man who goes without. This is not due to any selfishness on his part; the sacrifice of the women and children often goes on unknown to the husband and father. The woman knows that unless her husband is sufficiently fed he will be unable to do his work; his health would break down, and then there would be no more wages for days and weeks to come. So when food is dear, or wages are low, it .is the woman and children who suffer. It is the same with clothing, boots, and shoes. The man, who has to face all weathers at his work, must be well pro- tected against cold and chill. So the woman's clothes and the children's boots have to last a little longer. The man does not know about these things. He does not often inquire how it is that the woman contrives not to deprive him. of the comforts of life, or how she manages to have a bright fire burning when he comes home, although coals have gone up and the amount of money spent upon coal remains the same. 'What will happen, then, if we are to increase the burdens on the workman's home?</p>
        <p>Domestic Economy. To understand the conditions of domestic economics and their relation to Mr. Chamber- lain's proposals, it is necessary to consider three points</p>
        <p>1. What the workman now earns. 2. What he now buys with his earnings. 3. Will Protection increase his expenses</p>
        <p>or his wages? . The above three points cover the whole problem so far as the workman's home is con- cerned, and upon the answer to these points depends the workman's decision for or against Protection.</p>
        <p>The Workman's 'Wages. THE average wage of the British town workman employed in a trade is (outside London) 365. per week. But throughout the country the number of skilled workmen is very much smaller than the number of unskilled labourers. Even in the best towns the number of labourers is much larger than -the number of skilled workmen. Thus in the city of York, where Mr. B. S. Rowntree has made such careful investigations into the conditions of the workers, no less than 22,159 persons belong to families where the income from all sources is less than 30s. per week. York is a city where women and young girls find a considerable amount of employment, and so the chances are that the earnings of the fathers of families are considerably less than 30s. Thus when we get into the next class (consisting of 24,595 people belonging to families where the total income is over 30s. per week) we find that the average wage of the father of the family is only RI 9s. Bid. So if we take the workmen throughout the towns, skilled and unskilled together, we find their wages will not average 30s. per family. On the contrary, a verylarge number do not average 21s. Now we have to see how the wages are spent.</p>
        <p>The Workman's Expenses. THE expenses of a workman's home may bo reckoned as follows: Food, rent, fuel, cloth- inn., household sundries, insurance, and sick club. How much has to be spent on each of the above items in an average family of five persons, consisting of husband, wife, and three children ? Let us take each item separately. Food.—The cheapest possible food provided by the workhouses of Great Britain, giving no fresh meat, and only bacon on three days of the week, costs as follows: Man, 35.• 3d. ; woman, 2s. 9c1.; three children, at 2s. 3d. each; total, 12s. 9d. To provide fresh meat would at least mean another 35.; total for food, 15s. 9d. _Rent.—This may vary from is. 6d. in the country to ss. 6d. in the towns. An average amount would be 38. 6d. per week. Fuel.—Coals and coke will vary from is. 6d. to 9s. per week. A. safe average would be Is. 10d. Clothing.—The lowest estimate at which the cost of clothing can be put is 6d. per week for each adult and sd. for each child. Total, 2s. 3d. Household Sundries.—These include soap, cil, matches, utensils, repairs, and cannot bo reckoned at less than 6d. per week. Insurance and Sick Club.—ln all probability the husband, wife, and children are insured. The cost will be about 2d. each per week; total, 10d. " These amounts will total up as follows: £s.d. Food. Rent . 0 3 6 Fuel . ...... 0 110 Clothing 0 2 3 Sundries 0 0 6 Insurance</p>
        <p>Total It will be seen that no allowance is made fcr beer, tobacco, papers, books, or any form of recreation, either mental or physical. In addition, no mention is made of provision against loss of work, either through slackness of trade or sickness.</p>
        <p>Protection, Expenses, and Wages. IT will be perfectly clear from our review of the earnings of the working classes that it would be impossible to increase the cost of living without creating very serious hardship. There is no doubt that even under present conditions a very large section of the working classes have not sufficient food, clothing, and home comforts. Any increase, therefore, in the cost of the workman's housekeeping would inflict terrible penalties upon the workman and his family. Protection we were told by Mr. Chamberlain, would increase the cost of living. The one purpose of Protection is to raise prices. But by raising prices you must necessarily decrease the demand. By decreasing the demand you must necessarily decrease employ- ment. By decreasing employment you must decrease the wages of the worker. \ What, then, is the prospect which Protection holds out to the workman and his wife Less Employment. Less Wages. Dear Living. As the British workman begins more and more to understand the truth about Protection, so Mr. Chamberlain's chances of capturing the country fade away. Norwich, Gateshead, Mid- lierts, Normanton, East Dorset, all tell the same, tale; the workman . will not touch Protection.</p>
