      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>EMPEROR OF JAPAN</head>
        <p>THE EMPEROR OF JAPAN</p>
        <p>and the Empire from degradation." Thus ran the wording of the official protest, and the principal speaker was Mr. John Burns, M.P. for Battersea, who always carries great weight, especially with the London working man. He was supported by Mr. W. Crooks and other Members of Parliament; Dr. Clifford, the Rev. F. B. Meyer, Mrs. Despard, who is a sister of General French; and many others. The speeches lasted for two hours, after which silence was called by bugles, and the motion was put to the entire meeting simultaneously. It was received with tre- mendous cheering, after which the crowd gra dually broke up in an orderly manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>WAR IN THE FAR EAST.</head>
        <p>THE WAR IN THE FAR EAST.</p>
        <p>The war in the Far East still continues be be the most important topic of the moment, but, despite a great crowd of correspondents. very little actual news emanates from the seat of the campaign. This is due to two causes, one that there is no news in particu- lar, and the other that a very strict censor- ship is exorcised, more especially by Japan, over details of any real importance. Though neither side is wasting any time, nevertheless nothing much is expected to occur for another month or two, unless Port Arthur should fall. Constant attacks are being made upon the great fortress, but in this country we are really in the dark as to how much damage has been actually done or how far the garrison is in straits. Since Vice- Admiral Makaroff assumed command things seem to have taken a tone of more quietness and confidence in the garrison at Port Arthur, but Admiral Togo still continues his system of perpetual harassing attacks. How- ever, it is reported that the morale of the Russians has been restored, and they speak cheerfully of their prospects, especially with the aid of the great Kuropatldn. The fact of the matter is that the weather is dictat- ing the present lull as much as any other cause. At the start of the war the Japanese struck several deadly blows at sea, which left them complete masters afloat, and nothing particular was left for their fleet to do except to blockade Port Arthur and prevent the remnant of the Russian ships from inter- fering with their convoys. Turning their attention to the land campaign, they found that the weather made it impossible for them to progress very quickly, and this was even more the case with the Russians. So several weeks have been spent mainly in preparations under adverse conditions, and until the weather breaks nothing sensational, and probably little beyond outpost skir- mishes, was expected. Experts say that the real crisis of the war will come in July and August, when both sides have their troops drawn up on land and favourably disposed, with plenty of transport and equipment, and Russia has sent round more ships from the Baltic to give her the preponderance at sea. Meanwhile, the excitement has not abated, and both countries are showing splendid pluck and patriotism. There are even rumours of both the Tsar and the Mikado going to the front in person in the summer, and already members of both Royal Houses are in the fighting line, of whom the Grand Duke Cyril is reported wounded from Port Arthur.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>REPRESENTATION OF THE ISLE OF WIGHT,</head>
        <p>THE REPRESENTATION OF THE ISLE OF WIGHT,</p>
        <p>Parliament rose last week after a very exciting opening of the Session for the Easter vacation. Meeting on February 2nd, the House of Commons sat forty-one days and worked their full time of eight hours and a half. These sittings have principally been remarkable for the large num- ber of set de- bates and the various votes of "no confidence" in the Govern- ment and mo- tions to adjourn the House. Once the Government was actually de- feated on a catch vote on the Irish Educa- tion Estimates, and many of the debates have been very I, heated. How- ever, probably the most sen- sationalincident AJOR D.S 0. . of all was Major Seely's public resignation of his seat and renunciation of the Government on March 2 Ist during the official Opposition vote of censure. He has openly differed from his party for some time on fiscal matters and the Army problem, to which is now added the question of Chinese labour in Africa, and it was on that rock that he finally split. Major Seely, who represented the Isle .of Wight, sent his resignation to his constituents, but now offers himself for re-election, and is likely to be un• opposed.</p>
        <p>05(;/* / g /10 • , :41 SEELv</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>HYDE PARK DEMONSTRATION.</head>
        <p>HYDE PARK DEMONSTRATION.</p>
        <p>knother outcome of the Chinese Labour 4nestion was the great demonstration held in Hyde Park to 'protest against the Ordinance. Twenty thousand people, accompanied by bands, took part in the actual procession throuLth London, which mustered on the Victoria Embankment and made its way by Victoria Street and Grosvenor Place to the Park, where platforms had been set up round the Reformer's Tree. Some fifty thousand A</p>
        <p>cl.egrd</p>
        <p>1/</p>
        <p>MR. JOHN BURNS, M.P., SPEAWING FROM NO. 4</p>
        <p>PLATFORM.</p>
        <p>more there swelled the gathering, and there was a great deal of vigorous oratory in favour of the motion that "this mass meeting of all classes of the citizens of London emphatically protests against the action of the Government in granting permission to import into South Africa Chinese labour upon conditions of Elavery, and calls upon them to protect the ggew colony from the greed of the capitalists,</p>
