      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>KENSAL RISE TRAGEDY.</head>
        <p>THE KENSAL RISE TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>The horrible tragedy of Kensal Rise has tome and gone in a day or two; and it will soon doubtless be forgotten, as the mur- derer took the law into his own hands, dispensing with the necessity of a trial. Had one taken place, it would have ranked with the most sensational of the century, and Crossman only missed being put upon his trial by a few hours, as the police had arranged to ar- rest him the day after his suicide upon a charge of fraud upon the Post Office, when thegreater tragedy would have stood re- vealed. The story of the Kensal Rise hor- ror recalls the deeds of Deem- ing the Rainhill NURSE SAMPSON. murderer, and, to a lesser degree, those of Wainwrighk.; whose case is historical. The tale of the awful evening, when the lodger called in the police to investigate the heavy tin trunk, which stank so ,terribly, and Crossman bolted upon their arrival, cutting his own throat as he ran, when the police began to overhaul him, is now familiar to the whole world. His first wife died in 1897; and shortly after- wards he mar- ried both a Miss Annie Farley and a Miss Pollie Osborne. This act of bigamy was dis- covered when be was under- going his trial on a lesser count, and im- mediately he was prosecuted upon the charge of bigamy, and stntenced to seven years' penal servitude. Since he came out on ticket-of - leave, GROSSMAN, THE MURDERER towards tihe AND BIGAMIST. end of 1902, nothing deterred, he married four other wives and played them one off against the other, except in the case of the wretched victim of the murder. She turned out to be a Nurse Sampson, a widow, whom he married on January 15th of last year, and murdered the following morning when she was dressing. Seldom has a more cold-blooded case of cri.ne been brought to light; and the world is well rid of Crossman.</p>
