      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION CUP COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION CUP COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>THIRD ROUND.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON EXTINGUISH PONTEFRACT'S HOPES.</p>
        <p>The Warrington team journeyed to Pontefract, full of confidence in their ability to overcome the Pontefract barrier to the semi-final in the cup competition. Two special excursion trains conveyed about 1,000 Warring- ton supporters to the Yorkshire town, and the great interest manifested in the match was reflected in the attendance on the Pontefract enclosure, which reached over 11,000, the receipts being £254. Promptly to time, the teams lined up as follow:— Warrington.—Hallam, back; Fish, Ishermsod,Dicken- son, and Harris, three-quarter backs; Bate and Davies, half backs; Boardman, Swift, Cook, Lunt, Naylor, Edmondson Morrison, and Jolley, forwards. Pontefract.—Smith, back; Davison, Driscoll, Ward, and Connell, three-quarter backs: Brimyard and Booth half backs; Sims, Eyre, Colne, Parkinson, Debney, Robinson, Cookson and Foster, forwards. . Referee, Mr. W. Cookson, Touch judges, Messrs. H. Sewell, Leeds,and H. Wainwright,Wakefield. Pontefract won the toss, and they had the first advantage of playing with a strong wind in their favour Dickenson set the ball in motion,imd play at once settled in the visitors' quarters, Smith, the home back find- ing touch by judicious kicking. Fish and Isherwood put a check to a couple of dangerous rushes, bil; the home forwards were playing a dashing game, and had it not been for the clever defence of Hallam, Pontefract would have been over. Davies wastackied by Brunyard and from the succeeding scrummage Wairington were penalised. Smith sent the ball over the bar, and thus early on gave his side the lead. On the re-start Warrington reached the home end through some good forward work, but Booth on two occasions saved in brilliant style, first tackling Fish and then Harris. Getting possession in the next scrum- mage the home forwards worked their way to the other end of the field, but the resourcefulness of Fish was again brought into play, his tackling being exceedingly strong. For some time the visitors were kept busily defending, the home forwards attacking in a most de- liberate style, but little opportunity was given the backs, the front line evidently relying on theirstrength to break down the barrier. The defence,however,of Hallam, Isherwood, Dickenson, and Fish was superb,and through their efforts Pontefract were gradually forced back. Dickenson received a nasty kick on the shin and was in- capacitated a few minutes. Then came a fine rally on the part of the Warrington forwards,Lunt being at the head of a smart dribble, which took the play to the Pontefract" 25." Here the home side were penalised for obstructing Fish. Davies had got away, with Fish at his heels,when Davison foully charged Fish before he could get the ball. Referee McCutcheon allowed a penalty, and Fish made a splendid attempt at goal, only just missing by a few inches. Again asserting their strength the home forwards broke smartly away, Sims and Parkinson not being pulled up until the Warring- ton line was reached. A dribble by Swift, Boardman and Davies transferred play to the other end, and after a series of exchange kicks, Smith was forced to touch ' down. The home forwards continued to play a bustling " game, and some close scrimmages took place near the I Warrington line. The referee, under the false impres- ' sionthat Davies was off-side, penalised Warrington and "Smith kicked a second goal. This brought about half- time, when Pontefract led by 2 goals (4 points) to War- rington nil. I Warrington now having the advantage of the wind, it was fully expected that they would make good use of their opportunity, but on play settling down after , Parkinson's kick off it was again seen that the Ponte- fract forwards were sticking gamely to their work, and the Warrington halves never seemed able to open out the game for the backs. Davies was injured in a rough tackle. and limped about the field for.. some time. At last, Warrington' through some judicious kicking into andtouch by Fish and Dickenson reached the Pontefract end, but Driscoll after covering a mistake made by Smith once more gave his side relief with a run and kick into touch. At leng'.l Pontefract were penalised, and Fish took the kick from near the half-way line He kicked the goal. Following the kick out the home side attacked in a most determined manner. It was a final effort, however, as near the half-way line the Warrington backs began to give a taste of their quality. The Pontefract captain, with the idea of keeping down the scoring, took a man out of the pack and placed five three-quarters. This led to the downfall of the home team. The Warrington forwards now got possession and Pontefract had to take up a defensive position. From a scrummage near the touch line Pontefract were penalised,and from a difficult angle Fish landed a penalty goal. Now that the teams were even, both, sides tried their utmost to get the mastership, which eventually rested with Warri4ton. The latter portion of the game was bristling with incidents, and some really first-class I play was shown by the Warrington men. A pass between Bate, Davies and Dickerson sent the ball along to Harris, who cleverly evaded Smith, and scored a try,but Fish failed at goal. Shortly afterwards. Bate tricked his opponent and serving Fish at the right moment that player wriggled himself over the line but he failed at goal. A stiff contest ended with the result :—Warring- ton 2 goals and 2 tries (10 points), to Pontefract 2 goals (4 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>SALFORD v. HUNSLET.</head>
        <p>SALFORD v. HUNSLET.</p>
        <p>At Weaste,Salford,in wet weather,before 7,000 people In the first half, Hunslet played a fine forward game, and, after Lomas had kicked a penalty goal for Salford, Price got over for the visitors. J. Goldthorp landed the goal. The second half saw play of an even charac- ter, but there was no further score. Result—Hunslet, 1 goal 1 try (5 points); Salford, 1 goal (2 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>HALIFAX v. LEEDS.</head>
        <p>HALIFAX v. LEEDS.</p>
        <p>On the Hanson-lane enclosure. Halifax before 18,000 people. Leeds won the toss, playing with the wind. Halifax scored a try in the first ten minutes, but Little- wood reduced the lead by two points before the interval The homesters quickly added five more points after the interval. Leeds could not reduce the lead, playing against the wind. Result—Halifax, 8 points ;. Leeds, 2' points. BROUGHTON RANGERS v. BRADFORD.</p>
        <p>This important match was played at Broit ghton,before 8,000 spectators. The ground was in a wretched condi- tion, being practically a sea of mud,and,naturally under the conditions, a poor game was witnessed. There was no score in the first half, and, though Whitehead was ordered off, the Rangers held their own in the second half. R33ult—Broughton Rangers, nil ; Bradford, nil. —The replay at Bradford on Tuesday was witnessed by 26,080 people, bat again the teams failed to score a single point.</p>
        <p>No Breakfast Table complete without</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <p>. -</p>
        <p>GRATEFUL-COMFORTING.</p>
        <p>Co 0A</p>
        <p>The Most Nutritious and Economical.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>NORTHERN LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY'S MATCHES.</p>
        <p>SWINTON v. HULL.</p>
        <p>At Swinton, before 600 spectators. Heavy rain fell througbout,and the game remained drawn at the close of a'scratubling encounter. In thefirst half Swinton scored from a forward rush, through Smith, but Morgan failed to add the extra points. Hull were credited with a try, registered by Jacksciiii Whose fine play redeemed an otherwise unattractive gatife. Result—Swinton, 3 points; Hull, 3 points. HULL KINGSTON ROVERS v. WIDNES.</p>
        <p>At Hull, before 4,000 spectators. Widnes kicked off against a smart breeze. In about ten minutes Madeley crossed the line near the posts, and Starks easily con- verted. Carmichael soon after scored an unconverted try, and Gordon also crossed the line. On the restart Starks scored an unconverted try. Madeley followed with a further try, Gordon a try, Ctrmichael a goal, and Beacock an unconverted try. Result—Rovers, 2 goals, 6 tries (22 points) ; Widnes, 1 try (3 points). HUDDERSFIELD v. KEIGHLEY.</p>
        <p>At Huddersfield, before 3,000 p3ople. In the first half Sutcliffe scored an unconverted try, and Lewis dropped a goal for Huddersfield. Immediatelvafter the restart Brady scored an unconverted try. Walton had an unsuccessful drop at goal. Lewis scored an uncon- verted try. R3sult—Huddersfield, 1 goal, 3 tries ; Keighley, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>MONDAY'S MATCHES.</head>
        <p>MONDAY'S MATCHES.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN LEAGUE.-FIRST DIVISION. WIGAN v. SALFORD.</p>
        <p>At Central Park, Wigan, before 15,000 spectators. Salford pressed at the start,and Norris failed to negotiate a pass from Lomas, when a score appeared certain. Anderson and Mason missed goals. Salford kept up a strong attack. Anderson relieving in good style. Wigan retaliated, and Mason goaled from a penalty. Preston scored for Salford, and Lomas droppeda goal. Result— Salford, 5 points; Wigan, 2 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>BROUGHTON RANGERS v. KEIGHLEY.</head>
        <p>BROUGHTON RANGERS v. KEIGHLEY.</p>
        <p>At Broughton yesterday, in fine weather, before 5,000 people. From the start the Rangers attacked, and Hardisty scored an unimproved try. Crocker dashed over for the visitors, and Walker kicked a goal. The second half went in favour of Broughton, for whom Ruddick and Widdeson scored. Walker also kicked a goal. Result—Broughton Rangers, 1 goal 3 tries (11 points); Keighley, 1 goal 1 try (5 points.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>OLDHAM V. SWINTON.</head>
        <p>OLDHAM V. SWINTON.</p>
        <p>At Watersheddings, before 4,000 spectators. it was a, pretty open game all through in the first half. Ferguson kicked two penalty goals, and Dicky 'Moms dropped a' goal, this being the extent of the scoring. In the second half Spottiswood scored a try, and Dixon kicked a goal for Swinton, and Sam Lees one for Oldham. Oldham won by 11 points to 2 points. HALIFAX v. HIINSLET.</p>
        <p>At Halifax. The homesters kicked off before a moderate attendance. Hunslet quickly added a try, which was converted. Halifax tried to score before the interval, but failed. Hunslet added a dropped goal. Halifax were near scoring on several occasions, but lacked finish. Hunslet pressed continually until the close. Rasult—Hunslet, 7 points; Halifax, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>LEEDS V. HULL.</head>
        <p>LEEDS V. HULL.</p>
        <p>At Leeds, before 6.000 people. In the first half Leeds had the aid of a strong wind, and Ward scored, Little- wood failing at goal. After the interval, a mistake by Evans in losing possession ended in Bland getting a try for Hull, Jackson's kick at goal proving abortive. Evans and Mosley scored again for Leeds, who won by U. points to 5 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>WARRINGTON v. ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>MTH-WEST LANCASHIRE LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>As a Bank Holiday engagement, St. Helens proved a great attraction on the %Vilderspool enclosure, and a crowd of übout 6,000 spectators witnessed the match. The Warrington team had undergone a little alteration from that which played on the previous Saturday, Bate and Dickenson being given a rest. The Warrington team was as follows :</p>
        <p>Warrington : Hallam back; Fish, Isherwood, Mahoney and Harris, three-quarters; Hockenhall and Davies, half backs; Jolley, Boardman' Edmondson,, Swift, Cook, Morrison, Lunt and Naylor, forwards. The visitors kicked off against the wind, and after a quiet opening Fish took a penalty kick, but missed the goal. Following this, Davies got clear away from the scrummage, but Jones overhauled him on the line. Davies parted with the ball to Hockenhall, but that player failed to hold the leather, and lost a certain try. From a scrummage on the quarter line, Davies got popession and passed to Mahoney, who transferred to Isherwood, and that player passed the ball on to Fish, who outran all opposition, and grounded the ball behind the post. The same player missed the goal kick. Jones restarted, and soon afterwards Hockenhall got away from the scrimmage and passed to Fish, who when challenged by Barton retransferred to Hocken- hall, who scored easily. Fish again failed at goal. Warrington continued to press, and the next score came in rather a peculiar manner. A scrimmage had been caused by the home forwards, and Davies shot away. He tried to pass to Hockenhall, but St. Helens endeavoured to intercept him, and the ball was knocked backwards. Davies secured again and scored at the corner. Fish once more failed at the goal. After the restart Fish was almost over again. Creevy tackled him after a long run. St. Helens were again penalised and Drvies failed at the goal kick. A splendid dribble by the St. Helens forwards gained an immense amount of ground. and the ball was carried over the Warrington line, but the referee disallowed the try. A long kick in touch by Fish again placed them on the aggressive, and St. Helens being penalised in front of their goalposts, Fish scored a goal. Half-time : Warrington Erpoints, St. Helens, nil. Fish restarted and St. Helens making full use of the wind forced Fish to touch down. The Warrington backs combined together splendidly and a beautiful move- ment between Isherwood and Fish proved to be the tit- bit of the afternoon. The ball was passed and repassed between the two players and the movement resulted in Fish clearing himself of all opposition and scoring a try under the posts. Fishadded a goal. A loan' dribble initiated by Davies and taken up by Hockenhalrresulted in another try for Warrington Davies being the scorer. St. Helens profited by a mull by Mahoney, and Briers scoreda try. This completed the s3oring and Warring- ton_won by two goals, five tries (19 points) to St. Helens one try (3 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>" INTERNATIONAL"</head>
        <p>THE " INTERNATIONAL"</p>
        <p>ENGLAND V. OTHER NATIONALITIES.</p>
        <p>This postponed match was played at Wigan on Tues- day, before 5000 spectators. Fish opened the scoring for England, but Thomas (Oldham) and Thomas (Salford) replied for other Nationalities before half- time. Subsequently Harris (Wigan) added a third try for Other Nationalities, who won by 3 tries (9 points) to a try (3 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>WARRIKGTON " A '• v. \VEGAN " A."—These teams met at Wilderspool on Saturday in miserable Weather. In the first half Warrington had the advantage of the breeze, and were almost persistently on the attack. Kenyon scored a couple of tries, from one of which Knight placed a goal, so at the interval the Warrington men led by a goal and two tries (eight points) to nil. There was no further scoring in the second half.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>V/lIRRINGTON WORKS LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>V/lIRRINGTON WORKS LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>LOCO'S v. RYLANDS'.</p>
        <p>These teams met to determine the championship of the league 0.1 Monday Morning. The Loco's had the assistance of a strong breeze during the first half, and utilizing it to the full were chiefly aggressive. Two tries were scored by Allen and Jackson, but in neither case did the goal follow, and at the interval the score was six points in favour of the railway men. Turning round, Rylands' pressed, and Morris kicked a penalty goal. The Loco's again asserted themselves, and a fine round of passing resulted in a try by Allen, which Timmins improved upon. Final: Loco's, one goal three tries (eleven points); Itylands', one goal (two points.)</p>
        <p>FAILURES IN ENGLAND AND WALES.—According to Kemp's Mercantile Gazette," the total number of failures recorded in England and Wales during the week ending Friday, April Ist, was 170, viz.:—New Bank- ruptcy Proceedings published in the London Gazette, 76; deeds of arrangement registered, 94. The res- pective numbers in the corresponding week of last year were : Bankruptcies, 74 ; deeds of arrangement, 77 total 151; being a net increase in bankruptcies of 76, and a net increase in deeds of arrangement in 1904, to date, of 124.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Money back</head>
        <p>Money back</p>
        <p>That's one of the differences between Fels-Naptha and other things to wash-with. They don't dare; you'd take the money. Fels-Naptha 39 Wilson street London E C</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. i The Final Meeting of the Old Board. A meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians— the last of the expiring board,—was held in the : Board room at the Workhouse on Wednesday morning. Mr. A. Brooks presided and there were also present: Mrs. Clay, Rev. T. R. Matthews, Messrs. r. H. Ashcroft, J. Billinge, J. Clarke,S. Davies, T. Halton, W. Hickman, W. Houghton, A. J. Jolley, A. Jolley, P. Longton, G. Mather, J. Turton, with Messrs. J. C. Sutton (clerk), J. F. Awbery (master) and A. Wright (superintendent of Padgate Schools).</p>
        <p>CONTRACTS. The House Committee recommended the acceptance of the following tenders : drapery and tailors' sundries, Mr. T. J. Lee; boots and shoes, Messrs. Roberts and Son; coal and slack, Messrs. Evans and Company; groceries Messrs. H. Milling and Company. It was re- ported groceries, the number of inmates in the House for the week ending Lady Day was 530. Mr. AIATHER moved the confirmation of the minutes. Mr. S. DA VIES seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. HICKM,,LN asked whether a letter had not been received with reference to the coal contract. The CHAIRMAN replied in the atfirmative,-and said it would be considered by the House Committee at the next meeting.</p>
        <p>Mr. McimAs asked whether the coal contract was signed. The Clerk said, it was. The minutes were passed. THE CHAIRMAN THANKED FOR HIS SERVICES. Mr. S. DAVIES said all the members would be aware that that was the last meeting, and he had therefore pleasure in proposing a hearty vote of thanks to the Chairman for his services. He had no hesitation in say- ing that the Chairman had performed the duties to their entire satisfaction, and had displayed tact and judgment equal to any of his predecessors. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Brookes had not only been faithful in the discharge of his duties, but he had shown strict impartiality, and that wasa great matter. (Hear, hear.) None of them had any cause to regret their decision in electing Mr. Brookes to the chair. If Mr. Brooks were re-elected by the new Board he would have to preside over a differently constituted Board, because it would be graced by the presence of three lady Guardian. (Hear, hear.) Mr. WILLIAM HOUGHTON seconded the vote of thanks and observed that the vote had been well and worthily earned.</p>
        <p>Mr. BILLINGE, in supporting, said Riston had always sent the very best of its men to the Board. On his (Mr. Billinge's) first visit to the Board Room he came in contact with the late Rev.G. F. R.Roberts,who at that time represented the district Mr. Brooks so ably repre- sented at the present time. The late Mr. Roberts per- formed much useful work on the Board, and influenced him (Mr. Billinge) in his early days of poor law work. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said the Board owed a debt of gratitude to the present Chairman. They had had chair- men of different types, and if he were disposed to be sarcastic it was quite possible that he might shoot a few arrows that would hit himself. (Hear, hear.) Of the different types of chairmen they had had, there were some who had been mqrely ornamental and others useful, but he ventured to say that Mr. Brooks had been both. (Hear, hear and laughter.) He had made the Board feel that they had had the ruling of a gentle- man who was not only impartial but a gentleman who hada good grip of the details of the work and able to guide them on trying occasions. (Hear, hear.) Mr. WILLIAM Hrcg.m.4..x observed that the Chairman had strapped down those who wanted strapping down and put the " shackles " upon them. Personally he held that no man should occupy the position of Chairman who did not do his duty on the committees. Mr. Brooks had made great sacrifices in order to do the committee work as well as that appertaining to the Board. With reference to the new Board, he felt that "Women may come and men may go but the Board goes on for ever." (Hear, hear and laughter.) Mr. GEORGIC MATHER supported the vote and it was carried unanimously. The CHAIRMAN, in responding, said great stress seemed to have been laid on his impartiality). in the chair. What was he there for if it was not to be impartial? _</p>
        <p>Air. A. J. JOLLEY: We don't always get it. The CHA.IRMAN said so far as he was concerned he had endeavoured to do his duty. He wished to extend his thanks to the members of the Board for the kind way they had supported him. The Board then rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>SLAVEt REVENGE.</head>
        <p>THE SLAVEt REVENGE.</p>
        <p>"The Stomach," says Souvestre, "is a slave which is obliged to accept everything imposed upon it ; but finally avenges its wrongs with the slyness and cruelty of a slave."</p>
        <p>. The eminent French author has happily expressed an important physical fact. When one comes to reflect upon it, there is something unique and pitiable about the position of the stomach in the human body. Upon no other organ except the lungs can you impose any- thing from the outside world. And it is your interest as well as your desire to draw into your lungs pure, fresh air, only. And air in some form is the only thing the lungs ever receive. On the other hand, the stomach is a general receptacle, entirely at the mercy of its owner as to what shall be put into it, when, where, and how much. A man can throw into it anythinn, than can be swallowed—every variety of food, any sort of drink, any amount of both, up to the bag's elastic capacity: and if he wants to, he can put a layer of marbles on top,and finish off with Prussic acid.</p>
        <p>The point is, as M. Sou'vestre says, that this most important of all our organs is our slave. It cannot re- sis,t, it cannot call the police, it has no recourse to the law ' • it just takes what comes, and abides its time. But how to take care of the Health, how to prevent the outbreak of ailments, is a problem much more complex than it seems to be. Therefore, when the enslaved and outraged stomich turn.; and lashes its owner, with the cat-o'-nine-tails called dyspepsia,, it must not be assumed, offhand, that the owner intenled to drive his servant to desperation.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas-Anderton, of 5, Mill-lane, Newcastle-on- Tyne, described in writing on June 3rd, 1397, how his stomach punished hin and what he did to cure his ills. He said: "For many years I sufferBd with indigestion and weakness. My appetite was bad, and after eating I had a pain at the chest, and bowels. I never felt well rested, was heavy and weary, got very weak, and at times had to leave my work. I tried several medi- cines and spent pounds in physic, bat got little benefit. A friend told me of the benefit he derived from taking Mother Seigel's Syrup. I got a bottle of this medicine and after taking it could eat well, and the food agreed with me. I soon found that the medicine was doing me more good than anything I had taken. By taking a dose now and again I keep in good health. It will be seven years next June since Mr. Anderton made that statement, and he has not been ill since—his stomach lashes him no more. Read for yourself what he says to us inn letter, dated at his old home, on February 15th, this year :—" Gentlemen, it may interest you to know that since sending you my testimony in. 1897 to the value of Mother Seigel's Syrup, I have con- tinued to enjoy good health. I still take the medicine as I need it, and would not be without it in the house. When I think of how I suffered lam sorry I did not know of it sooner. I never had a proper night's rest and I became so weak ancllanguid that I could not work. Nothing did me any good but Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup. That cured me,and I shall always be grateful. It is better never to be sick, bat when we do fall ill it is a grand fact that a cure is at hand in the form of Mother Seigel's Syrup, which soothes the stomach and transforms it once more into a willing and faithful servant—not a rebellious slave.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>THE NEW VICAR OF .ET. HELENS. The Rev. C. C. B. Bardsley, who has been appointed vicar of St. Helens in succession to Canon Willink, has resigned the living of St. Ann's, Nottingham, where be has laboured for three years. Mr. Bardsley is a member of a well-known clerical family, being the son of Canon Bardsley, vicar of Huddersfield, and the nephew of Dr. Bardsley, Bishop of Carlisle (formerly a Liverpool vicar). A Nottingham journal says of Mr. Bardsley : His work in Nottingham has been marked by much activity, and in a parish of such an extensive area he has found ample scope for his exceptional powers of organisation. During his comparatively brief residence in Nottingham, Mr. Bardsley has made himself exceed- ingly popular amongst his parishioners, and his work among the poorer classes of the city in particular has been of an arduous and self-denying nature. The living at St. Helens is worth about £7OO per annum, and carries with it the right of presentation to four other churches. The patronage of St. Ann's! Nottingham, is vested in six trustees.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>SAVAGE ASSAULT AT RAINHILL.</head>
        <p>SAVAGE ASSAULT AT RAINHILL.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday morning, at a special sitting of the Prescot Sessions, Henry Taylor, of Thatto Heath, was charged with unlawfully assaulting John Cross, a bar- man, residing with his father at the Quiet Woman Inn Thatto Heath, St. Helens. Mr. H. L. Riley (St. Helens) represented the prosecutor, and Mr. Molesworth (Prescot) defended.—Mr. Riley said Cross had been to Liverpool to see a friend off to Scotland, and returned by train to Rainhill Station at ten minutes to twelve at night. Defendant must have been on the same train and when they left the station complainant went out first, but defendant followed very hard on his heels, and committed a very serious assault on Cross. He got hold of him by the shoulder, threw him down on the pavement, and punched him on the face. He also referred to a bet he had made with another man, and Cross told him he knew nothing about it. Taylor then took a running kick at Cross badly breaking his nose.— The Bench said they felt satisfied that a savage assault had been committed, and fined Taylor £3 and costs and doctor's fee or in default one month's hard labour.</p>
