      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDIT3III</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDIT3III</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>Authors of letters intended for publication must mall eases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith. Matter intended for publicgtion must be written on one side only of the paper and sent in, if possible,not later than Thursday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>Cheap Credit it. Usury—A National Scheme.</head>
        <p>Cheap Credit it. Usury—A National Scheme.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The publicity so kindly accorded to me by the Press in connection with my proposed National Credit Institution for the suppression of 60 per cent. money lending and its attendant evils, has resulted in such an overwhelming expression of opinion in favour of the project from all classes of the community that 1 have, in conjunction with the best known man of the day, registered a People's Bank under the Industrial and Provident Societies Act,and received the necessary cer- tificate from the Chief Registrar of Friendly Societies. We have been empowered to open a Central establish- ment in London and to found branches throughout the Kingdom. The share capital requisite for launching the scheme on an adequate basis has been so arranged that even working man can become a shareholder by the invest- ment of .£l, payable by instalments ;and so as to prevent the Bank from falling into the hands of large capitalists the holding of any individual shareholder cannot exceed £2OO. 5 per cent. dividend is guaranteed on shares and 31 per cent. on deposits, thereby directly encouraging thrift and constituting a practical example of "Five per cent. Philanthropy." The institution will be managed by men specially qualified for the purpose, and no ornamental person or "guinea pie" will ever find a place on the Board of Control. For myself, lam devoting my whole life to this new constructive movement, but I am most anxious to impress upon the public the fact that the success of the project largely, if not entirely, depends upon their prompt and practical co-operation.—Yours faithfully, THOMAS FARROW, (Author of "The Moneylender Unmasked," ec.c.) 57, Gracechurch-street, London. E.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Chinese Indentured Labour in the Transvaal.</head>
        <p>Chinese Indentured Labour in the Transvaal.</p>
        <p>Sir,—lt is being freely stated, and often repeated, that the opposition to Chinese labour under the pro- posed conditions is hollow and insincere, and arises solely from the desire of politicians to make party capital out of the situation. Those who state this sre sadly ignorant of the issues involved, and the principles which the ordinance calls in question. This is part of the great struggle for freedom which has been going on in Europe for the last five hundred years. In England, the ground has been won inch by inch, foot by foot,and not by any tour de force, as in the case of the - French Revolution. The general advance is one that is gather- ing power, and at the present time is backed by a depth of conviction, a degree of resolution and a sense of courage which will make it irresistible. Prominent features in the march of progress.are the abolition of slavery,the passing of the Factory Acts,the broadening, of the Electorate, (not on the basis of property qualifica- tion, but on the rights of manhood), the abolition of the truck system, and the right of combination. Now every one of these positions has been won by great effort, and at great cost, and must be held by that eternal vigilance wbich is the price of liberty. They are directly challenged by the system which it is pro- posed to perpetuate, and aggravate, by the introduction of indentured Asiatic labour into the Transvaal. The abolition of slavery included the right of complete freedom, which the compound system denies. It includes the right of a freeman to possess property, which uncle; the ordinance is impossible. The right of combinktion, althougk possessed to the full by the mineowners is denied to the workers. They are not electors, nor can they ever be so. There is no hope of their ever becoming citizens,or living members of the community, as every right to this is denied. They cannot combine to resist any reduction in their wage. although their employers are free to use this power to the utmost. To talk of resistance to these proposals, (which endanger all that has been won by the class which carries society on its shoulders), as a mere question of political party, is to exhibit a total ignorance of the forces of social evolutiou, which are and have been at work in western civilization for F.everal centuries. The challenge which this ordinance, indeed the whole system of indentured labour, hasthrowndown to British democracy will be, and is being taken up. if the demand is persisted in, if the Ordinance is allowed to stand, any political party which approves it will be, as it ought to be, swept away, by a rising tide of popular indignation. Old political landmarks may be submerged, new combinations may arise, but of this, we may be assured,that the progress of the race towards individual freedom, and equal opportunity for all, will not be allowed to be set back by any party, and any Government which by its action impedes national pro- gress will be swept aside like straw before a torrent. CHARLES E. PARKER.</p>
        <p>Penketh, April 7th, 1904</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>Stockton Heath Election and Education. _</head>
        <p>The Stockton Heath Election and Education. _</p>
        <p>Sir—The result of the recent elections at Stockton Heath evidenced in my opinion several very gratifying features. In the first place there were unmistakable signs that religious bigotry and political prejudices were not so much to the front in this election and it appeared as if these curses of political life were on the wane in the district. Of course, it interested some and pleased others to noticethat the men who came out on the top of the poll were Churchmen,and that those who were at the bottom were Nonconformists, but it will, I think,be generally acknowledged that this class of individual is in a state of mind that is not at all to be envied. What was,how- ever, gratifying to notice was that there were only 82 votes between the first 14 candidates, and as over 400 voters went to the poll it was therefore distinct evi- dence that the majority of the voters were too broad- minded, and had sufficient commonsense, not to be unduly swayed by.the narrow creeds of men. In fact, the evidence of the election went further and showed unmistakably that if many of the voters had not been met on their way to the poll and taken into a certain Committee Room and instructed how to vote,and others fetched to the poll, that several who were elected would not have been ele3ted,and that several of those whowere not elected would have been elected in their places. May I say too that the absurd ridtculous reports that were circulated in the district that if myself, Mr.' Sherburn and Mr. Miller were elected, that it was our intention to " rob the Church of their school " natur- ally affected some ignorant voters and also exaggerated statements as to increased rates consequent upon a new school building,alarmed others and it was therefore not at all surprising that many voters did not even take the trouble to go to the poll at all. It will not be out of place for me at this juncture to throw a little further light on the subject of education in Stockton Heath. It will no doubt be news to many of the ratepayers to be informed that there is now being more money lost annually by the lack of attendance of children at the school, than it would cost to meet the annual charge for a new school building. I have this on the authority of a member of the Cheshire Educa- tional Committee. In conclusion may I gay that to my mind the moral and lesson of the election is, that a great proportion of the residents of Stockton Heath are clearly convinced that a new school is urgently needed and that the inten- tion of using the Church Room and consequent fitting it up will lead to some Waste of the ratepayers' money and thus be carrying on a scheme of false economy.— Yours truly,</p>
        <p>E. ENGLAND.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>CRANE DRIVER'S SHOCKING DEATH Singular Accident at Dallam Forge.</head>
        <p>A CRANE DRIVER'S SHOCKING DEATH Singular Accident at Dallam Forge.</p>
        <p>George Woodward (37), a crane driver residing at 14, Wilson street, met with a shocking death whilst fol- lowing his employment at Messrs. Pearson and Knowles forge at Dallam on Tuesday afternoon. The circum- stances were detailed at the inquest which was conducted at the Police Station yesterday afternoon by Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner. Mr. A. Lewis,H. M. Inspec- tor of Factories was in attendance ; M. T. S. Steel represented:the relatives; and Mr.F. G. Taylor appeared for the firm. The coroner read the police report of the accident. By means of a stationary steam crane, it appeared, de- ceased was transferring a pair of railway waggon wheels, from the ground to a small bogie. The crane post broke off near the fOundation socket,causing the crane to fall over. Deceased jumped off. but fell under the crane boiler and_was crushed. When extricated he was dead. Evidence bearing out this statement was given by John Egerton, who had charge of the loading and un- loading of the crane. Thomas Gilmour, foreman of the wheel shop, said the crane had been worked in its present position for four- teen or fifteen years. It was kept in good repair and frequently examined. Twelve months ago it was thoroughly overhauled. Witness could not account for the accident happening. A subsequent examination revealed the fact that the post had broken at the root The jury returned a verdict of "Accidental Death." Mr. Atherton, general manager for Messrs. Pearson and Knowles. statei that under the works scheme of insurance deceased's widow would receive I:183 10s. Bd. The firm thoroughly sympathised with the family and would do all they coald for the two children.</p>
        <p>THE GAME OF BLUFF. Mr. H. Whitham and the Labour Problem in America. Thanks to friend Joseph's temper, and the masterly work of Campbell-Bannerman, the British people are now in possession of how it came about that a handful of Boer farmers could and did make such havoc with the best laid schemes of Britain's Colonial Secretary. The question now arises,—ls the Right Hon. gentle- man now playing an Imperial game of Bluff? Pos- sibly he was in a deplorable state of ignorance touching the preparedness for war and the courage of the Boers, and thought a bluff would go, but is it reasonable to suppose for a moment that he knows nothing of the question he is now championing? Is he bluffing the Britishers in the hope that they will lose sight of his South African programme, and its dismal. failure, or is he working a bluff on the working classes in the hope that they will fail to see his yet deeper scheme to oust the white man from South Africa and replace him with the yellow man from China? Whatever name the various shades of Chamberlainites give their pet bantling,it must have but one when in the thoughts of the workingman, and that name is " Protection." Before I proceed to speak of lc as it is in America I must quote from a speech or report of an interview which appears in the "Daily Chronicle." Mr. Arthur Cham- berlain is a business man,so perhaps he knows nothing of politics. He leaves that side of public life to his brother Joseph. Mr. Arthur Chamberlain says— " The local industries of which I am a director are not depressed. Of course there are ups and downs in all businesses, and one would naturally expect a depression f ter a great and expensive war, We can't `have our ake and eat it. W 3 9 in't spend £3OO, 000,000 in war and find ourselves as flush of capital as if we had spent that amount on productive industries. But, looking at trade as a whole, and considering it in averages of five years —and this is the only proper way of looking at business —trade has increased and is still increasing. I am perfectly certain if Protection were introduced it would make the products of industry dearer. If the products of industry were dearer, consumption would be less. If consumption were less, employment would be less; and if employment were less, wages would go down. To sum it up, Protection would add to the cost of living for the working classes; and whilst the whole country would suffer, the working classes would suffer more than any other class, because their earnings are already at the limit of bare necessity. I have been in business for forty-five years, and am responsible now, in conjunction with my colleagues, in finding employment for, and paying wages to, upwards of 10,000 people. I have reduced the hours of work for all my workpeople. When I began work it was ten and even twelve hours per day, and now I have brought it down in nearly every case to about eight hours. At the same time I have seen the wages increase t* consider- ably,more for eight hours than used to be paid for</p>
        <p>twelve." The whole matter is in the above few worth, and let workingmen note, they have the ring of good honest truth, and should have a sledge hammer force in putting Mr. Crosfield at the head of the poll when the voting</p>
        <p>day comes round. I Germany, Russia, and China may be cited as giving good object lessons to the workingman as to the evils arising from Protection in its various forms, but let us stay with those of our own tongue. I think Mr. Chamberlain is playing his greatest Bluff with the Britishers in withholding from them things he must know touching the ways of Protection in America. Business in the States has during the past three years been very very good. Large fortunes have been made, and the workmen have in most cases received big</p>
        <p>figures as wages. I may give two samples of this. We have two strikes on just now in California. The plumbers have been re- ceiving 16s. a day,and they have struck for 18s. The hod- carriers have been receiving 14s. a day and they have struck for 16s. These unions are very strong, and al- though the employers say 'No 'the men will very likely get what they want. They work eight hours a day. I know of no unorganized class of workers that re- ceives more than Bs. a day of ten hours and yet all have to pay Protection prices, One of my family has found itconvenient to take a room in a flat. She pays 14s. a week for it. I find it utterly impossible to speak generally of the industrial situation in America. It is one of great con- tradictions. Some men with powerful organizations behind them are doing well, others differently situated are steeped in misery. Throughout the mining districts (gold,silver,copper,coal,&amp;c.) the company stores exist,— that is the" tommy shop,"—and men must trade with these stores or quit. Throughout the copper districts the conditions are simply slavery. Take the following instance which I could duplicate a score of times. MINER CHAINED TO A POLE WHEN HE purrs WORK. [SPECIAL DISPATCH TO "THE EXAMINER."' DENVER, March 2.—According to a telegram received here to-day from Telluride by Secretary W. D. Hay- wood of the Western Federation of Miners, the striking miners of the San Juan country are being subjected to the most vile and harrowing abuses by the civil authori- ties, aided by the militia. The telegram of to-day follows : "See Governor Peabody. One of our men shackled to a telephone pole. All arrests by civil authorities." Communication with Telluride confirmed the state- ment, but the military censor, who stood beside the correspondent as. he talked through the telephone, would not permit him to divulge any details of the incident nor allow the name of the persecuted miner to be given out. It is believed by officials of the Western Federation that the act is part of the mineowners' programme to break the strike, and that it is but the forerunner of the tortures mapped out by them and the Sheriff to be perpetrated on the down-trodden strikers. The year 1903 was, in my opinion, one of the most remarkable the States has ever seen for labour troubles and yet I fancy it was but a shade of what 1901 is , likely :to be. .An Industrial Revolution is on. Trade ' Unions are growing into mighty powers, and they are ' determined to prove that labour is not the only thing man is born into the world for and lives for. The em- ployer has become immensely wealthy and wants to become more so. His women folk flaunt this wealth in the eyes of the public, and the yellow journals make the most of it. It is all a gamble. • The workingman is determined to work no more than eight hours a day, and through his Union, he is doing a vast amount of lobbying at Washington. I quote the following as illustrating my subject, merely stating that the Mr. Hearst mentioned is a very rich San Franoiscoan,owning the most widely read papers of New York, Chicago, Los Angelos, and an Francisco, and is ikely to get the Democratic nomination for President. NVASHINGTON, February 25th.—Hearings before the House Committee on Labour onithe eight-hour bill are bringing out facts instructive to the country at large. To-day during a five-hour session of the com- mittee Representatives W. R. Hearst of New York and William Hughes of New York by submission of facts and interrogatories emphasized the selfishness of certain capitalists and their indifference to the welfare of the working people. Representative Hearst, analyzing the statements by Wallace Downey, ft New York shipbuilder,inarunning colloquy demonstrated that the expressed sentiment of many employers in Amnica is to bring about a wage rate and conditions of labour to the bottom level of the workmen of Europe. Mr. Downey addressed the committee in opposition to the eight-hour bill and in the course of his argu- ment predicted that disaster to all American industries is sure to come unless the manufacturers succeed in obtaining and retaining an outlet for surplus pro- duct in Europe and Asia. To acquire these desirable markets Mr. Downey argued that American employers must be at no disadvantage with the manufacturers of England, Germany, Bel- gium, and Holland, and American •workingmen must meet the competition of foreign artisans on equality of wage rates and hours of toil. This nod the full limit of human an mechanicalpower of production, Mr. Downey contends, alone will save the manufactories and shipbuilding industries of the country. "Our workingmen must contribute to the success of their employers," said Mr. Downey, " and give full measure of product. This cannot be done by limiting the length of the work day to eight hours, or by fix- ing an arbitrary limit on the number of hours the individual may toil. Development has been tremend- ous, but there are sure signs of a check to our prosperity. Legislation that will in any manner tend to diminish the total product of any industry will hasten the disaster. We have a foreign trade which amounts annually to 800,000,000d015. ; we must hold that and get more. Eighty per, cent of that sum is paid to the wage-earners of America. Another 20 per cent increase in the cost of production which will result from the application of the eight-hour law will kill our export trade." WANTS LOWER WAGE FOR WORKMEN. "Do you not think the protective tariff tends to protect you in our foreign trade,when it is a fact that American goods are sold for less in Europe than in America ?" asked Representative Hughes. "Yes, as to the general trade," llr.Downey replied, "but in the shipbuilding industry it makes no difference." • "Then you want the American workingmen to com- pete with the European on an equality of wage?" in- terjected Representative Hearst. If we are to hold the foreign market labour must so compete," was Mr. Downey's rejoiner. "In my business the facts are : England is to-day building ships cheaper than ever before in her history. It cost me 90,000d01s to ascertain that American mechanics, with tools I imported from Scotland and with the same materials used in British shipyards, do not work so well. The Scotch mechanic does 60 per cent. more work than the American"— "Then in the last analysis you want the American workingman to compete with the foreigner and, according to your statements, he will have to work 60 per cent. more time or accept 60 per cent less wages in order to do it ?" said Mr. Hearst. "It is up to him to decide whether he wants to compete and draw down 610,000,000d015. in wages annually."</p>
        <p>Mr. Downey then continued, arguing that English, French and Japanese ship-builders are absorbing all the ship building business; that Americans cannot compete because of differences in industrial conditions wages and other circumstances. During this argu- ment Representative Hearst put this question: "Von contend that wages and hours of work of Americans must be regulated by the industrial condi- tions and we rates in Europe?" 'That is :fact?' "What percentage of your work is for foreign ship- owners?" "About 25 per cent, including repairs. We cannot compete with the foreign yards and the protective tariff is no benefit to us. We are disadvantaged by 20 per cent difference in wages." Would you decrease wages and increase the num- ber of hours of work ?" "We might be able to compete under present con- ditions, but a reduction of hours would add 20 per cent more on the wage cost of production." "You say that 25 per cent. of your work is foreign trade," suggested Mr. Hearst. "Do you believe that this has got to regulate the hours and wages affecting the remaining 75 per cent ?" "Not absolutely,"Mr. Downey answered. "You state that England is building ships cheaper than ever and cheaper than the rest of the world," resumed Mr. Hearst. "Is it not a fact that wages have been increased and hours reduced in Great Britain ?" "That is true, qualifiedly. English meclaanics in shipyards work piecework and they turn out more in a short day than they did under the day wage rate." Resuming comparisons in the ship building industry Mr. Downey declared that American shipyards are heavily handicapped and that the enactment of the eight-hour law will paralyze them all. If I could conscientiously believe that that bill was beneficial to capital or labour." Mr. Downey said "I would work for it heroically and conscien- tiously. I say that it is absolutely a mistake at this time to attempt any such measure. Even with my hard experience, I have absolutely no hard feelings towards the workman to-day that contributed to it." ABOUT OTHI.III BUSINESS. "You think our workers should be ready to do the same as the English, French, German or Belgians ?" pressed Mr. Hearst. "They have got to do it." Mr. Hearst : It may not be his fault, the hard ex- perience. Now, the shipbuilding industry cannot be peculiar. I know something about some other busi- nesses. The newspaper has gone down in price from 3 cents., to 1 'cent., and during the period of that decline in price there has been a large increase in the amount of wages paid and a decrease in the hours of labour, while the profits made on newspapers are larger than ever before. M. Downey: lam delighted to hear it. Mr. HEARST : Consequently, I have little respect for the business ability of an employer who has to filch his profits out of the wages of his employees. Mr. Downey: Now, what time was the newspaper worth 3 cents and when did the price become 1 cent? Mr. Downey: Ido not know a thing about news- paper producing. I want to say that if the newspapers are worth to-day 1 cent as against 3 cents fifteenyears ago, which I am delighted to hear, I believe that,. to be the result or improved machinery, of increased demand for newspapers, of brain that realizes the demand and stays up night and day to supply it. Mr. Hearst: Is there any reason why those brains should not be put into operation for the benefit of the shipbuilding trust? Mr. Downey? Do not speak of the shipyard trust. I was never connected with it. Mr. Hearst: Do you know what it is? Mr. Downey: There are' several shipyards, that never were connected with the shipyard trust at all that are in the hands of receivers. It is pretty hard to keep them out. Now, the demand originated for newspapers. I want people to want ships as they want newspapers. We can never accomplish the re- duction from 3 to 1 in the price of building ships. Mr. Hearst: But it is impossible to reduce the price of a product to one-third what it was and to give shorter hours for labour and increase wages and still make a profit in some businesses. Mr. O'Connell: The hours of labour have been re- duced in the manufacture of paper. Mr. Downey: The answer to Mr. Hearst's question covers that subject. His question is how are these commodities becoming cheaper in the face of shorter hours and higher wages? Mr. Hearst : Yousay that it is impossible for that to happen? Mr. Downey: No, sir, it is not impossible. Mr. Hearst: Then why is it not possible in your business? Mr. Downey: The whole secret of the manufacture of paper is the tremendous demand for it and the tremendous quantities of it that you use and the qnantities of papers that you print together. If you are printing 800,000 papers a day, logically, you can print those papers for oae-tenth of what you could if you only printed 10,000 or 15,000 a day. Mr. Hearst: Let us take another example. The cost of the manufacture of white paper used by the newspapers has decreased from 5 cents. to 21 cents. per pound in the last fifteen years. Mr. Downey: Yes. Sir. Hearst: And ther3 has been a decrease in the hours of labour and an iacrease in the pay of the labourers in that manufacture, and nevertheless the cost of the product has been halved. Mr. Downey: Yes, sir. Nowtake the blank white paper. I must apologize for any ignorant statement that I make on that. Mr. Hearst: The point is simply this: It is ob- viously possible to conduct business in which the hours of labour are reduced and the wages are in- creased and the prices of the product reduced, and still make a profit. Mr. Downey: I will admit frankly, and lam glad to admit to you, that it is possible to conduct a busi- ness in this country— Mr. Hearst: And evidently more than one. INI r. Downey (continuing)-1 say a business, and perhaps one or two, or three, or several, that the bill would not materially injure. But what I want to say most emphatically is that I believe most conscien- tiously, from a most unprejudiced consideration of the matter, that the business of the country broadcast one hour more or less of performance by the armies of mechanics of this country will make a gigantic difference in the business of the world that we want and must compete for in the future. Mr. Hughes : It is impossible, practically, under the statement of facts submitted by you for us to get any part of that world's business. Mr. Downey: Oh, no, do not deceive yourselves. Out of a business that has been, through protection. or through some other reason, develoned in this country to such a fine point that we can sell to a foreigner in competition with a foreigner, a profit might be made. What we want is some aid for a period of time so that the shipbuilding business can b 3 developed like the newspaper business, so that we will catch ap to the place where we can compete. Mr. Hearst: The manufacture of white paper is a much better illustration than the newapapsr business, because the white paper manufacturers of America not Only sell their product here but in London—in fact, to nearly every newspaper in London. Mr. Downey: Yes, sir; and I am delighted that they do. They have no trouble in meeting the com- petition. Mr. O'Connell: And they operate eight hours. Mr. Hearst: Yes. Mr. Downey: Protection has been a marvellous ' good thing to this country. It has resulted in just what you say, that we can print newspapers and make the paper and do a great many things and sell to the foreigners in man} lines in 'direct competition with him. Mr. Hearst : The printing of a newspaper does not have any advantage from protection. Mr. Downey: No, but the making of the paper does. Now, I know you gentlemen are interested in tariff questions as well as in labour questions. In fact the involve the wnole labour situation of our country. On that question the fact is that a man might give away the surplus. There have been cases where a surplus has been actually burned at the tail of the mill. Mr. Hughes: That is a crime is it not? "The discussion then digressed to the effects of the protective tariffs and the desirability of subsidies to encourage shipbuilding in America. Mr. Downey asserted that American-manufactured products greatly exceeded the home demand, and that foreign con- sumers were sought. He thought it an economic and industrial benefit to America that onr manufacturers are able, by reason of the tariff, to sell goods cheaper abroad than at home. It is a logical result, he argued, and this advantaged the employees so they could keep their mills running. Concluding his argument, Mr. Downey insisted that a universal eight-hour law in America will destroy industry. Other representatives of employers' interests opposed the eight-hour law. They were W. B. Cowles, manu- facturer of ships fittings at Cleveland, 0.; H. N. Covell, manager of the Lidgerwood Manufacturing Company, Brooklyn; Captain C. H. Smith, Aurora, 111., andlt-I. C. Stayer of Cllicago, representing the Illinois Manufacturers' Association; Edward Freegard, Secretary of the United Typotheate of America, and Charles Waltz, who read a resolution adopted at the recent meeting of the National Industrial Association and Citizens' Alliance at Indianapolis. All these representatives of employers' interests protested against the enactment of an eight-hour law and contended that it is Socialist legislation and inimical to all business investments. Representative Gilbert of Kentucky, the metriber of the committee inquired of Charles Waltz, the repre- sentative of the Citizens' Alliance, if that organization is not, in fact. Socialistic in its purpose and effect. Waltz denied this,and said the organization was formed for the purpose of enforcing the law. I am afraid you will find this communication much too long. I could not cut it up. It contains the whole argument of the labour problem as we in the States are facing it in 1904. Its bearing upon the case for Protection and against it can be seen at a glance. Find room for it if you can. H. WHITHAM.</p>
        <p>San Francisco.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>SOCIAL SERMONETTES FOR WORKING MEN,</head>
        <p>SOCIAL SERMONETTES FOR WORKING MEN,</p>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.]</p>
        <p>BY JOHN TAYLOR. Anthor of Search Lights on Lantl, Labour,</p>
        <p>and Social Problems."</p>
        <p>impoverished in both countries. Bacon tells us that this quarrel "pinched the merchants of both countries very sore, and they besought their Kings to open the nterercourse again." When the English merchants reamed to Antwerp "they were received with pro- 'cession and great joy." If the Tories have got their knife into the aliens, why do they not tackle Pierpoint Morgan, for cornering our ships, and Sully, for cornering our cotton, and Levy Lawson,Steinkopf, and Waldorf .Astor,for cornering our newspapers? And while they are about it they might banish Wernher, Belt, Barnato, Albu, Goldsmith, and the whole gang of aliens, who have captured our mines in South Africa, and are dictating their own terms to Lord Milner and the Government. The British work- ing man has more to fear from the tyranny of any half dozen of the names I have mentioned, than from all the starving, stranded, helpless aliens in the world. Let the Tories tackle the big sinners first, and free us from their oppression, and we will deal with the small sinners afterwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>ALIENS BILL.</head>
        <p>THE ALIENS BILL.</p>
        <p>"It, is an extraordinary fact that the House should be called upon to consider a measure, which, for the first time is going to prevent white men from coming at their own cost, and as free men, to a free country, at a moment when the same Government is engaged in making. regulations for the importation into one of our colonies of yellow men under servile conditions. —Sir Charles Dilke, Just before the House of Commons adjourned for the Easter holidays, Mr. Akers Douglas introduced a bill "to make provision with respect to the immigration of aliens, and!other matters incidental thereto." The Bill only applies to this country, so it will not prevent the dumping of Chinamen into South Africa and you may judge of its aims, and character, from the fact that it was received with rapturous applause by all the Tory Protectionists in the House of Commons. I.—THE OUTCRY AGAINST ALIENS IS A PROTECTIONIST DODGE. It is a political net, to capture labour votes, for the Tory party, and it is being worked just now for all that it is worth. It is easy enough to fool the average working man, by making him believe that vast hordes of destitute foreigners are being dumped down on our shores, every year, to undersell him in the labour market, and take the bread out of !his mouth, by pro- ducing "sweated "goods. He is told that these poor foreigners are being imported at so much per gross, and employed in unhealthy workshops, and lodged in filthy slums. Political newspapers publish these cock-and-bull stories every week, and professional agitators repeat them daily till the silly voter accepts them as gospel And when the election comes, the rallying cry will he "Britain for the Britons—and the Devil take the</p>
        <p>foreigner." Just think for a moment, what a splendid election cry this will be for the Tories, among thousands of boozing,ignorant mouchers,who do not know their right hand from their left, and who swallow Tory lies, as greedily as they swallow Tory beer. These drunken patriots will regard every foreigner as an enemy, and they may be tempted to riots, and blood-shed, if they come into personal contact with the unlucky aliens. Such things have happened before, and they may occur again, if you appeal to all that is basest and worst in</p>
        <p>man nitu re. It is easy enough to mislela ignorant, blind,unreason- ing men, by lying, and misrepresentation, and some of us rememberhow the War Fever was kindled,and fanned to a flame, to give the Tories a new lease of life at the last election. But the War Fever will not help them next time,so they have put their money ona horse called "Protection." That blind spavined jibber has gone wrong now, so they are looking out for a more likely winner. They are desperately hard up for a new election cry, that will rally the labour vote to their side, so they are backing the Alien Bill,for alwin, or a place. It is true they are acting the part of consummate hypocrites, in opening the mines of South Africa to Chinese slaves, and closing our own labour markets against white free men, who pay their own fares to this country, but trans- parent hypocrisy of this kind will not deceive the Trade Unionists, and the Labour Leaders of this country "Surely in vain is the net spread in the sight of any. bird."</p>
        <p>CASEIHAS BEEN MADE OUT FOR THIS ALIENS BILL.</p>
        <p>All the facts and figures that, have been published go to prove that the Bill is absolutely unnecessary, and it is only being passed as a political trap, to catch labour votes. Let us see how many aliens we have in this country,l and what they are doing, and how they are behaving themselves ? Sir Charles Duke estimates them at 250,000, all told. Mr. Akers Douglas, who had to make out a case against them. says there are 290,000 of them residing in the United Kingdom. But we have over forty million people in these islands, and if we take the Tory estimates, the aliens will only number about one, out of every one hundred and forty, of our people. Or to put it another way, if they were all herded together, they would hardly fill a single town like Salford, or Hull, or Nottingham. Then we are told that they are coming over here in swarms, and multiplying on our hands at an alarming rate. The way the Tories use figures in this case, is a fine example of the art of lying, and misrepresentation. One paper says that 702,555 people landed in this coun- try from the various European posts in 1901,but it does not say that 613,813 of them went back again to the Continent. And it does not say that 64,600 of them simply passed through this country on their way to Canada, and the United States. If you work a simple sum, you will find that only 24,000 of the new corners in that year were left in our midst. If these were all swept into one little town, they would hardly fill places like Colne or Bacup, or Loughborough or Welling- borough. What influence can a handful of people like this have on our vast population? If we cannot absorb one solitary alien, for every sixteen hundred of our own people, we must be in a bad way. A very large per- centage of these aliens are Russian Jews, who have been banished from the Czar's dominionsby religious persecu- tion. In that land of tyranny, no Jew may acquire land, or houses, or real property, and if he accumulates any money by his own industry, or comes into the pos- session of any visible wealth, the chances are that he will be fleeced, and robbed, and then kicked out. When these Jews have suffered the loss of all things for conscience sake, their friends in England often sub- scribe enough money to bring them here, and give them a new start in life. So they settle among the little colonies of Jews in Stepney, or Manchester, or Leeds. Twenty thousand of them are engaged in the clothing trade. Three thousand five hundred of them are cabinet- makers, and about three thousand of them are boot and shoe makers. Mr. Akers Douglas tells us that "a very large proportio of the aliens who come,are among the most loyal, most industrious, and most law-abiding citizens!' Now I have shown you on the evidence of the Tories themselves, that our aliens in England only number about one in every one hundred and forty of our people and that the majority of them are "loyal, industrious, law-abiding citizens." Then why do the Tories introduce an Alien Bill to prevent these men from landing on our shores, or to ship them back again to the country from whence they came? Because they believe that "hatred to the foreigner," and "Britain for the British," will be good trump cards to play for the next election. They do not tell us that we are sending out emigrants from our own shores,who will be aliens in some foreign land, a great deal faster than we are taking others into our midst. lII.—THE ALIENS HAVE BEEN MORE OF A</p>
        <p>BLESSING THAN A CURSE TO THIS COUNTRY. They have been coming to us for nearly a thousand ' years, and enriching us in ten thousand wa vs. In the thirteenth century we were a nation of shepherds and agricultural labourers. We did not know how to spin, or weave, or manufacture woollen cloth. We had to send our wool to the continint, because we could not manufacture it, and yet we wanted to be an industrial ' nation. Who helped us out of our difficulties and taught us to spin and weave? The despised aliens. Edward 111. invited hundreds of families from Flanders and they settled in York, and Norwich, and Lincoln. We learned to use theirspinning wheels, and looms. We picked their brains, and discovered their secrets, and then we wanted to banish them all back to Flanders. But the King Apuld not let us, so their descendants are with us to this day. In the sixteenth century we knew nothing about smelting, and forging iron, or casting, andl moulding metals, and yet we wanted to become engineers and miners, and coarrymen. Who taught us the arts and mysteries of these industries? The persecuted aliens. That inhuman wretch, the Duke of Alva, was extermin- ating the Protestants of the Low Countries, and carry- ing fire and sword through Holland and Flanders. Queen Elizabeth invited the persecuted workmen to flee to this country. London readily absorbed eight thousand of them. Norwich took four thousand, and the rest of them settled in the Midland Counties, and in Ireland. They taught us their trades and callings, and they brought ovpr with them carrot., and celery, and cab- bages, and many other vegetables we had never seen before, and planted them in our gardens. When we had got out of them all we could, and learned all that they could teach us, we wanted au Alien Bill, to drive then ; all home again,lbut they found a friend,and protector,in our Queen Bess. In the seventeenth century, we knew little about the manufacture of silk, or cotton. We could not make any decent pottery, or earthenware, or glass, and we knew precious little about bleaching, or dyeing. Who taught us all thnse mysteries ? The poor aliens. The massacre of St. Bartholomew, in the reign of Louis XIV. drove tens of thousands of the best artisans and skilled workmen in Europe,to this country. They came as destitute aliens, and settled in London and the Mid- land Counties. From these poor outcasts we got the silk manufactures of I Spitalfields and Cd(rentry, and some of the best bleaching works in the Kingdom. We enter- tained these helpless strangers, and we little knew that •see were entertaining angels unawares. We owe nearly • all our arts,and crafts,and technical knowledge, to the ideas our fathers picked up from the aliens, and if we have any gratitude for the past, or hope for the future, ;we shall think twice before we shut our door in any ! man's face. I We have tried banishment,and it did not pay. Henry i VII.. drova all the Flemish manufacturers, and traders out of this country in 1493. Philip of Spain said two could play at that game, so he packed all the English ' out of his dominions, by way of retaliation. , Shops were closed and trades were ruined, and families</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>EASTER VESTRY MEETINGS.! THE TANNING TRADE. Mr. C. E. Parker and the Past Year's Conditions.</head>
        <p>EASTER VESTRY MEETINGS.! THE TANNING TRADE. Mr. C. E. Parker and the Past Year's Conditions.</p>
        <p>[Continued from Page 6.]</p>
        <p>Mr. C. E. Parker. hon. secretary t ) the Manchester Liverpool and District Tanners' Federation, contributes- the following interesting notes on the trade of 1903 to the Federation year book. All our members will agree that the trade of 1903 was very like the bad trade of 1902, only worse. The continued scarcity of hides has made profitable tanning well nigh an impossibility. Our members have been sorely puzzled as to what course to pursue. The danger of being caught by a falling leather market, with a yard full of high-priced hides, induced many of the members to diminish their input and so lessen their stocks. It is a moot question which are to-day better off as a result of the different courses of action—those who continued putting in their usual quantity trusting to- fortune to bring things right or those who reduced their input. Hides have been scarce and dear right throughout the year. In the fall there was a substantial break in the high prices that had for some time ruled in America,. and English tanners were able to secure about seventy- thousand packer hides at moderate prices. Prices, how- ever, in the States soon recovered, and are now out of the reach of English tanners. The hides available from imported cattle were two hundred thousand in excess of last year, and these were eagerly absorbed. The' imports into Liverpool of salted hides are less from, almost every country than last year, and the year closes with practically no stock. The imports of tanning material to the United King- dom in value show a considerable reduction. Imports into Liverpool hold their own, but only show a slight increase, confirming the opinion that the tanning trade in this district did not make its usual advance during: the plan One of the firms in our federation has suffered loss by- fire—we refer to Messrs. J. H. Fleming and Company. Limited, whose yard was completely gutted. Their' fellow members extend their sympathy to Messrs. Fle— ming in the dislocation of their business which must have resulted from the destruction of their premises. Although hit by Messrs. Fleming, and other fires, the Leather Trades' Insurance Company has had a fairly- good year. Some of our members have,during the year adopted automatic sprinklers, and others the Pearson's Alarm System,as safeguards against fire. Labour troubles have been, happily, nil. Several firms have introduced the system of drying by fans and warm air. Experiments have been and are- being made, to effect the better extraction of the tann- ing from the various materials used. Tanners are realiz— ing that they have to save at this and every otherpoint. We have seen at Exeter, at the works of Mr. Edward Wilson, a most ingenious fleshing machine. To describe - same would be impossible without diagrams, but the machine was doing excellently, although not quite per- fect We trust the maker will be able to perfect the machine, and to secure a good sale for it. Mr. Wilson's ingenuity and resource as a maker of tanners' machinery` is well known, and we hope, both for his own sake and that of the trade, that he will be successful in this per- haps the most difficult of all his undertakings. Other machinists eontinue to do good work for the trade, and in regard to machinery generally, the tanner's require- ments are well looked after. The Fiscal Controversy has engaged the attention of many of our members as a sort of relief from the task. of looking for profits which did not exist. No doubt our members differ in our views of the general question and will agree to differ. There is, however, one aspect of the question in regard to which we must all stand shoulder to shoulder—l mean in resisting any claim for a tax on imported hides. Should the agriculturist's ' interest secure a tax on meat, it is a very short step- further, if further it be, to demand a tax on the skin that encloses the meat. This was done in the United States, and a tax of 15 per cent, found its way into the Dingley Tariff. To effectually oppose such a claim in England, the federation must have a well-filled war chest.</p>
        <p>TANNERS AND THE FISCAL QUESTION A number of letters on the fiscal question have been. addressed to Mr. Parker in response to a circular requesting an expression of opinion. Mr. Robert Mellor, the President of the Federation, closes a letter in support of Free Trade as follows All we ask for, is freedom (our forefathers fought to secure this priceless heritage)—liberty to work out our own salvation. If we are to defeat this wicked move- ment, which will only lurick those who are already' possessed of a large share of this world's goods at the. expense of the deserving poor, we must ever be on the alert, remembering that the price of liberty is eternal. vigilance." Mr. William Long, J.P. writes :—My dear sir,—You ask in your circular letter of the 4th for the expressions of opinion from your fellow members of the Tanners' Federation on Mr. Chamberlain's Protection policy so far as known, particularly as such changes might affect• our own industry, and as one who appreciates your la- bours as our hon. secretary, and your earnest desire for the welfare of the trade and your fellow members (em- ployers and employed alike), I will venture to give you shortly my personal feeling on the question. While granting that we have often smarted from the fierce, and as I have heard the American tanner admit,. the unfair competition we have had to contend with, I am convinced that, as Englishmen, we have been the gainers in the long run by our;freedom'of trade,and that it will be an evil day for England if she is ever led to throw away that freedom, and take again to those miserable shackles she threw off finally nearly two generations ago, to the benefit not only of her own - children, but, in a large and very real sense, to the ad- vantage of the whole world. The ramifications of such benefits are so great in various ways: shipping, banking, etc., etc., on the material side, and still larger and more far reaching on the world's intercourse, that such a bickward step as some contemplate. would. I believe, be an irremediable blunder.—Sincerely yours,</p>
        <p>WILLIAM LONG.</p>
        <p>Charles Parker, Esq. In a footnote to the correspondence, Mr. Parker regrets that " our efforts to find any who would cham- pion the Protectionist cause were unavailing."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>ST. LUKE'S CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ST. LUKE'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. R. WILD presided at the vestry meeting on Thursday evening.—The statement of accounts showed a credit balance of us. Messrs. Jackson and Oldfleld were elected wardens; Messrs. R. Graham, J. Peers, J. Peers jun„ Ellison, Warburton, Dendy. Arm- strong, Taylor, Burton, and Hewitt, sidesmen ; Messrs. Horrobiii and H. H. Ashcroft, auditors; Mr. W. J. Forshaw, vestry clerk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>CHRIST CHURCH, PADGATE.</head>
        <p>CHRIST CHURCH, PADGATE.</p>
        <p>The vestry meeting was held on Tuesday under the presidency of the Rev. W. Pollock-Hill—The statement of accounts showed that during the year the debt of between £l4 and £l5 had been wiped off—Mr. J. D. Buckton was appointed Vicar's warden and Mr. G. Mather people's warden—The Vicar nominated the fol- lowing sidesmen :—Messrs. Hoult, Hatton, London' Gorton, and Denham; and the meeting appointed Messrs. Critchley,Houghton, Atkinson,Boyd and Rigby Mr. G. Mather was appointed lay representative, and Messrs. Blackwood and Denham auditors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>ST. BARNABAS' CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ST. BARNABAS' CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The vestry meeting was held last evening under the presidency of the Vicar (Rev. J. H. G. Hollingworth). The elections were as follows --Vicar's warden, Mr. James Iddon ; people's warden, Mr. W. A. Proud ; sidesmen, Messrs. A. Allen, F. J. Dowling, E. Evans, E. Iddon, W. P. James, G. Rolfe, C. Skinner, J. J. Siddall, P. Sudlow, and J. Tomkins ; lay representatives, Messrs. John Looker and E. ddon.—The balance sheet showed that after paying all expenses the wardens had been able to hand over £25 to the curate's fund and £5 to a decoration fund.—The Vicar said it was thought the timehad come whenthe chancel should be exteneded and an organ introduced. An effort, would shortly he made to raise funds for that purpose, and £2 2s. had been set aside as a nucleus.—The usual votes of thanks concluded the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>EMMANUEL (REFORMED) CHURCH,</head>
        <p>EMMANUEL (REFORMED) CHURCH,</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The annual open vestry was held in the schoolroom I on Thursday evening, the Rev. F. Vaughan presiding over a good attendance of Church members. The wardens presented the accounts shewing a. balance in hand of £9 10s. lid, which were passed subject to a, final audit. A hearty vote of thanks to wardens retir- ing officers and all church workers was passed. Reports of work under different branches were presented and proved very interesting. The chairman appointed Mr. Thomas Allen as his warden and the follov:ing officers were elected for the ensuing year :—People's warden, Mr. W. H. Dickens; parish council, the churchwardens; svnodsmen, Messrs. F. Allen and W. Atherton; vestry; Miss Lees, Messrs. E. Minards, A. Dickens, J. Furnival, W. Heron, G. H. Heald, F. Lloyd,J. Lloyd, E.Ditchfield and W. Brown; auditor, Mr. H. Landon.—A vote of thanks to the chairman for presiding was passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>ST. PAUL'S CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The Vicar(Rev. W. Bracecamp) presided at the vestry meeting held on Thursday evening.—Mr. J. H. Smeth- urst (people's warden) presented the statement of accounts which showed an income of £514 125. sid., as against £506 last year. The year closed with balance in hand of ss. 6d. The restoration fund had been increased to £45 2s. 6d., and the endowment of the church had been raised by £l2 per annum. At All Saints' Mission the year commenced with a deficit of 10s. 9id., but there was now a credit balance of 9s. 11d. During the coming year they proposed to undertake the restoration of the stone work at the west end of the church at a cost of £57 10s. They had a balance in hand of £45 2s. 6d, and he did not think they would experience any difficulty in raising the remainder.—The balance sheet was adopted.—The vicar nominated Mr. J. H. Smethurst as his warden, Mr. ,R. Chorley having resigned in consequence of his leaving the district.—Mr. A. H. Burgess was unanimously elected people's warden.—The following sidesmen were elected :—Messrs. J. F. Lord, H. Houghton, J. W. Hayes, D. Bate, W. H. Thompson, P. Warburton, G. F. Freeman, J. Houghton, J. S. Bate, H. F. Greenough, J. Hankiuson, J. Lawton, J. W. Parker, E. Powell, J. Wainwright, E. Healey, J. Rigby, A. C. Binks. J. W. Robinson, R. Hosker, F. Bennett, H, Broadhurst, R. S. Finney, W. E. Collin, J. Wilson, T. Locker, W. Flood, J. Pennington, D. Lewis, J. Everett. and H. Collin. Messrs. W. Hall, W. Narroway, T. Addison, jun., T. Darbyshire, J. Narroway. and T. Spencer were appointed sidesmen for All Saints' Mission. Mr. A. J. Jolley and Mr. J. Richardson were eledted lay representatives; Mr. J. W. Parker, auditor; and Mr. W. D. Jeans, vestry clerk. Votes of thanks were accorded to the church officers, organist and choir, special reference being made to the services rendered by Mr. Chorley during the seven years he had acted as Vicar's warden.—The Vicar expressed his indebtedness to the congregation for the noble way in which they had rallied round him, and specially thanked them for their Easter offering of £3O 15s. 3d. Next year he would like to see the extension of the east end of the church taken in hand. Tho cost would be about £2,000 but he hoped that at their next vestry meeting they would see their way to undertake the work. The Vicar also cordi- ally acknowledged the services of Miss Westivell.—A vote of thanks to the CaairmAn concluded the meeting</p>
        <p>The Parr Hall Services. "A Young Man's View of Life." The Parr Hall was again crowded on Sunday evening when the Rev. Herbert Dunnico preached his monthly sermon to young men and women the subject being "A young man's view of life." Basing his remarks on Ezekiel 2 xi. " Son of man stand upon thy feet and I will speak to thee" thi, preacher said that those words contained a spiritual message to the men of to-day. When he looked out upon life as it presented itself to him he was impressed by many things and one thing which no thoughtful ob- server would fail to note was that the people of to- day failed to take either themselves or their destiny or their life seriously. Now life was a very serious thing ; this world was a place of strange and solemn mysteries and the man who took life flippantly or lightly not only wronged the God who had made him but wronged his own soul. What was called manhood to-day was often a base and sordid thing, for if God was absent from a life whatever else it might posi,ess it was barren and poor. Another thing that impressed him as he looked out upon life was, to use Dr. Watson's phrase, "the coarsening of national ideals". He (the preacher) loved his country as much as any one, and he would yield to no man in his loyalty to this great Empire.No weakness was more deplorable than the weakness which praised every other country but its own, still it would be suicidal to ignore those things which were threatening to demoralize and degrade us,and to put the stamp of decay upon our brow This little island of ours had played a great and an amazing part in the history of the world, for if ever there was'a country for which a man might work with pride, surely it was our own. There was much in our history that one could not do other than deplore, yet on the whole this country had exer- cised its great trust and its enormous power in a wise and liberal spirit. Step by step this great Empire had grown to its present position, and we should be degener- ate children if we did not seek to band down to our children the heritage which we had received, not only unimpaired but strengthened and improved. Were we seeking to do that? He feared that on the whole we were not, and that we were in danger of losing our position among the nations of the world. For many years we had been confident and rightly confident of our political and commercial supremacy' among the nations of the earth, but we were no longer so. A great change had come over the country during the past two years and if we were wise we would ascertain the cause. In his opinion the cause lay in ourselves, as the men of to-day lacked that moral and spiritual fibre which had been the I , possession of those men who had made England what she is and the inevitable result of a weakened vision was Tie pa i the loss of noble ideals and without these any nation sate Parochial Amateur Dramatic Society is making subatantial progress. Established only two was doomed. They heard a short time ago much years ago, the society has already embarked upon three talk about patriotism and imperialism and yet dramatic ventures, and the large measure of support he feared that those words had been degraded and they given by the public, is in itself a tribute of their had been robbed of everything noble and heroic. That appreciation of the work of the soziety. The perform - patriotism which revsaled itself in such forms as ances which were given in the national schoolroom on Mafficking was a spurious and false patriotism. The Thursday evening, and again yesterday, were in aid of true patriot was a man who loved his country but loved the Warrington Infirmary, and there is reason to hope righteousness more, a man who honoured his King that a sum of about £l5 will be handed over to the when he was worthy of honour but who honoured truth more. A true patriot was a man who blushed, to see a treasurer of this deserving : charity as a result of the ragged hungry child, or an hon