      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-09_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Sharp-Street Primitive Methodist Chapel. A company of about 40 assembled in this misSion the other evening on the occasion of a tea and entertain- ment. Mr. James Whitley, of Stockton Heath, presided Recitations were given by Mr. Lightfoot, Mrs. Guest, May Perry and Eva Jones, and songs by Mrs. Grady, Mrs. Thompson, Mrs. Carrick and Mr. Chadwick. The prizes (81) gained by the scholars for the last year's I attendances were distributed. A vote of thanks was accorded to the Chairman.</p>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyan Church. Special services were held at this church on Sunday the preacher in the morning being the Rev. R. Wilkins Rees and in the evening the Rev. John Gunnell. Excel- lent music was given by the choir assisted by Mrs. Booth and Mr. A. Griffiths. An organ recital was ably given in the afternoon by Mr. Frank Hickman, and was much enjoyed. Mr. E. Pownall contributed several sacred bass solos and the choir were also heard to advantage. Mr. Johne,Taylor presided and expressed the pleasure it afforded him to be present to support the organist and choir in their worthy effort to raise the necessary amount for the new tune books. He was very desirous of seeing installed a new organ in order that the music of the church could bo up to the mark. He considered the congregational part of the service of great importance. The collection amounted to £5.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society. Owing to the holidays there was not as large an attendance as usual at the weekly meeting of this society. Mr. John Smith, of St. Helens, presided. In the course of an address, Ir. Smith pointed out that the time of the members was wasted unless their lives were influenced for good by what they heard at the P.S.A. meetings. He advised them to be better men for the sake of those around them who were dying without Christ.—Th3 address was delivered by the Rev. Hirry Wall, who referred to their Eister joy in the Resurrection of Christ, which opened up the way to salvation. They must let him be notlonly their Saviour but their friend.—Mr. R. H. Pearson played two violin solos with great taste and precision. Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. Bac.' presided at the organ. Mr. B. Farrar announced that two new members had been enrolled, makingthe membership -160.—The service was also taken part in by Mr. C. Cotton and Mr. Healey.</p>
      </div>
