        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS FOOTBALL. SHE: "Don't you think there should be =Sit I in every home?" He: "By all means! What l fIitIOKHTHRS SUBSET'S Demon Driver Bats are the object to is music next door." NJ grandest made. BussET's New Mitch Bells vat sin their SHE: " What do you think of my portrait?' shape, and lest longer then any others. BuSsEe4 Le 4 Guards : "Sincerely, it is not beautiful—but the like are exceptionally good. BUesET's Batting Glove, are far nesS is perfect I" superior to the usual class. BUSSKY'S Begs are made to IT WAS FALSE .—He gaped upon the maiden fair, wear. EithlSEY'B Diary and Oompanion le a Gem for 6d.— p A ly for BUSSEY'B 04111.10 ue from Local Sports Outfitters ; and then he softly sighed. "Your hair is dyed.' " 36 and 38 Queen Victoria street, London. Factories Peck- he said to her, "'Tie false! 'tis false!" she cried. ham, London; and Elmswell, Suffolk. (4) Fools and tileir money are soon parted; but a good deal of the money finds its way into the hands of other fools. IT is poor rule that won't work both ways: !sat it is no uncommon thing to find a rule that svoret work either way. TOMMY: "Come, Bridget, play with us. We're playing soldier." Bridget: " G'wan, yea imp Oi ain't no soldier.' Tommy: "No, Briti• get.; but you're a red cross nurse." Dacron (to lady patient) : "You have a slight touch of fever; your tongue has a thick coat—" Patient (excitedly): "Oh, doctor, do tell me how it fits." A PARISIAN medical journal, ridiculing the microbe theory, says that "there is absolutely no danger in kissing." That paper ought to look at the breach of promise records. (NNE LITTLE GIRL IN THE SLUMS: " Wot yer say she died of?" The other one: " Eatin' ice- cream on the top of 'ot puddin." The first-men- tioned : " Lor' ! What a jolly death!" BOTANIST: "This is the tobacco plant." Fair Visitor: "How interesting! And when does it begin to bear cigarettes?" MRS. A.: "Is it true that yonr son holds the appointment of warder in a gaol ?" Mrs. B. : " Yes; but only criminals of good families are imprisoned there." " WHATEVER made you make Brackins a present of a pocket-comb? He's as bald as a billiard-ball." "That's just it. I want to make him think I never noticed it." "I HEAR that Miss Moody is inconsolable over the loss of poor little Fido.' " Yes, poor thing. Every time it rains cats and dogs she rushes to the window to see if he is among them." A FRENCH confectioner, proud of his English, and wishing to let his patrons know that their wants would be attended to at once without any delay, put out the notice, "Short weights here." THE CONVERSE No BEING ALWAYS TRUE.— " Wnen you are conversant with a subject, you can converse about it, can't you?" " Usually ; but many people can converse allout a subject without being conversant with it." ENGLISH AS SHE IS SPELLED. —A little school- mistress named Beauchamp said, "These dreadful boys! How shall I teauchamp ? For they will not behave, although I look grave, and with tears in my eye-. I beseauchamp." TANGLEFOOT : "Oh, isn't this waltz divine?" Miss Smilax "Well, perhaps it is divine; but it happens to be a polka instead of a waltz, and the sooner you are acquainted with the fact the better we are likely to get along." MASTER "Tombs, this is an example in sub- snit:Mon. Seven boys went down to a pond to bathe, but two of them had been told not to go in the water. Now, can you tell me how many went in ?" Tombs: "Yes, sir; seven." " Tnis," said the school friend who had not seen her for a year, "this is the girl who vowed to me that she never would belong to any man." "I don't," said she who had been married a matter of some few months. "He belongs to me." "EVEN when I was at work, lady, it was very hard. in my trade, you see, we ain't even allowed a little time for dinner. Not a minute." "Dear me, how hard. Here is twopence. How is it you are allowed no dinner-hour?" " Well, I'm a night wat ch man." DOING WITHOUT BREAD.—Mr. Forthort (read- ing paper) : " Well, here's bread up again a half- penny a loaf. I . don't know what we shall do !" Mrs. Forthort (suddenly inspired) "Why, live on biscuits, of course ! " A DONKEY WANTED.—A man, with a donkey for sale, hearing that a friend wanted to buy one, sent him the following written on a postal-card : "Dear Jack, if you are looking for a really good donkey, don't forget me." ON E day, at the late Dean of Ely's table, a legal gentleman was lamenting the gaps which death had recently made in his profession. " We have lost," he said, solemnly, "not less than six eminent lawyers in as many months." The Dean, who was quite deaf, at once rose and repeated grace "For this and all His mercies," etc. "YESTERDAY," said Jabson, "I refused a poor woman a request for a small sum of money, and in consequence of my act I passed a sleepless night. The tones of her voice were ringing in my ears the whole time." "Your softness of heart does you credit," said Mabson ; "who was the wotecto ? "—" My wife." WHY HE MEASURED THE DISTANCE.—A young lawyer, in a case of assault and battery, asked an old man of the vicinity at what distance he was fren the parties when the assault happened. "Just four feet five inches and a half," answered the wi:ness. "How came you to be so exact?" asked the counsel. "Because I expected some fool or other to ask me, and so I measured it." No COMPLAINTS.—Horse Dealer: "Well, John, how about that horse I sold you? Was he quiet enough ?" Undertaker: "Well, sir, he did give us a little trouble at first. We put him in one of the mourning coaches, you know, but parties don't like to be shook up in their grief. But we've put him in the hearse now, and we haven't heard any com- plaints so far. A WELL KNOWN eccentric colonel, when walk- ing, came upon an old man busy at work thatching a wayside cottage. Said the Colonel. "Man, ye're makin' a grand job o' that." Thatcher: "Ay, no' sae bad." Colonel: "Do you think you could do anything to this?" showing his bald pate. Thatcher: "I'm afraid no', sir. That's rayther oot o' my line. I'm thinkin' it's a slate it wants." A SUCCESSFUL PREACHER. —A bishop, as he was going about his diocese, asked the porter of a lunatic asylum how a chaplain whom he (the biehop) had lately appointed was getting on. "Oh, my lord," said the man, "his preaching is most successful. The hidiots henjoys it par- tick tar " AN UNEXPECTED SHOCK.—Brown: "Heavens! We are in for it now. Here comes Trotter, just back from a six months' trip abroad. He'll bore us to death with his talk about it." [Gleans from Ike rest. Trotter : " Well, boys, how are you all? I have just returned from a six months' trip in the East." (More groans). "I am just. going (to sit down here for two or three hours "—more groans)—" and I want "—(groans)—" you to tell me all that has happened here while I was away." THE RULING PASSION.—A Scotch minister, who is a keen angler, was out walking one Sunday when he accidentally came upon a boy fishing. Taken by surprise, the youngster was perplexed to know how to act, but seeing that he was in a fix, he ashamedly let the line sink to the bottom of the stream, while the rev, gentleman began lec- turing him on the evil of Sunday fishing. '1 he minister, however, on seeing the float suddenly bob under the water, brought the lecture to an end by exclaiming—" Pu' it oot, nton 1 Pie it oot! ate ye no' see ye ve got a bite?" A SNIPE STORY.—A story comes from a keen sportsman who was invited for a day's snipe shoot- ing in the neighbourhood of Abingdon. The in- vitation was accepted, and host and guests shouldered their guns and sallied forth in quest of game. After a time a solitary snipe rose and promptly fell to the visitor's first barrel. His host's face fell also, as he remarked that he might as well return, for that was "the only snipe in the neighbourhood; but, as it had afforded excellent Sport to all his friends for the last six weeks, he had hoped it would continue to be equally use- ful." THE POPE AND THE ARDENIEII —While walk- i G ng in the gardens Leo XIII. said to his under gardener, digging, "My good man, how much do you receive a day for your labour?" "Two francs, your Holiness." "And how many children have you?" "None, your Holiness." "And does your wife also work for her livelihood?" "Holy Father," said the man, "I have •never been mar- ried." The Pope paused and reflected. "Then, my good man," said he, "I shall give instructions that from henceforward, instead of two francs a day, you shall receive a franc and a half, and that half a franc shall be added to the wages of a work- man who has a family to support."</p>
